A Republican strategy to oppose Obama's re-election
Republican leaders justify their policies with the need to combat voter fraud.
However, the Brennan Center considers the latter a myth by reaffirming that electoral fraud in the U.S. is rarer than the number of people killed by lightning.
By the way, Republican lawyers have recorded only 300 counts of electoral fraud in 10 years in the U.S.
One thing is certain: these new provisions will have a negative impact on turnout.
In this sense, these measures partially undermine the U.S. democratic system.
Unlike Canada, U.S. states are responsible for conducting elections in individual states.
With this in mind, the majority of American governments since 2009 have proclaimed new laws complicating the procedure for registration or ballot.
This phenomenon has gained in importance after the November 2010 elections, in which 675 new republican representatives in 26 states could be recorded.
As a result, 180 draft laws were introduced in 2011 alone, restricting the exercise of the right to vote in 41 states.
The new electoral laws require voters to provide photo ID and proof of U.S. citizenship.
In addition, these laws also shorten the periods for early voting, invalidates the right to register as a voter on Election Day, and deny citizens the right to vote, for which a court file is available.
Ahead of the 2006 elections, no U.S. state demanded voters to show photo ID.
Indiana was the first state to make such a demand.
The U.S. Supreme Court upheld the constitutionality of Indiana's law in 2008.
The Republican authorities rushed to extend this practice to other states.
Over the past two years, they've been promoting bills in 34 states that force voters to show photo ID.
It's important to note that U.S. citizens, unlike Quebec, don't have a universal ID card like the health insurance card.
In fact, 11% of U.S. citizens, i.e. 21 million eligible persons, do not possess photo ID issued by a government agency of their state.
In addition, five million new voters do not have such ID in 2012.
However, more than a hundred dollars are often to be paid in order to obtain the required identification.
Young people, minorities and low-income low-income people are disproportionately affected by the new restrictions.
In fact, 25% of African-Americans, 15% of those with an income of less than $35,000, 18% of citizens over the age of 65, and 20% of voters between the ages of 18 and 29 do not have the required photo ID.
This extends even further.
Students who are seen as voters increasingly casting their votes for democratic candidates are not allowed in several states to use their photo ID issued by their institution.
On the other hand, the same states allow members of fishing or hunting associations who vote more strongly for the republican side to use the cards issued by these associations for elections.
Prior to 2004, none federal state has demanded proof of citizenship.
Arizona was the first state to introduce such a demand.
Since 2011, a dozen states have passed laws requiring voters to prove their U.S. citizenship.
These measures are clearly aimed at limiting Hispanic votes.
Because it is obvious that two out of three Hispanic voters who prefer democratic party.
What's more, Republican lawmakers promoted laws in eight states in 2011 that abolish the registration of voters on Election Day.
In addition, you have limited the right of individuals and groups to provide assistance to those voters who wish to register.
These limitations aren’t without consequences.
For example, voter registration campaigns in the 2004 general elections helped to register approximately 10 million citizens.
As a result of the measures adopted since 2009, the share of the registration of new voters has decreased by 17% compared to 2006.
In addition, Republican lawmakers in five other states have passed laws aimed at shortening the period for early voting.
For example, in Florida's 2008 general election, 33% of voters who voted in advance were African-American, even though they make up only 13% of the state's voters.
The same is true of the Hispanic population.
The latter accounted for only 11% of voters, but 24% of citizens who voted in advance.
By contrast, whites, who represented 76% of voters, accounted for only 46% of voters who voted in advance.
Of course, democratic lawmakers and their supporters have vigorously opposed the adoption of laws restricting the registration of voters.
Several draft laws have been blockeded by the vetos of Democratic governors.
U.S. Advocate-General has intervened to suspend the most controversial laws.
They were able to partially limit the damage.
For example, only 16 of the 34 states have passed laws that require the presentation of photo ID.
However, the newly introduced rules in 2012 will undoubtedly complicate the exercise of the right to vote.
The Democratic critics denounce the partisan political nature of the laws passed and see them as a clear goal to influence the results in the states responsible for 2012.
A 2011 report by the Brennan Center shows that those states that have passed these laws make up 171 of the 270 votes needed in the electoral men's panel to win the presidency.
It's too early to confirm with certainty that these changes in legislation on the electoral system will have a significant impact on the outcome of presidential elections in 2012.
But one thing is certain: these new provisions will have a negative impact on turnout.
In this sense, these measures partially undermine the U.S. democratic system.
Prestate cancer early detection: should the test be done or not?
The PSA test, in fact, sometimes exhibits erroneous results, with false negative or even false positive results that lead to unnecessary medical interventions.
This leads to greater hesitation of the already undecided men for carrying out early detection tests.
Should the test be done or not?
We've caught up with the opinion of two specialists.
In studies conducted in the U.S., there have been numerous disturbing factors between control groups, making it difficult to interpret this data and make clear recommendations.
Another study, this time from Europe, came to the conclusion that there is a difference in the mortality of patients with and without early detection.
This study has also shown that in a follow-up after 12 years the probability of formation of metastases is 30 to 40% higher if no early detection has been carried out.
That’s why I recommend the test from the age of 50 years or 40 years, if you have a direct relative who was already diagnosed with prostate cancer.
There is also a higher risk for men of African-American origin.
The most important thing is to make the right decision as soon as a cancer has been detected.
There are types of cancers that are aggressive and others that are indolent.
The patient must be really understood the extent of the risk of his cancer by showing him the possible options in which prostate cancers can remain untreated if they do not impair the quality of life in the long term, and in these cases one is more likely to opt for active monitoring of the disease.
Today, no treatment takes place in many men who have detected cancer, because their cancer is not aggressive and poses no threat to their lives.
They are suggested active monitoring and are offered treatment as the disease progresses.
One determines with increasing accuracy the criteria for a decision, who should be treated and who should not.
That’s why I still recommend carrying out the test.
The most important thing, however, is to talk to your doctor to determine whether he should be carried out or not.
In collaboration with the International Society of Urology, Movember has developed an instrument that makes it possible to assess the advantages and disadvantages of PSA testing.
This document (currently only in English, but a translation will soon be available) can be downloaded at: http://ca.movember.com/fr/mens-health/prostate-cancer-screening
Preventing the disease
Unfortunately, there is no miracle cure for the prevention of cancer.
Despite advances in research, adopting a healthy lifestyle remains the best way to reduce the risk of developing it.
It is estimated that 30% of cancers could be prevented if everyone were to eat well and move adequately.
"If there were no more smokers, this figure would rise to at least 50%," emphasizes André Beaulieu, spokesman for Canada's cancer society.
However, it is assumed that about 10% of cancers are caused by inheritance.
Several also remain completely unexplained.
For Canada's cancer society, the fight against tobacco addiction remains a priority, despite the decline in the number of smokers.
Smoking is responsible for 85% of lung cancer cases.
It's also a risk factor for several other cancers.
It severely damages people's health.
"There are still 1.5 million smokers in Quebec today," said André Beaulieu.
Encouraging dates: 10 years after abandoning the cigarette, the risk of cancer decreases by half.
The Weight
According to the Canadian Cancer Society, overweight and obesity also promote the onset of the disease.
They appear to increase the risk of breast, greasy, rectal, esophageal, pancreatic and uterine cancers.
"Research shows that regular physical activity throughout life protects against grimmary cancer," he adds.
Diet
The organization also recommends limiting the consumption of red meat.
In too large a quantity, it increases the risk of developing grimm or colon cancer.
Sausages should also be avoided.
Preservation of meat by incensing, drying or salting can lead to the formation of carcinogens.
"The latter can damage the body's cells and lead to the development of cancer," he explains.
Vitamins
In recent years, several scientists have studied the association between vitamin supplements and cancer.
However, however, their investigations are currently inconclusive.
According to the Canadian cancer society, the studies on vitamin E are contradictory.
While one study has founded a reduction in the risk of prostate cancer, another showed an increase.
The effect of vitumin D on cancer has also not yet been clearly demonstrated.
In addition, Mr Beaulieu stresses the importance of talking to his doctor about his concerns and his familial prejudgment.
“Conducting an early detection test does not lead to cancer.”
The Higgs boson analyzed
Announcing the probable discovery of the Higgs boson caused a stir last summer, and for good reason.
It is indeed believed that this boson is involved in nothing less than the mechanism responsible for the mass of all objects in the universe.
And finally, it is the last particle whose existence was predicted by the standard model - our best or "leastly false" explanation of nature and the behavior of matter, but has not yet been observed empirically.
But for physicists, it's not yet completely certain that it's really the Higgs.
We know without the shadow of a doubt that we have a real new particle, and that it strongly resembles the Higgs boson predicted by the standard model.
In addition, this seems to be confirmed by new data presented this week at a major physics congress in Kyoto, but data is still lacking to be completely safe.
Nevertheless, let’s assume that it’s actually the Higgs, because there seems to be a small risk of being mistaken, and let’s look at what it’s all about.
There is an inevitably unavoidable law in this world that causes that two objects cannot exist simultaneously in the same place.
It's not possible to violate that rule - and don't try it too much as you're going to hurt yourself.
For even if particle physics is a really strange world, it turns out that such a law also applies to it: Pauli's exclusion principle, which states that two particles cannot occupy the same space at the same time when they are in the same "quantum state," whereby this "state" consists broadly of some of its properties.
Based on this, the physicists divide the particles into two categories.
In one corner are the good citizens, who are referred to as fermions and kindly obey the Pauli principle.
And in the other move the bosons, a nasty bond of anarchists to whom nothing is sacred - in any case not this principle, meaning that they may indeed be in the same place at the same time.
According to the website The Particle Adventure of the Berkeley Laboratories (which, by the way, is absolutely amazing), these bosons are divided into two groups: the mesons that we will not deal with here and the "power particles" through which the great forces of nature spread and with which the Higgs boson would somehow be related.
However, it must be emphasized here that these bosons are not all such exotic creatures as you might think.
If you are able to read this chronicle, then only thanks to a boson of extraordinary banality: the photon or the "light particle," which is the "message" of the electromagnetic force.
Namely, if a particle with an electrical charge accelerates or changes direction, the electromagnetic field is "disturbed" precisely at this location, roughly like a pebble that has been thrown into a pond.
As a result of this "disturbance", an electromagnetic wave (light, infrared or ultraviolet radiation, etc.), and this wave is nothing more than a photon and thus one of the boson "carriers of force".
Stabler field
The same applies to the Higgs boson, with the difference that another field, the Higgs field, must be "disturbed" in order for this boson to appear.
However, the Higgs field is much more stable than the electromagnetic field; the pathogen must reach very high energies, a little comparable to a frozen pond whose surface could only be furrowed by a very large rock.
That's why a huge particle accelerator like the CERN is required - the Large Hadron Collider is a ring with a circumference of 27 km! - to achieve such energies.
The analogy with the electromagnetic field is once again helpful in explaining the relationship between the Higgs and mass.
It's in fact not all the particles and not all materials that interact with the electromagnetic field.
Some, like magnets, don't, but others don't - for example, a sheet of paper would never stop at the fridge by itself.
And equally, not all particles interact with the Higgs field: those who do it possess a mass, while the others (like the photon, by the way), have no mass.
For what can all this research now "be good," Ms Plamondon asks?
For science, they serve to review the standard model (SM), and they also allow physicists to closely examine all deviations between the observations and the predictions of the SM.
There are, by the way, numerous physicists eagerly hoping that such deviations will be found, since the slightest difference could open a door to a "new physics" and stuff some holes of the model.
It must be said that the latter still has major deficiencies, as it does not provide an explanation for gravity (ups!) and the dark matter that forms about 80% of the matter of the universe (again ups!).
However, such deviations have not been found at CERN until now.
Implications
The impact of this research on the general population is more difficult to predict, but it would be wrong to assume that there were none.
By the way: in the early 1960s, the pioneers of the laser in the Bell laboratories did not suspect that their works would trigger such a revolution.
They saw scientific applications, but nothing else.
In fact, the late physicist Willard Boyle - who worked at the Bell Labs where the laser was invented in the 1960s and who himself developed the first continuous wave laser (the first were pulsed) in 1962 - has already told us that in the beginning the laser was considered more of a "laboratory gimmacy."
Imagine...
In addition, applications can also result from all the instruments surrounding research.
Willard Boyle, for example, developed a small light sensor in 1969 during his work in optics.
Although it wasn't the original purpose, this sensor now serves as an "eye-eye" to all digital cameras worldwide and earned it the Nobel Prize in Physics in 2009.
Of course, this doesn't mean the LHC's activities will inevitably change our lives, but it means you really can't ever know...
Palliative care - the best way to die... | Le Devoir
With its committee on "dying with dignity," Quebec has recently debated the delicate issue of the end of life.
The debate must resume shortly, although a bill is being prepared.
However, there is still a lot of work to be done in this important subject area.
Le Devoir has tried to take a closer look at this.
A few weeks agove, Mr. L. still lived alone in his Montérégie apartment.
The prostate cancer that consumed him had left him a two-year deadline.
"They gave me five years to live, I've managed seven years," he said half-heartedly, lying in his bed at Victor-Gadboi's palliative care home in Beloeil, where he had arrived the day before.
"However, it's always a shock, you can't be prepared for that," he says.
The disease does its job: an immeasurable weake prevents him from going to the bathroom alone and even eating herself.
Sitting at the table in front of a delicious lunch, he resignedly agrees to get helped with food.
Courageously, he even manages to smile and talk to the strangers who are busy around him, bring him his medication, and offer him a bath.
Courage of ordinary death.
"My greatest wish is to heal my diarrhea, as this is humiliating," he shares.
A few hours later, the team found a cure for this evil.
"Over the course of our lives, we learn that a man is peeing standing," says Pierre Brodeur, a psychologist at Victor-Gadbois.
Returninging to the childhood phase is an unacceptable humiliation for certain persons.
"That depends on the person's ability," he notes.
In the opinion of several people working in palliative care, great events are taking place in the midst of these regressions.
The patients who are admitted to the Victor-Gadbois palliative care home all suffer from cancer.
have a maximum life expectancy of three months.
At this stage, they are no longer showered with so-called "healing" care by the doctors and nurses.
For Ms. A., 89 years old, the worst fear of dying "consciously and through suffocation."
But because of the disease, I really got to know my children.
"I've got good kids," she adds.
"I don't have any wishes in life anymore," she says, before accepting that she's putting on a mask that helps her breathe.
In the next few days, she is still expecting a last visit from her son, who comes from Italy.
Victor-Gadboi's home offers body care with a group of volunteers and assistance with food intake.
Palliative care is used when there are no other options.
To make dying more enjoyable.
In Quebec there is 11,700 residents with a bed for palliative care.
This is very little when you take into account that one day we will all die.
Here, life continues in the best possible conditions, explains Dr. Christiane Martel, one of the doctors of the home.
Both on the level of physical, emotional or spiritual comfort.
Depending on their wishes, the dying person is granted oral care with brandy or pepsi.
Diabetics no longer need to control their blood sugar levels.
Death is part of everyday life.
Last night we served Mr. X a beer that died over the course of the night.
This morning his son will drink the beer at the feet of the deceased.
"We help relatives and patients alike," reports nursing director Nathalie Savard.
Victor-Gadboi's home isn't a day like another.
In addition to an old man of 93, who enjoys the last family gathering in the yard and is well propped up by his pillows while toasting in his honor, a young man of 36, who is surrounded by his parents, his wife and his two young children, dies tragically after trying everything to survive.
"For six months, three to five beds have always been occupied with cancer patients aged less than 45," says Dr. Christiane Martel.
53% of patients admitted to Victor-Gadboi's home come from their residence, 47% come from the hospital.
problem of access to palliative care
It is said that 77% of Canadians do not have access to palliative care, i.e. to a care designed to alleviate the suffering of patients when the latter have reached the final stage of their lives, both at home, in the hospital or in a nursing home.
And several organizations, such as Victor-Gadboi's home, and home palliative care society in Greater Montre, specialize more or less exclusively in the care of cancer patients.
It is precisely this great inadequacy in Quebec’s health care that ensures that several physicians specializing in palliative care are looking forward to the adoption of a law regulating euthanasia and assisted suicide.
There has been circulating a manifesto signed by prominent palliative care figures such as Dr. Balfour Mount and Dr Bernard Lapointe since October to profess their opposition to such an initiative.
According to Dr Christiane Martel, Quebec's health care system isn't powerful enough to ensure that everyone has the right to high-quality palliative care before accepting to euthanasia.
Recently, she says, I have seen a patient who has been in the emergency room for 14 days with great pain, without taking any action to alleviate her.
I'm afraid that patients want to die just because they don't get adequate health care.
And at the same time, several oncologists treat their patients, despite the worst prognosies until the last day.
Hélène Richard's chances of survival were already minimal when she discontinued arduous chemotherapy.
When I told my oncologist that I was going to discontinue treatment, she replied that she regreted that I was giving up the fight, she recounted.
However, she had told me that I were at the end!
No omnipotent care
Dr Martel believes that 90% of patients who have asked to die thank nursing staff for failing to this wish after their pain has been alleviated by a palliative care team.
However, it must be clearly stated that palliative care is not omnipotent in pain treatment.
According to Elsie Monereau, head of palliative care for the Society of Home Palliative Care in the Greater Montreal area, 8% of patients do not respond to pain treatment.
At the very end of life, doctors often resort to terminal sedation, which results in euthanciating the patient either sporadically or permanently until his death.
We can no longer pretend that we don't perceive this side of suffering.
Patients without pain relief will increasingly have the opportunity to resort to this terminal sedation.
Patients whose pain will not be alleviated will all say the same phrase: "I want to die."
But that doesn't necessarily mean "I want you to put me to sleep," but "I want my pain to be alleviated."
The realization of this report was possible due to a journalism grant of Canadian health research institutes.
Real estate scandals on a large scale in Quebec
Officers of the road construction office, corporations of the main construction industry, money procurers of political parties and specialists of the Italian mafia tell every day what they know about a great "system" in which construction industry, civil servants, politicians, trade unionists and mafioso are entangled.
An "industry," which was very expensive for Quebec's taxpayer, particularly in the 1990s and 2000s.
"It's strange how the system disintegrates since you took drastic measures," jokes Jacques Duchesneau, lawmaker in Quebec and former head of Montreal police.
The scandal was uncovered by him due to an in-depth investigation into misappropriations related to the Treaties on Road-Way Construction in Quebec, which the then-Prime Minister had reluctantly consented to.
The "Duchesneau report" established a direct link between industry, secret financing of parties and bribery of officials.
"Since the beginning of the investigation in 2010," he emphasizes, "the Department of Transportation alone would have saved a billion dollars on the contracts," because some curbed their instinct to preserve their share!
The Charbonneau Commission "has already brought down two mayors," he adds, in the hope that it will succeed in "pointing out the tactics behind the individuals."
A permanent anti-corruption unit launched in 2011
Launched in 2011, the permanent anti-corruption unit is also making great efforts with its troops on auditors, investigations and analysts from the government.
In addition, the officers of the "Marteau force", which since 2009 has effected that the "seweral cartel" of Montreal in the artificial bloating of the treaties...
In recent weeks, she has carried out in rows of house searches and made accusations of fraud and corruption against municipal mandate holders such as Frank Tampino and Richard Marcotte, who is mayor of a suburb.
The next on the list would be Gilles Vaillancourt, who has just resigned as mayor of Laval, Quebec's third-largest city.
He is suspected of having repeatedly received bribes in exchange for public contracts.
Explicitly charged were engineers of the road construction office of Montreal, as well as entrepreneurs of Italian origin, such as Tony Accurso and Lino Zambito.
The latter caused a stir by explaining to the Commission the functioning of the "system" for the maintenance of public contracts.
For several years, he himself paid 3% of the value of the treaties received in Montreal to an intermediary associated with the mafia who transferred the money to the Union Montreal, the party of Mayor Gérald Tremblay.
Mr. Zambito had his hand everywhere in the 2000s by giving more than 88,000 Canadian dollars (about €68,000) to provincial parties, most notably to the liberals in power at the time.
He also confessed to having organised an illegitimate raising of funds for former Liberal vice-president Nathalie Normandeau.
Sewerage contracts whose costs were artificially bloated by him
In Montreal, the "system" of corruption worked smoothly.
Former civil engineer Gilles Surprenant described this in detail before the commission: in ten years he received gifts from the construction company, invitations to travel, golf tournaments, restaurants and ice hockey games, as well as bribes of $736,000 in return for sewerage contracts, the cost of which he artificially inflated.
Other road construction officials have admitted that they let themselves be smeared by artificially inflating the bills with false additional benefits by 30-40%.
In addition, an organizer of the mayor's party, Martin Dumont, has accused Mr Tremblay of intentionally looking over a shadow budget that filled his coffers with black money.
As a result of these revelations, Mr Tremblay resigned at the beginning of November, which plunged Montreal into a serious crisis.
Chantal Rouleau was one of Montreal's first MPs to sound the alarm.
As mayor of Rivière-des-Prairie's district, in the east of the island, from 2010, she opposed the sale of a municipal property that was acquired for $5 million and resolded for 1.6 million to property developers in the booming real estate market.
70% black money in election campaigns
From the investigation ultimately carried out, she says she "draws by a thread that makes it possible to understand the functioning of the system submigrated by ants to stop the evil and denounce the culprits."
She says the procedure is "painful but positive."
We are in the process of cleaning up the wound, but would need an investigative unit intended only for Montreal, as well as oversight to prevent a return of questionable practices.
how to clean up.
On a regular basis.
Jacques Duchesneau, for his part, notes that "officials have stolen several hundred million dollars," but above all thinks about the role of "mandate holders who are educated about the tactics" if they weren't involved in the scam!
Starting from a share of 70% black money in the financing of electoral campaigns in Quebec, he jokes: "I was told that this was only a weak clap of reality."
Quebec's government is proposing limiting donations to parties to $100, which, according to him, wouldn't change the situation: "Unless campaign spending is strictly limited, black money will be in circulation in politics."
He recommends a complete revision of the system for the award of public contracts and the financing of the parties: "We can't fall deeper; by getting to the bottom of things with courage, we can rebuild the House on a more solid basis with more controls and laws."
If this story damages the international prestige of Quebec and that of Montreal, Mr. Duchesneau urges those who are laughing about taking a look into their own backyard.
“PSG isn’t FC Barcelona!”
This season, you've taken on a new proportion with PSG.
How do you explain this progress?
It can be explained by a personal insight, but also through the new dimension of PSG.
Great players have come.
Every day I make progress at their side.
The technical staff also gave me a lot.
These elements spur me every day to improve my game level.
And in a game, it's even easier.
In football, everything goes very quickly.
But I don't break out in enthusiasm.
Since my beginnings at the center of the preparatory training of the INF Clairefontaine until my season at Saint-Etienne, I have always made gradual progress.
They thus make use of the competition introduced by Carlo Ancelotti...
This summer's new additions are accustomed to playing games of very high levels.
They also know that every training session is crucial.
As a result, a player like me needs to stand up and wants to do his best.
By the way, Carlo Ancelotti gives me a lot in terms of my playing position.
He's backed by co-trainers like Claude Makelele, who played in the same position as me.
Is Ancelotti the right person for the task?
Of course.
Ancelotti inspires respect for all technicians.
There is currently no comparable person in Ligue 1, and he is one of Europe's best coaches.
He looks back on a rich experience and has won many titles with respected clubs.
He's been in contact with big players.
I think he's going to win more titles in Paris.
In January I had an encouraging conversation with him.
At that time I had just recovered from an injury streak.
The trust he has placed in me is also an explanation for my accomplishments.
What do you think about the first part of PSG's season?
In Ligue 1, Lyon have taken the front-runner's place.
We're lurking in ambush though.
One of our most important goals is the Champions League: we qualified for the quarter-finals in this way.
What is the club's goal in this competition?
We will try to get as far as possible.
From now on everything is possible.
However, we will have a say against very good European teams.
First of all, we want to become the first of our group before Porto, so that we can play the return leg of the quarter-finals as a home game.
Can PSG become a major European club in the short term?
He's already got the budget for this...
To become a big European club, Paris must win titles and make a name for itself in the long term.
This isn't the case today.
The PSG is financially giving itself the means to ensure that this project takes concrete forms.
Would it be a huge failure not to win the title in Ligue 1, as was the case last season?
Of course, this would be a huge disappointment.
This year it's really important to us to write off this title.
Last season we were close.
In May we were disappointed as we were strong enough to win.
We ended the season with a very strong performance.
We graduated with 79 points.
Normally you should be champion with 79 points...
But another squad, that of Montpellier, has scored an even better season.
I believe that this year is the right one.
Even though the big teams such as Marseille, Lyon or Bordeaux are wrestlingling for the title, I believe we have what it takes to win it.
Do you think the media are expecting too much from PSG?
Considering what has been invested and the players we have, it's normal that a lot is expected of us.
We fully accept this.
However, if we win in a home game against Troyes 4-0 and you still find something that you can accuse us of, that's certainly a bit frustrating.
One wonders what else people are expecting.
We couldn't win every weekend with 4-0.
We aren’t FC Barcelona.
We're trying to set up a game concept.
A team builds up over time.
We have shown strength in the Champions League.
Consider Manchester City, a club that hasn't been able to qualify for the quarter-finals for two seasons, and which has also made huge expenses!
With regard to the invested sums you were believed for the game break with a 15-point advantage!
That would mean our opponents and the French championship would be disregarded.
Lyon and Marseille, who weren't well last season, were "boosted" by this new PSG.
This shows that Ligue 1 is exciting.
I hope that we will be the smiling winner in the month of May and tell ourselves that we finally won them despite all the difficulties.
PSG appears to be entirely dependent on Zlatan Ibrahimovic's performances.
All the better that one speaks of a "Zlatan dependency."
That means Ibrahimovic is powerful and scoring a lot of goals.
That's why he's come and he's proving he's the star of Ligue 1.
Wherever he's played, he's shown that he's a great gambler and a world star.
Within the squad we respect both the player and the person.
He also respects the people who surround him.
What he's done is truly extraordinary.
This encourages the others to improve their level of play.
Thiago Silva, who is one of the best defenders in the world, also allows everyone else to make progress.
How did you experience the 2012 European Championship with the French national team?
It's like a disappointment.
It was important to me to participate in this European Championship.
Unfortunately, my injury prevented me from knocking out some playing time.
I've seen things there from which I've emerged strengthened.
Today I manage to realize good games in the national team.
This has been my hope since my baptism with the "Bleus."
From what has happened in Ukraine, we have learned our lesson, and today we must show exemplary behaviour.
What do you think about Didier Deschamps's first few months at the head of the "Bleus"?
He's achieved promising results.
We have a good position in the qualifying group for the World Cup.
The national coach is strict, close to the players and encourages victory.
Just like Laurent Blanc.
But I don't want to make a comparison.
Blanc had achieved his goal by qualifying us for the European Championship.
I hope Didier Deschamps will lead the "Bleus" to Brazil.
Is the decision (1:1), which was forced on 16 October in Spain, a kick-off match?
This game has given us confidence.
Everyone stood up for everyone else.
Before this meeting in Spain, I've never experienced a comparable game in my career.
With Bitcoin, payment and selling without a bank is possible
The opposite of the current monetary exchange based on central banks, identified transactions and processing fees between stakeholders.
Moreover, as is so often the case in these technologies, a political vision is palpable: the conviction that the current monetary system shaped by banking monopolies leads to financial crises.
In fact, Bitcoin invented by Satoshi Nakamoto (a pseudonym) is both a virtual currency (but convertible into dollars, euros, etc.) and a protocol for secure exchange like BitTorrent, which allows the direct exchange of files.
With 15,000 computers on the network, about 200,000 transactions have already been recorded.
A few thousand websites accept Bitcoin as a donation or as a means of payment.
Aftend Bitcoin's price peaked at $30 (€23) in June 2011, it fell to $2 five months later before recovering to around ten dollars today (prices are listed on bitcoincharts.com).
Nothing particularly impressivent compared to the world trade of real currencies or financial products.
Nevertheless, the European Central Bank (ECB) has showned interest in doing so in a report on virtual currencies published in October.
It describes Bitcoin as “the virtual currency with the greatest success”, “in competition with the dollar or euro” and “similar to conventional currencies”.
Bitcoin differs from other types of virtual currencies, such as the “credits” that are used to move forward in a video game that you can gain or buy (and sometimes re-exchange) through gaming.
The social network Facebook has also developed such a system.
However, the trade is inspected and processed each time by a central authority.
With Bitcoin, all network nodes are at the same disposal of the invoice book, examiners, money issuers, as well as buyers and sellers.
How does this network work?
In reality, every transaction between two users takes place between two e-mail addresses in the form of an e-mail.
With the difference that the user can choose a different address for each payment, which ensures anonymity.
A number of the data associated with this transaction is signed electronically by a double encryption system.
In this way, the network can check the authenticity of the transaction.
Using the content of the file, it is also possible to ensure that the traded bitcoins are present in the public account book distributed throughout the network.
The crucial step is to enter this new transaction into this book.
This is done via a mathematical task launched on the computers, with the winner who is a kind of provisional landicator having the privilege of adding this extra line.
It involves dismembering the file, i.e. the conversion of a large file into a shorter and unique electronic fingerprint.
The computers "take" the new transaction and add a number to it before the whole "chopped".
The aim is to find the number that yields a special fingerprint (many zeros at the beginning).
When this number has been found, the other nodes can effortlessly check if it is the correct one.
Thus, the transaction is permanently linked to the chain of all other transactions, and any modification would change the fingerprint.
If one user wants to pay with the same money for cheating twice (less than 10 minutes), only one of the two transactions would be validated by the network and the other would be left without linking, since they both have different fingerprints.
The computer that solved the task receives 50 bitcoins.
To prevent inflation, this reward is regularly halved, probably before the end of 2012.
The bitcoins in circulation are thus limited to €21 million, but divisiblable to one hundred millionth, which leaves some room for manoeuvre.
In addition, the difficulty of the task is increased with every improvement in computing power.
The network's life has already had ups and downs.
The websites that provide services for Bitcoin have been attacked and the bitcoin deposits stolen.
"The used vulnerability doesn't affect the actual protocol," assures Pierre Noizat, who has just introduced Paymium, which is a company for payment with real money and uses the network Bitcoin.
The ECB also reports on the possibilities of money laundering through this anonymous service.
However, cash also exhibits these deficiencies.
Important players such as Wikipedia deny donations of this kind.
They are accepted by others such as the WordPress blog platform.
Recently, Adi Shamir and Dorit Ron from the Weizmann Institute in Israel analyzed the accounting book and showed that almost 80% of bitcoins are not in circulation.
A "mega-closing sale" was launched in November.
"Thirty thousand dollars have been exchanged," rejoices Jon Holmquist, who works for Coinabul, which converts bitcoins into gold.
Pierre Noizat, who is also the author of a book on this currency, firmly believes in the potential of this technology as a transaction network.
Its Paytunia system corresponds to a credit card (with real money) or a contactless payment with a mobile phone, but uses Bitcoin to validate the transactions that are therefore cheaper.
In addition, the owner manages his identity and can therefore remain anonymous.
The system can be easily implemented at the dealers, and the latter do not need to install new terminals or computer programs.
You simply need to specify an address that can be "photographed and recognized" by a phone, Pierre Noizat, who assures that he has thousands of users.
There is a general trend in which hierarchical systems are reconsidered in favour of more horizontal systems.
"It's going to take some time for Bitcoin to prevail, but 2013 could be a turning point," he predicts.
Incidentally, in its report, the ECB envisages reassessing the various risks, which are now considered high, in the event of the success of this currency.
We're withdrawn from Afghanistan.
And after that?
French troops have left their area of responsibility in Afghanistan (Kapisa and Surobi).
NATO and the United States are likely to follow at the end of 2014.
It is time for the Afghan army to seize possession of its territory again, and for the Afghan population to decide its future without expecting everything from us.
We, in particular, have punished the Afghan peasants by viewing them as terrorists.
And ourselves with our 88 soldiers killed, as well as the wounded and the injured.
The Taliban consists of foreign extremists, former leaders who have fled to Pakistan, but frequently also of peasants denying an armed foreign presence, as at the time of the Soviets.
They want to defend their centuries-old and archaic traditions, even if jihadists, Pakistanis, Arabs, Uzbeks and Tajiks have joined them.
Tolerated, sometimes supported by the local insurgents, the latter will no longer be there as Western countries become more discreet.
The withdrawal of the French troops from the base in Nijrab, which I watched from a hill planted with almond trees, which was financed with the help of French loans.
The lorry and tank convoys skimmed by helicopters have reached Kabul without being attacked.
From the end of 2014, there will be no more influence by the Taliban into Kabul
Since their irresistible advance from 1994 to 1996, conditions have changed.
At that time, Kabul was empty and the land overwhelmed by war due to the struggles of the various riotous groups
Their seizure of power at the time was seen as a kind of liberation and a return to security.
The Afghan women have paid the price for the obscurantism overwhelmed by the Al-Qaeda Organization, but their situation has not improved to date.
Former mujahideen, the Afghan government and current Taliban have a common desire to keep the women in a subordinate position.
The most important personalities of the anti-Soviet war came back to power in 2001.
They have turned into businessmakers by seizing land from the government to sell them as building land to refugees from Iran and Pakistan, thus drawing benefits from the massive American awarding of subcontracts.
They brought themselves into disrepute, and most of them were not involved in the fighting themselves.
As I've heard in the campaigns, people want a government that isn't made up of thieves.
Many young people want to leave the country, just like those who were able to take advantage of American grants: capital flight is considerable.
Young people are tired of the war and its ideologies.
During their exile in Iran or Pakistan, they have had contact with the modern world and have enjoyed their advantages.
About 65% of the population is under the age of 25; Kabul now has 5 million inhabitants, which is a fifth of the total population.
In the cities, state schools are full of girls as well as boys.
These young people, who do not want to return to the obscurantism of the former parties or the corruption of certain leaders, will have to provide jobs.
Everyone, including the armed parties, is obsessed with mobile phones; television with its Turkish series depicting a modern world is watched everywhere.
There's an army present now.
Are the authorities under whose authority she will be under whom she will be seen as legitimate?
Former commanders of anti-Soviet struggle are already striving for the restoration of militias in the province, which will evade the power of the central government.
Afghanistan, the land of the mountains with strong local identities, should have benefited from some decentralization using the example of Western nations, but by abolishing the office of prime minister, which has existed since the constitution of 1964, the U.S. wanted to turn him into a centralized state with a strong presidential force.
President Karzaï does not want foreign checks, particularly on the occasion of elections scheduled for April 2014.
However, his country has been dependent on foreign aid since the 1950s and long before.
Industries have not been rebuilt, functional dams are non-existent and important irrigation systems have not been repaired.
Everything is imported; apart from fruits and vegetables, nothing is produced.
The private initiative is given priority.
In a country destroyed by 30 years of war, state control of infrastructures would have been required.
The rumor has been circulated that Afghanistan possessing immense mineral resources.
This reinforces the impression that Western countries were only on the ground to seize it.
Mining is not possible without energy to process the iron or copper ore on site, or without means of transport for export via the mountains.
The Chinese have already almost left the copper mine of Mes Aynak, let the international archaeologists (funded by the World Bank) do their excavations at the vast Buddhist site and remain the province's main employers.
One day it will also be necessary for Afghanistan and Pakistan, on which imports and exports are largely dependent, to reestablish normal relations.
The withdrawal of French combat troops was completed on November 20.
The new cooperation agreement provides for the continuation of traditional aids: girls' and boys' gymnasiums, Institute of French at the Universities, French Institute, cooperation in the military, legal, medical and agricultural fields, support of the archaeological delegation.
Since 2000, a "civil-military actions" service of the Ministry of Defence (Cimic), which was discontinued in dozens of mountain villages, has carried out numerous charitable works and measures for the rehabilitation of agriculture in dozens of mountain villages and had it carried out by a small French non-governmental organization to try to "gain hearts and spirits" and the impossible task of reconciling aid and offensive action.
These works, for which numerous local workers were required, have been able to help suppress the uprising: irrigation, wells, drinking water, reforestation, fruit trees, soil protection and increase of agricultural land.
Which souvenir do we leave behind after military spending of two billion euros?
A much more modest budget will help improve local living conditions, which are very hard at these valleys, often at an altitude of more than 2000 metres.
The embassy has received dozens of written inquiries of small agricultural projects from municipalities in the province of Kapisa.
In order for farmers to free themselves from the uprising led by foreign groups, which, according to their own statements, is their desire, the continuation of less, well-controlled and directly related civilian aid would be necessary in their favour.
A Constitution with the Crowbar in Egypt
It is a new poker game by President Mohammed Morsi.
While Egypt remains more than ever divided in terms of the constitutional declaration, which temporarily grants him unlimited power of attorney, he has decided to put everything on a map.
To everyone's surprise, he announced on Wednesday that the Constituential Assembly would pass its final version the next day.
Just a week ago, the head of state had granted this plenary two additional months for the completion of his work.
Egypt has been relying for almost two years on a provisional, multi-amplified text, weakening institutional stability and leading to a legal mess.
This new initiative has only made for an even greater division of the country.
For his opponents, the president persists in his "autocratic delirium" by continuing to "break his word" and "tramp on the right."
On the part of its proponents, it is assured that it is a way to end the institutional and political crisis as quickly as possible and accelerate the transition process.
Within 14 days, a referendum should be held.
A very short period of times, which forces the brothers to refrain from their intention to explain to the Egyptians the text section by section.
For the president, it's also a way to find his public and democratic legitimacy again as the protest rages across the country.
After affirmations in an interview with the American newsreel Time, Mohammed Morsi seems to be convinced that the Egyptians will vote benevolent.
Especially since an ultimatum is issued to the Egyptian people with this resort to a hasty vote: "Either you vote in favour of my draft or I retain the unlimited power of attorney", the latter should supposedly come to an end after the adoption of the constitution.
85 mostly Islamist members of this Constituential Assembly passed the text yesterday in a strange atmosphere.
The majority of liberals did not comply.
Shortly before the constitutional declaration in mid-November, they averted because they felt that they have not been able to highlight their views.
Representatives of human rights organisations, religious minorities or civil society have acteded equally.
In order to achieve quorum, 11 people, deputy members, were hastily addeded yesterday morning.
Some are very close to the Muslim Brotherhood.
It's not surprising that the articles were mostly passed unanimously.
The commentators, by the way, found it entertaining that the only disagreement of the disagreement of the day was expressed in the time for prayer, as some members of the board were of the opinion that the commuting clock of the Constituential Assembly did not go exactly.
The text, which was adopted last night, consists of 234 articles.
Article 2, which attracted all attention, finally remains identical to that of the 1971 Constitution, and states that "the principles of Sharia are the main source of law."
The Salafist parties, for which the establishment of Islamic law is an important requirement, had the hope of replacing "the principles" with "the rules," which would have made stricter application possible.
For the Islamists, the fact that this article has not been changed is a testament to their goodwill and their respect for the other members of Egyptian society.
"Honocricity," reciprocates the liberals, who see this only as a propaganda blow.
According to them, the Islamization of the constitution takes place through other articles.
In doing so, they have in mind in Particular Article 220, which grants the University of Al-Azhar an advisory function, in particular with regard to the verification of conformity with the laws of Sharia.
According to Egypt specialist Sophie Pommier, this is disturbing, as "the persons who are urged to comment have not been elected and have no democratic legitimacy whatsoever.
You can see the first signs of a theocracy.”
The fears of the liberals are also fueled by the fact that the next rector of this university is likely to be far less moderate than the present one.
“Currently there is no concrete religious commitment.
With this constitution, we remain within the framework of the bourgeois state.
Most lawyers who have worked on this text are not Ulema, but academics who have been educated in part in the French system," appeased Alexis Blouet, who writes a doctoral thesis on Egypt's constitutional transition.
He acknowledges, however, "that there may be ambiguity with regard to Article 220, since the terms used are borrowed from the religious vocabulary.
One refers here in particular to the "Fiqh" [Islamic jurisprudence, note].
And in the future, the question of the extent to which civil judges are competent to decide about it could be raised."
In addition to its religious aspect, the text adopted yesterday is heavily criticized for the far-reaching powers it grants to the president of the republic.
The Muslim Brotherhood argue that these are significantly reduced compared to the former regime.
Another point of contention: the powers of the army.
In line with the military's wish, the examination of the defence budget will not be submitted to parliament, but to a national defence council.
Likewise, there will be no prohibition on civil proceedings in military courts, as the associations for the defense of human rights have demanded.
The latter also share their fears regarding the text, which they consider to be freedom-threatening.
The fact of blasphemy is maintained, and insults are now banned, which could have serious consequences for the right to freedom of expression, especially for the press.
In addition, no more article refers to the protection of women, emphasizes Heba Morayef of Human Rights Watch.
In their opinion, the only positive point is the prohibition of torture by Article 36.
This word was not included in the previous constitution.
While the Egyptian president had to speak on television last night, demonstrations are planned for this afternoon.
Proponents of the head of state will march Saturday.
In Israel, Ukrainian tourists expect sacred sites, the world's navel and a sea of brine
The Promised Land unites into itself the glory of biblical truths, modern comfort and an untouched nature.
AIF will give you the five most important reasons for visiting Israel.
Bowind before the holy sites
Visit the river Jordan where Jesus was baptized.
It is said that all those bathing in this river receive God’s blessings.
Galilee is the place where Jesus performed his miracles: the transformation of water into wine at a wedding celebration, walking on the water, the quenching of the sea tower and fishing.
This is where Jesus appeared to his disciples after the resurrection.
However, most sanctuaries are found in Jerusalem.
Believers celebrate the way of suffering or the Way of the Cross of Jesus Christ (in Latin: Via Dolorosa).
The path begins at the Roman Fortress Antonia, Pretorium, where the judgment was, and leads through the streets of the old town to the Church of the Holy Sepulchre on Mount Golgotha, where the crucifixion, the anointing stone and the tomb of Jesus are located.
According to the Christian conception, the navel of the world, which symbolizes the salvation of humanity is located here.
The Monastery of the Holy Cross in Jerusalem stands in the place where, according to a Christian legend, the tree from which the cross was made for the crucifixion of Jesus grew.
In Jerusalem there is also the holiest place of the Jews - the lamination wall, the remnants of a temple destroyed by the Romans in 70 A.D.
According to tradition, people of different faiths leave notes there with wishes, which then come true.
Take a trip to the heights
The ruins of Massada fortress form the remains of the secret hiding place from enemies that Herod the Great built for his family in 25 A.D.
They are located on a rock wall in the mountains at 450 meters above sea level.
Only mountaineers can reach the ruins on their own.
Everyone else brings an aerial cable car to this historic summit.
In the north of the country at 1600 to 2040 meters altitude is the famous Hermon skire, which attracts countless tourists during the winter months.
From the foot of the mountain you can get there with a shuttle bus.
The ski slopes have a total length of 45 kilometres.
According to an ancient legend, pagan gods allegedly lived on the mountaintop.
Visiting unique museums
There are nearly 300 museums in this country.
With just one trip, not all of them can be visited.
However, at least the five most interesting of them should be paid a visit.
Among them is the Israel Museum not far from the Knesset (Parliament).
There, old Qumran manuscripts or Dead Sea Scrolls, which were found in rock caves in the Judean Desert, are preserved, as well as about 500 thousand archaeological and anthropological exhibits.
The Museum of Art in Tel Aviv is also worth a visit.
There, the collection of works by the Impressionists and Expressionists Monet, Pissaro, Renoir, Cézanne, Sisley, Matisse, Modigliani, Chagall and Picasso will be exhibited.
In Akko you can visit the museum in Bad Al Basha, which houses several halls of old Turkish baths with sculptures by the visitors of that time and bathing servants.
In Caesarea you should visit the unique private Ralli Museum, where you can admire sculptures of Dali and Rodin.
There are neither museum guides nor souvenir shops.
Admission is free, and donations are generally rejected.
The fifth museum, namely the Holocaust Museum or Yad Vashem in Tel Aviv, tells about one of the most harrowing chapters in history.
The most poignant department is the monument to the children, which was built to commemorate the 1.5 million children who were destroyed in the concentration camps and gas chambers.
Upon enterincing the room there is complete darkness.
Stars sparkle.
And the names of the Jewish children and the countries in which they died.
Ukraine is also mentioned there.
doing something for health
Israel's three most important recreation areas lie on the shores of the Mediterranean Sea, the Red Sea and the Dead Sea.
In each of the three places there are swimming pools, water parks, dolphinariums and oceanariums.
Surprisingly, in the Red Sea is also bathed during the winter months, as the water temperature there does not fall below 21 °C and the air warms up to 23 °C.
The Dead Sea is even warmer and is bathed throughout the year.
By the way, this unusual sea with a location of 417 meters below sea sea level is the deepest sea in the world.
Its turquoise blue water has a high salinity and even carries non-swimmers easily on the surface.
The surrounding countryside is indescribably beautiful.
Visitors come here for treatments using salt water (natural salt brine) and healing mud as well as for the treatment of skin inflammation, allergies, asthma, eczema, arthritis, bronchitis, diabetes and to soothing strained nerves.
Touch the secrets of antiquity
These mysteries hold the old town of Tel Aviv, namely the city of Jaffa on the Mediterranean coast.
Through this city ran the famous sea route that connected Egypt, Syria, Anatolia and Mesopotamia.
The city is mentioned in ancient Greek and ancient Egyptian legends.
According to tradition, Noah built his ark just here, and Perseus saved the beautiful Andromeda here, with which he led a long and happy life in this place.
Tourists like to stroll through the narrow alleys, which are named after zodiac signs.
Fortuna is said to be according to all the hold that touch the walls in the alley with their zodiac sign.
In Jaffa, you can meet newlywed couples from all over Israel and even other countries at photo shoots.
In Caesarea, the city of King Herod, you can roam through the Roman theater and conquer the fortress of the Crusaders.
In the Roman period, Caesarea was the most important city of Judea and residency of the Roman governors, including Pontius Pilate.
The carefully restored theater performs evening concerts and operas today.
Wishlist for tourists
If you're going to Israel, don't worry about your poor English proficiencies: About 30% of Israelis speak Russian.
The money in the trip you prefer not to take with you in euros, but in dollars, which can simply be changed into shekels (current exchange rate: 1 dollar to 3.8 shekels).
In the city you usually advance by bus, but in Jerusalem there is also an express trail and in Haifa the only metro line in the country with six stations that connects the lower city with the upper city.
Strictly speaking, it is an underground funicular.
A trip within the city costs 6 shekels regardless of the means of transport, which is enough including changing trains for a journey of up to 1.5 hours.
According to Jewish tradio, the Sabbath is celebrated in Israel on Saturdays.
Markets and shops are closed from Friday evening to sunset on Saturdays, and public transport rests.
And on Sunday morning the working week begins.
Many cafes, restaurants and restaurants only serve kosher food, which excludes pork, seafood products, fish without sheds and food that contains both dairy and meat products.
Instead, you have the choice between different dishes with mutton and beef, soups and desserts (prepared with coconut milk), traditional Jewish humus paste, various sauces, fried balls of pureed beans and peas (falafel) as well as fruits and vegetables.
There are no stray dogs in the streets of Israeli cities.
For this, there are many well-nourished cats straying comfortably and unmolested through the streets.
In the evening you can even see them sleeping on the roofs of parked cars.
The cats like busy places and don't say no to treats.
Depending on the brand, a rental car costs between $37 per day (Hyundai Getz) to $188 (Audi A6, Volvo S80).
Insurance costs an additional $15 per day.
And bicycle rental costs 15 shekels a day.
Entrance tickets to museums cost an average of 30 shekels.
In numbers
In 2012, more than three million tourists from all over the world visited Israel.
Most adventurers and recreation seekers came from Russia, France, Germany, Italy, England, the USA and Ukraine.
From January to October 2012, 118.8 thouand Ukrainian tourists entered the Promised Country, which corresponds to an increase of 51% compared to the comparable period in 2010, before the visa requirement was lifted on 9 February 2011.
'The big and mighty' leads to Moscow: migrants save money for language
While MPs and civil rights activists are discussing what benefits the law brings about binding language tests for migrants, criminals are already dealing with fake certificates
Every year, 13 million guest workers come to Moscow, Saint Petersburg and other cities of Russia.
Most of them are the citizens of Central Asia: Uzbekistan, Tajikistan and Turkmenistan.
Their only goal is to make money for the upkeep of families remaining in the homeland.
On December 1, a law came into force, according to which every migrant worker must take a test certifying knowledge of the Russian language.
Until now, this scheme only applies to migrants wishing to work in the service sector, in the municipal housing industry and in the retail sector.
However, according to the Federal Migration Authority, the tests should become mandatory for all guest workers in the future.
Not only language tests, but also tests in Russian history and the basics of legislation have to be taken.
Language proficiencies must be demonstrated both for the receipt and the extension of the work permit.
Exceptions apply only to citizens of countries where Russian is an official language.
Also excluded from this law are persons who received their training certificates and diplomas prior to the disintegration of the USSR in 1991.
Meanings, problems and protection of rights
The entirety of the Examination Offices will stand under the aegis of the Pushkin Institute for the Russian Language, the Russian University of People's Friendship, the Moscow State University, the Saint Petersburg State University and other Russian universities.
Migrants can take the tests in all cities; more than 160 corresponding centres have already been opened in Russia.
The initiative to introduce the tests was supported by deputies from the State Duma and the Federal Migration Authority.
Civil rights activists, on the other hand, asked the following question several times before the law came into force in the press: What does the whole thing actually bring?
What changes does the requirement to have language skills bring to both the Russians and the guest workers?
Above all, said spokespersons for the migration authority, it is about reducing the number of forced labourers.
"Many are now talking about protecting the rights of migrant workers," says Viktor Sebelev, head of representation of the Federal Migration Agency in Tajikistan.
However, their rights must already be protected before their departure.
Only organized recruitment will 90% solve the problems of foreign workers.
Migrants without a profession, education, language skills and medical evaluation encounter problems.
Sebelev is convinced that a future migrant without language skills will inevitably meet people with evil intentions who put him under the pretext of help in a dark, tight barrack, where he will endure with dozens of his equal months without food and documents and work 12 to 14 hours a day.
We receive a lot of complaints from our migrants.
"In the homeland you will be promised one thing, and on arrival you will be cheated, your passports are taken and do not receive the promised wages," confirms the head of the head of the headquarters for labour migration of the Tajik migration authority, Tolib Swharipov.
Don't scold, boss!
Nevertheless, many citizens of the Central Asian republics, who want to earn their money in Russia, admit that they not only do not master the language of the destination country, but can hardly express themselves in writing in their own language.
Of course, this is not so much their fault, but rather the evil: only few Turkmen, Uzbeks and Tajiks can only afford a reasonable elementary education.
Because in their families, there isn't even money to feed the kids, let alone buy them decent clothes, shoes and school supplies.
As teenagers, these children immediately seize the first opportunity to make money.
"It's difficult without language skills," they admit.
“You feel humiliated and inferior.”
Civil rights activists emphasize the following important aspect regarding the law.
Only migrants with legal status have to take the test.
If you do not have this status, there is no examination and consequently no official work.
Meanwhile, the majority of the guest workers continue to reside illegally in Russia.
"Welcome" or "Accession prohibited for unauthorised persons"
Many migrants confirm that it is not easy to obtain official status in Russia.
The reason are bureaucratic hurdles and, of course, linguistic difficulties.
In addition, legalization costs money: 12 to 16 thousand rubles.
A fake registration can be made quickly and only for one and a half thousand.
The employees of the Russian police know that our documents are mostly falsified and we are unregistered, and from this then cases of extortion arise.
"They want one or two hundred rubles for cigarettes and tea," Tajike Umed Hushkadamov told journalists.
“Just don’t shy away from costs for false paperies”
Already on the day the law came into force, it turned out that not only the migrants' registration documents can be forged.
Some forged certificates, which certify the completion of the language test, have already been seized by employees of the Federal Migration Agency.
The faked papers were printed with an ordinary color printer.
Of course, the new owners did not receive them for nothing: every migrant who hoped to be able to spare himself the tests paid seven thousand rubles for the document.
That's more than twice as much as the cost for the official test: it costs a migrant worker three-thousand rubles.
Both officials and civil rights activists agree that the most important immediate task is to protect the system from corruption so that the certificates cannot simply be purchased.
For the moment, the authorities can only promise guest workers who fall through the test for the first time that they will be given time for a basic language course.
In addition, newcomers without language skills are offered positions in areas where active communication with people is not necessary.
Interior Ministry ignorating illegal arms market
Proportion of legal weapons in crimes extremely low
Russia's Interior Ministry is planning to tighten the gun law for private gun owners.
Authorities are responding to recent events: Injured in shootings at weddings as well as seven deaths in the rampage of Moscow-based lawyer Dmitry Vinogradov.
The police want to ban the carrying of non-lethal weapons in public and raise the age for the possession of firearms from 18 to 21 years.
The chairman of the Duma Committee on Security and Anti-Corruption, Irina Yarovaya, has backed the proposal and promised that the amendments to the Weapons Act will be submitted to the Duma shortly.
Not everyone is satisfied that the Russian authorities want to combat the problems by "attracing the screws."
An open letter has been published on the Internet, the author - representatives of various arms associations - calling on the authorities to refrain from "senseless intensifications".
The percentage of crimes with registered weapons is very small, the criminal lawyer, Vasily Lesnikov, told the Russian service of the BBC.
According to statistics from the Ministry of the Interior, 142 crimes with the application of registered weapons were committed over the course of six months in 2012, while a total of 1.168 thousand offences were recorded for the same period.
The authors of the open letter are convinced that tightening the law for private gun owners will not deter criminals from making use on the black market.
Currently, according to the authors, you can find every possible weapon for little money in Moscow.
Meanwhile, the Interior Ministry stresses that the proliferation of illegal weapons is under scrutiny.
suppliers: from the factories to the officers
The weapons enter the black market through several channels.
There are five such main channels, recounts a former Colonel Viktor Baranez, who spent ten years working in the Ministry of Education and the General Staff.
screenshot of a web page that accepts gun orders
The first canal is the army or the military, i.e. weapons that were stolen during battles in the Caucasus.
"The weapons were stolen by both the Russian officers and the Caucasians themselves," Baranez says.
On the second canal comes illegal weapons stolen by law enforcement by criminals.
Baranez explains that this includes weapons that have been stolen both from the police's armory and directly from law enforcement personnel.
Other illegally sold weapons come from military weapons warehouses.
In many military camps, explosions often occurred.
"There's evidence that some cases were arson to cover up the absence of weapons," the former military member says.
According to Baranez, arms manufacturers also make a contribution to the black market.
"Today, there are countless private arms factories that cannot withstand international competition and therefore squander the weapons under hand on the black market, for example in Moscow," reports the expert.
Another channel of the black market is smuggling.
Especially many pistols and rifles come from poor countries like Kyrgyzstan.
"There is its own, sometimes artisanal production and a mafia that organizes smuggling," explains the former military member.
Where did the guns come from?
Experts calculated what proportion of each of the five canals in supplying the black market with illegal weapons.
In 2011, a report was written by the Center for Problem Analysis and State Administration.
The experts analyzed reports from the Ministry of the Interior and the Federal Service for State Statistics, Criminological Literature, and publicly available data on portals on the subject of weapons.
The vast majority of illegal weapons, according to investigators, comes from the army and legal protection authorities.
"Half of all weapons on the black market are due to "the fault of those responsible who are involved in weapons in their work," the report said.
According to the data of the researchers, 17% of the weapons come from conflict zones, 14% are due to thefts in weapons production, and 5% come from illegal excavations.
A sales consultant to an arms store, who does not want to be named, assures that weapons found during illegal excavations have long ceased to buy because they are outdated.
According to him, it is more common for traders to get the supplies in a weapons store.
A weapon, for example a TT-type pistol, is purchased via an ensign.
The weapon is exhibited and handed through the fence.
"The trader brings the weapon to the city and sells it there for 900 euros apiece with two magazines," he recounts
"Of course, the police are aware of everything and, of course, if they are unable to comply with the plan to detect crime, periodically carries out control purchases at merchants who offer illegal weapons," the sales adviser adds.
"Like in the luxury shop"
Buyers and sellers find most often about acquaintances to each other.
I had looked at websites and blogs when someone became aware of me and suggested me to come to "Begowajah", where a person would be waiting for me who would take me to a certain place where we could then negotiate the price.
The price for the gun I only found out there
Military commentator Viktor Baranez
"In order to get a gun, I need an acquaintance who has contacts," says the sales consultant. "I have such an acquaintance, but I don't trust him."
There are traders in the markets, but that's where you have to go and date with a code like "by Ivan Ivanovich, who has aligned that the daughter has lost a tooth".
At the moment, I even come up with some striking rings about an acquaintance I trust.
He's giving me the batting rings only because he knows I'm not rating him out.
Newcomers are looking for weapons in various ways.
Former military member Viktor Baranez attempted a buyer of illegal weapons in the mid-1990s when he wrote an article on the subject.
The schemes, according to him, have not changed.
He was given a photo album depicting "everything possible."
"I felt like I was in the luxury store," the military member recalls.
According to Baranez, the buyer does not get the cat in the bag, but can try everything.
As a potential customer, I don’t just buy, but drive into the forest with the seller, and that’s where we set up a target.
"I can then shoot there, and if I'm convinced that the weapon has a good shot, we negotiate with each other," the expert describes the procedure.
Shoping from the sofa
Internet search engines show search results for websites and groups in "Vkontakte", where weapons are offered "for various purposes".
There aren't any papers needed nor there are face-to-face meetings.
"You only need the appropriate sum of money," reads the advertisement on the "buying battle gun or rifle."
There, users place their orders and make requests.
Will it also be sold to minors?
"Of course without a license," asks user "Iwan" (literal quote).
"Buy a TT. Moscow," is the laconic request from "Fedorenkov."
The Federal Security Service recently switched countless mock websites, and buyers who want to acquire guns are going to the net at the service.
People come like hungry fish at feeding and then land in Siberia and are allowed to shovel coal
Military commentator and former military member Viktor Baranez
I've heard about the following scheme: The website is normally registered abroad, and Russian laws do not apply.
Those responsible accept the orders.
The buyer transfers the money through an ATM.
"In response, he receives a photo and a description of the hiding place of the weapon," reports the press secretary of the non-profit organization "Pravo na oryzhie" Dmitrij Kislow.
Viktor Baranez emphasizes that in the end, you can also go out empty and get behind bars if you place a request on a website.
The Federal Security Service recently switched countless mock websites, and buyers who want to acquire guns are going to the net at the service.
"People come like hungry fish at feeding and then end up in the Siberian labour camp and are allowed to shovel coal," he says.
A Makarov for $100
According to experts, when buying illegal firearms, prices ranging from $100 to $900 are expected.
According to Dmitry Kislow of the organization "Pravo na oryzhie" you can buy a Makarov-type pistol for $100 to $300.
You have to wait up to a month and a half.
The weapon is dispensed in military weapons warehouses by middle-range supervisors.
According to the official statistics of the Interior Ministry, the number of offences involving illicit weapons across Russia declined by 7% to 22.9 thousand in the period from January to October 2011, while the number of uncovered cases of theft and extortion related to weapons, ammunition, explosives and explosive devices shrank by 7.8%.
Fast-food and supermarket workers go on strike in the U.S.
One in four U.S. teens will eventually sit behind Mc Donald's coffers
In recent days, there have been massive protests across the U.S. against low-wages in stores of Walmart and restaurants of popular fast-food chains such as Mc Donald's, Burger King, Taco Bell, Wendy's and Kentucky Fried Chicken.
No one can yet predict whether the protests will swell even further or whether they will run out of air soon.
The protests are backed by unions and several left-wing nonprofits.
In addition to increasing the modest wages of ordinary Walmart and fast-food workers, organizers of the protests are pursuing the goal of creating unions.
So far, this industry of the economy is hardly unionized.
46 cents a year?
The protests began last week after Thanksgiving, on so-called Black Friday, on which sell-outs took place in the U.S. that attracted millions of customers, at times leading to handcuffs.
On this day, some Walmart employees, of whom there are 2.2 million worldwide, put down their jobs and, along with unions and left-wing activists, smashed branches of the chain, whose targets are low and middle-income individuals.
Walmart sells all products, from diapers to hunting rifles and car batteries to vacuum cleaners, eggs and milk.
On average, goods in the Walmart stores are between 8 and 27% cheaper than in large supermarkets.
That's why many of the marginally paid Walmart workers only shop at their own workplace.
The prices and range of goods made Walmart one of the largest companies in the United States.
According to critics, Walmart can often sell the goods so cheaply only because the employees are poorly paid.
The latter also complain of heavy working conditions, for example missing forklifts or handheld scanners.
Participants in the "Black Friday" protests demanded wage increases and voiced their annoyance that the contribution for the company's own health insurance had risen from €30 to $100 a month.
A typical Walmart employee earning $9.5 an hour couldn't afford that increase, they said.
Researchers from the University of California at Berkeley prove that if the company raises the average wage to $12 an hour, it would cost Walmart $3.2 billion.
That's about 1.1% more than they're donating for wages now.
If Walmart were to transfer the wage increases entirely to the customers, each purchase would cost them only 46 cents more in the future.
Per year, that would be additional expenses of only $12.39 compared to today.
Walmart’s proponents are pleased that there were only protests in nine states and that they did not result in any casualties.
Black Friday lasted the next day at its store from 8 p.m. on Thursday until midnight, and during that period Walmart sold about 5000 items per second.
In total, Walmart coffers carried out nearly 100 million transactions on "Black Friday."
The company's spokesman, Dan Fogelman, claimed in an interview on the left-leaning Huffington Post's website that in total "fewer than five" Walmart employees put down the work and the protest action had been merely "another PR trick" of the union organizing the protests.
"Free cash register!"
The protests continued this week in New York, where their target, however, wasn't Walmart stores (which aren't desirable in this progressive city and therefore so far non-existent), but McDonald's and other low-priced restaurants.
McDonald's says the company sells billions of portions, and you're not being granted sick days, and you won't get paid for your honest work!
Jumaane Williams, member of the New York City Council
Currently, according to federal and New York legislation, the minimum wage is $7.25 per hour.
In fast-food restaurants, the minimum wage is gradually raised, but not too much, and on average, their ordinary employees in New York get $8.90 an hour.
Less than her, no one deserves in this expensive city.
How it's possible to get along with so little money in New York is incomprehensible.
There was once a time in which almost a quarter of all U.S. young people stood behind the counter after school at McDonald's, but the youths were still living with their parents.
Hardly anyone saw the main source of income or planned to stay there any longer.
Now, during interviews with McDonald's employees, I'm constantly encountering people complaining that they're getting along with this wage and sometimes even having to feed their children.
On the other hand, there is a commentation in the Wall Street Journal’s forum, whose author notes that it is irresponsible to have children if you don’t know how to feed them.
Participants in the protests, which began at 6.30 a.m. outside McDonald's on 40th Street and Madison Avenue on Thursday, demanded that cashiers and chefs of fast-food restaurants receive a minimum wage of $15 an hour, more than a doubling of the current minimum wage.
They continued to call for the formation of unions in the fast-food industry.
U.S. law forbids corporate governance to stand in the way of this goal and warn or dismiss union members.
On the other hand, corporate governance doesn't make it easy for them.
However, establishing trade unions in the fast-food industry is also difficult for objective reasons.
The most important reason is the high staff fluctuation.
Disatisfied
Loud protests also took place in front of many other fast-food restaurants in Manhattan that day.
The culmination of the protests was a gathering at noon outside McDonald's in Times Square where some local Democrats spoke, including City Council member Jumaane Williams, who said: "McDonald's says it's selling billions of portions, and yet you're not granted sick days, and you won't get paid for your honest work!"
The protesters were also backed by other prominent New York Democrats, for example, mayoral candidate Bill De Blasio, who said that "we should collectively support the fast-food employees so that they can receive a fair wage and achieve the economic prosperity that every New Yorker deserves!".
According to the New York Times, this was the largest action of its kind in the history of the U.S. fast-food industry.
However, only a few hundred people were on the ground, not all of whom are employed in the fast-food industry where tens of thousands work in New York.
It is not yet clear whether the spark of these protests will mobilize entire masses.
“The brain can only be moderately outsmarted so far”
The theme of augmented reality, which is popular with supporters of modern technologies, has been usually perceived recently by the lens of special glasses.
First, Google presented a working model at its annual conference in the summer, in November it became known that Microsoft had also filed a corresponding patent.
Speaking to the head of the group for interactive 3D technologies at the Microsoft laboratory in Cambridge Shahram Izadi, however, it becomes clear that the glasses were only a transit station for the company's scientists.
It stimulates the possibility to manipulate virtual objects in the air with bare hands and to create virtual open spaces.
- Please briefly describe what research your group is doing.
- We deal with the interaction of man and machine, but we want to extend the limits of this interaction.
Until now, humanity has largely been stuck in the phase of working with pixels on flat screens, which are also sometimes touched.
However, we want to look 5-10 years ahead and foresee the radical changes of this interaction.
For example, the Xbox console and Kinect sensors are an advance, and these days no Xbox is sold without Kinect because everyone is interested in motion control.
- What awaits us in the future?
- Although Kinect has brought the interaction to a physical level, a lot of it still takes place on the flat screen, sometimes in 3D.
The input of information (the system receives more data) could be improved, the output not yet decisive.
We are trying to change that, working on true three-dimensional representation systems based on various technologies, including projections.
We need to release the computer world into our physical world and make it more comprehensible.
For this, however, it is necessary that not only the user, but also his environment is recognized.
Then we could complement the real world much more easily with virtual objects.
And first of all, we should get rid of these stupid virtual reality helmets!
- What do you think about voice control?
She's popular, but isn't she overvalued?
It is certainly not the all-purpose solution, as it raises questions about privacy, because you don’t always want to inform all bystanders about your actions and intentions.
Actually, any kind of interaction with computers is good, but each in its particular niche.
For example, in the field of controlling objects in public places, we worked on a project where we experimented with short and scarce gestures instead of sprawling gestures.
The gestures were not recorded by the object camera, but by a bracelet that registered the movements of the bones and muscles.
It is still relatively large, but could theoretically be reduced to the size of a wristwatch.
But overall, however, the future belongs to mixed control types, for example gesture and language.
- Like that?
- We'd ask me, for example, to hand you this bubble box?
They say it and at the same time point to it.
- I'll usually just say it.
Oh, that'd be very hard to see.
- Does this mean that you want the user to adapt to what a machine can and can't?
Not necessarily, but it's about a mutual approximation.
I think that in the near future we will have to work primarily on the development of new sensor types that make it possible to capture responses from people more accurately.
For example, these can be laser sensors, since they offer a good resolution at depth, which is very important.
- What demands do you place on modern cameras based on your work with Xbox Kinect sensors?
Is there a lack of dissolution, depth or something else?
The current generation is basically a good starting point for our work in the field of three-dimensional recognition.
Of course, 8 megapixels with a speed of 1000 fps wouldn't be bad.
But the main thing is not the megapixels themselves, but the quality of the matrix and the depth.
From this point of view, all the current technologies are not good enough for us, as the developers of the algorithms have more work.
Not only the resolution along the X and the Y axis must be taken into account, but also along the Z axis.
The speed and the number of frames per second are also important.
The movements of man are extremely dynamic and frank, the current 30 fps are too little, especially for gestures.
Stephen Betishes developed a contact sensor in our Redmond laboratory with an adjustable processing delay between 1 and 100 milliseconds, with today's commercial sensors closer to the second value (60 to 100).
Not everyone understands how much this influences the interaction between man and machine.
For my work I needed just such a device, only without touch control and with more frames per second.
- Wouldn't the number of cameras have to be increased?
Kinect is currently working with three "cameras", one of which is actually an infrared transmitter and the second is a receiver for the reflected signal.
And the third camera is actually an ordinary RGB camera.
It is not used to calculate the object depth.
More cameras could potentially solve the problem...
Or intensify the problem as more calculations would be necessary.
Nice would be a flexible, Kinect-like system, where we could play with different camera inclinations to determine how this helps us with the three-dimensional determination of a position.
If I remember correctly, Microsoft, unlike Google, didn’t present its glasses to the public.
Don't you think that with regard to the application of augmented reality technology in everyday life, glasses have the best prospects?
- Of course, it's not pleasant to always hold a smartphone in your raised hand on the way, but I think the following: The most amazing variant would be a "changing" augmented reality, in which we could switch from the glasses to the smartphone via a cloud,
The glasses are a personal gadget and therein lies their strength (Privates can only see), but at the same time their weakness, because an extended reality by means of glasses prevents you from working on virtual objects together with others.
Let’s imagine for a moment that the manipulation of virtual holograms in the air is not only Tony Stark from Iron Man, but also ordinary mortals.
There is one problem that critics frequently point out to this idea: there is no palpable reaction!
The hands don't feel anything at all!
What answers does your group want to give to this objection?
- In my lectures, I often talk that augmented reality is the seventh breakthrough in human-machine interaction.
I think the eighth breakthrough could well become the integration of the tactile perception.
An interesting method right now is the use of the second hand as a somewhat unusual projection pad.
She perceives pressure superbly!
However, there are also technologies that are actually designed to give the "images in the air" something tangible, for example, the interference of some directed ultrasonic beams at a certain point, where the finger is located, which at the moment, however, is still as weak as if someone was blowing over the palms.
There are also ligaments for the wrist that act on the nerve endings in the fingers, this is also a potential direction.
Have you tried to outsmart the brain?
Make it think that it feels what it should feel when it sees something right now?
- That's a good idea, we haven't tried that way.
Here hides another task, with which we will struggle for a long time, namely to make a person who is physically in an enclosed space to believe that he is instead in an open, practically infinite space; here we work on concepts with treadmills (other than in gyms), movable platforms and large spheres.
So far, the brain can only be moderately outsmarted, and there are still many busy years ahead.
This is precisely what makes the work in the field of virtual reality so appealing for a researcher: On many topics, we are only at the beginning.
Own taste instead of culture - rosbalt.ru
Rosbalt continues the "Petersburg Avant-Garde" project dedicated to city dwellers who form the avant-garde of art and culture.
On the exquisite list, high-ranking representatives of the Petersburg art scene, whose successes go beyond the borders of the city and who often find recognition in Europe without being known in the rest of Russia.
Rosbalt's new hero is flamboyant artist Kirill Miller.
The whole city knows Kirill Miller, the beardier dressed in red, whom you can sometimes meet at the Russian Museum, sometimes at the summer garden, sometimes at scene meetings or at vernissages.
Wherever the pictures of Kirill Miller are exhibited, they attract large crowds.
Kirill Miller is one of the typical Petersburg social-philosophical artists creating new myths.
Kirill Miller is a shining personality of the Petersburg avant-garde in the late 80s or early 90s.
He is also a man of the city and conjures a smile on the face of his fellow citizens through his presence on the street and ensures a good mood for them.
He recently grabbed a revolving organ and became a Petersburg revolving organ player as he finally felt ready for this difficult role in terms of his artistic existence, his philosophy and his image.
Kirill, why are you always in red and, for example, yellow or turquoise in the city?
I've chosen the color red as a fashion designer who deals with the optics and image.
In this world, red is the compromise between artist, image consultant and society.
In society, everything that differs somewhat from the grey norm provokes aggression and bad behavior.
However, I want to talk to people through my provocations.
Each of my past provocations was an invitation to talk.
When did you know you had to become a painter?
- At an exhibition in the Palace of Culture of Nevesky, where my works were exhibited.
I realized that I had to go down this path.
Then the time of the old free but not officially recognized painters came to an end at that time, and new and free painters like me were incomprehensible.
I befriended the painters of the new generation, i.e. with the representatives of the "Gazonewschina"; from this "Pushkinskaya 10" arose, and then it was over.
It draws me to theatre, to fashion, to music, actually to everything, except literature.
- And all of this was united in your art-clinik... - It was important to me to be at the heart of Petersburg's culture, where the best-creating artists were supposed to gather.
In 1995 I occupied a room in Pushkinskaya 10, and until the renovations there was a musical-artistic club for the bohemians, the house of the Bohemians.
Many were born there: N.O.M., Tequilajazzz, and I also remember how Schnur got there with his project "Ucho Van Goga."
Schnur and his friends sang simple songs for the play-back, wore tight jerseys and they also included the composer Igor Vdowin, who was trendy today.
When the group started playing live, it became "Leningrad."
Trachtenberg moderated many programs before the time of "Halli-Galli."
We gave them Trachtenberg, and it began his great career, but he learned the basics with us.
The gallery "D 137" or the club "Griboedov" also have their origins in the art clinic.
That's where our employees and regular guests went.
I'm a hero of the last century, when culture still meant something.
In 2000, there was a survey in the press about the "personalities of our city" award.
I won in the "Artist of the Year" category, and with that I had reached the climax.
It is not easy to work according to old rules in new times; I am a man of the truth, honesty and culture of the past century.
In modern times, it’s easy to become famous, but culture and popularity aren’t the same: you can be famous without making a cultural contribution.
Your works have an unmistakable style.
- Many of my paintings are real pearls and clearly express the topicality and sharpness of thoughts.
I'm going to have an exhibition with the theme "The Russian Museum as Clowns."
Clowns are timeless.
I used to be social, but now it hurts and is afraid to be like that.
In a clown, this blurs everything and loses its tragedy.
I love the grotesque, and I have grotesque ideas.
For example, let’s save the world by prescribing that everyone has to dress up completely.
Now everyone pays attention to their clothes and orientates themselves on the outside and not on the interior of man.
But maybe you can't shake your hand with a person, you should spit in his face.
The deception disappears by dressing up.
- Recently, we saw you in the role of the urban organ player. - A cultural city should have such a hero.
Who, if not me?
- Can commercial art be beautiful too?
These days, commercial art must be careful, considerate and flattering.
We see the disintegration of cultures.
In the past, people formed large groups: the bohemians loved one thing and the common people loved the other.
Now there are microcommunities, and it's difficult to please everyone.
I'm not a one-hundred-dollar note that everyone likes.
Now you have to think about who you're going to like.
Now every cult star has about 100 followers.
- The performances of Stas Mikhailov come several thousand!
Abandoned women come to him, where the sexual-social sphere plays a role.
For cultural reasons come 300 people and not 10 thousand, consequently there is less management, money, and everything perishes.
I have followers, and the most important thing is not deceiving them and not destroy what I have achieved.
In my youth, I painted such pictures, which even hung with a collector in a row with the works of Falk and Larionow.
I started with pictures that usually don't come until the end of your career.
There are often misunderstandings these days.
It's called spiritual culture and consumer culture.
Consumption has no culture at all, that's completely different.
I am a person of the past culture and grew up with artists who lived in poverty and died in poverty and refused money to be able to paint.
This is the culture I advocate.
Kirill, what's what's Petersburg lacking?
- Good cultural experts.
They say servants of culture.
However, not all of them can deal with culture.
It was different with the real Tsars, because the Tsars might not understand much about culture, but they understood that proper experts are necessary.
There are currently good advisors in Moscow.
In Petersburg, we have those who could be experts, but they will be blockeded because more high-ranking experts are needed to evaluate the former correctly and pave the way.
Now only one's own taste counts.
The same is true for Erarta, but with the difference that they honestly say that they don’t all embrace contemporary art and some painters therefore have to look for other museums.
- What's Petersburg for you?
Petersburg is not the cultural capital; there is much more culture and a corresponding breeding ground in Moscow.
On our stones, art is difficult to grow.
We need a cultural breeding ground, because, for example, we now have more writers than readers, and that’s not right.
In Europe, there are a lot of curious people attending exhibitions or concerts.
With us, that's much less.
Art must be modern again, just like at the beginning of the previous century.
The project is funded from grants from the City of Saint Petersburg
Birth in space
earth in danger.
global warming or collision with killer asteroid.
Caravans of spaceships with Earth dwellers on a search for a replacement planet.
To save humanity, the important question arises of how we can procreate in weightlessness or on the replacement planet?
There aren't many choices, in my opinion.
In fact, there are only two real planets that we could at least hypothetically inhabit.
"Namally Mars and Venus," says the research associate of the Astronomical Sternberg Institute Vladimir Surdin.
However, while the conditions on Mars are comparatively life-friendly, there is a temperature of 500 degrees on the surface of Venus.
Life would only be possible at high altitude or the orbit of Venus, i.e. in space.
The researchers first dealt with the question of procreation in space with regard to flora.
Already half a century before, experiments with plants were carried out.
The fourth generation of peas bred in orbit differed not in the slightest from the earthly species.
This was followed by the breeding of insects, more precisely from tiny fruit flies (Drosophila), in orbit.
In 1979, quail eggs were sent into space to explore how microgravity affects the development of embryos.
A normal chick is developing.
But then the problem begins.
"The problem is that the chick has to find support; it has to be able to stand on its feet and start to move," explains Vladimir Sychew, head of the Institute of Medical-Biological Problems.
Without a solid surface, the chicks skimmed wildly.
Ten hours later, the instincts of the newborns blunted off completely.
The chicks didn't react to light and sounds.
Four days later, they simply died.
"Twice we let chicks hatch and then set the whole thing because it's just impossible to work with them there," Vladimir Sychev notes for the failed experiment with the chicks in space.
The last breeding cage with animals flew into orbit 16 years ago.
The experiments will continue in the spring of 2013.
However, only same-sex species will be located on the biosatellite "Bion".
There was an experiment with rats that have already been sent fertilized into space.
In principle, nothing special has been identified.
"That was just with the biosatellites, but again it was only a single experiment, and the research needs to be continued," notes Vladimir Sychev.
After landing, the space rats got offspring.
The problem of reproduction directly in space could not yet be solved.
The task is difficult.
Animals ripped from their natural habitat simply cannot follow their reproductive drive.
In contrast to animals, humans could do that in principle.
Homo sapiens is capable of abstract thinking and can create a similar emotional background.
Out of ethical consideration, such experiments are not carried out.
However, women have been flying into space for 50 years.
The greatest risk was at Tereshkova.
The most valuable that humanity possesses is the female organism.
Our seagull flew away, and no one on Earth could have answered the following question: Does it survive space flight?
Will she be able to give birth to a child after this flight?
"After all, no one answered this question," says space veteran Wachtang Watschnadze.
In June 1964, just one year after her space flight, the first female cosmonaut, Valentina Tereshkova, gave birth to a daughter.
The child's father, Andriyan Nikolayev, was also a cosmonaut.
In 1988, the second woman in space, Svetlana Savitskaya, who was in orbit twice and even did work outside the spacecraft, gave birth to a son.
Nevertheless, there remains a risk.
We have very few cosmonauts, where everything went well after long flights and they got healthy offspring.
"The more, the more dangerous it is even on flights into orbit," concludes cosmonaut Valeri Polyakov, hero of the Soviet Union and hero of the Russian Federation.
And yet humanity must look for new ways in biotechnology, radiation protection and producing artificial gravity.
The hydro laboratory in the Cosmonaut Preparatory Center is a mandatory station during flight preparation.
Here in a limbo, the cosmonauts are gaininging routine for working in open space.
Water represents weightlessness.
Even though adults perceive water as pleasant, it is a foreign environment, but infants perceive water as a natural element.
It's as if the little Ichtyanders testify that life on the land descends from the ocean.
There is a correlation between the fact that a child finds himself in the mother's belly in the amniotic fluid for about 9 months, and that it can then get used to water more easily.
In principle, this is logical, since from the birth until the first swim, only 2 weeks pass.
"That's too little time to forget anything," says baby swimming instructor Marina Aksenowa.
Therefore, while for a newborn child weightlessness is a much more natural condition, the woman does not get along without gravity.
In the state of weightlessness, the abdominal and pelvic muscles repatriates enormously quickly and thus the ability to squeeze the child decreases.
Well, let’s get around this through birth-inducing aids.
The birth, for example, could also take place in a special chamber to squeeze out the child.
"But then what happens?" is Valeri Polyakov's not insignificant question.
On the other hand, the child also needs artificial gravity.
Without feeling the gravity, the organism cannot form the skeleton and muscles.
A newborn child cannot be put into an exercise suit like an adult for exercise in orbit.
He will simply lack what is of elementary importance to his life.
"This experiment, which we are doing together, namely the birth of a child in a foreign environment, will lead us to give birth to a disabled person, a completely inappropriate person," predicts Igor Pestow, head of the Bioethics Commission of the Institute for Medical-Biological Problems RAN.
So far, the birth of children in space is mere theory.
Eventually, however, it becomes a reality as the inhabitants of the Earth in their spaceships leave for a distant planet that will become the home for their offspring born in space.
NKÚ-chief: Control of the black system fails due to the fault of politics
In connection with public contracts, the Czech Republic has good control bodies and adequate laws, but falls back in their application.
Speaking for Aktuálně.cz, this is stated by the Vice-President of the Highest Control Office (Nejvyšší kontrolní úřad, NKÚ), Miloslav Kala.
"A law is never perfect, but it has to be applied properly - and I don't think it's the case with us.
A similar result seems to have been reached by a joint scrutiny by Czech and German examiners.
As a negative practical example, he cites Prime Minister Petr Neča's attitude to the so-called "black system".
Recently, the Prime Minister said that the ODS would not bother entrepreneurs with checks - so is the system now banned or allowed?
"A law must have its validity, and if something is prohibited by law, then the head of the government cannot simply come, and impede the control office, whose duty is to control compliance with the law, in his activity," Kala notes.
At the meeting of the control committee of the Chamber of Deputies, you talked about a joint Czech-German project, within the framework of which the laws of both countries regarding public contracts were compared.
What specifically was it about?
This is a parallel test that we started about two years ago.
To put it simply, it is a question that the European legislature determines how to handle public contracts. This is what national legislation builds on. And, finally, there is the real practical application.
We compared all this with each other, and although the test has not yet been completed, we were able to find extremely interesting differences. In general, the laws are perhaps even more "stricter and perfect", the actual application is better in Germany in some parameters.
This confirms that that it is not enough to always establish new and more precise rules. One must also deal with the actual application of law.
To what extent can the project help you? What does this project result for you from this project?
In particular, such joint control could help us to stop constantly striving to perfect the laws and to lower and balance thresholds, if this apparently does not have a particularly positive effect.
Profitability means acquiring a thing at a reasonable price (which is not always the lowest price) and excluding unlawful enrichment or even criminal acts.
But lowering thresholds for orders doesn't guarantee that we also achieve this goal.
Maybe it's even that in this way we only burden the system with excessive "paperwork," and those looking for gaps in the system, it's much easier to take advantage of the system than if the thresholds remained higher.
They talk about the problems in the practical implementation of the Czech Procurement Act.
How does the control system in Germany work?
Is there a similar authority to our NKÚ, or does the system work differently?
As far as the authorities are concerned, the German counterpart to the NKÚ is the Federal Court of Auditors, which is structured similarly with us and also has a college that is only formed somewhat differently. In principle, however, both authorities work similarly.
The competences are also comparable to a certain extent, although Germany is a federal state, i.e. that these courts also exist in the individual federal states - in which the German system differs from ours.
The BRH may only control federal funds, which corresponds to our state resources.
Public funds managed by the districts and municipalities are under the control of the State Audit Offices there.
As for German legislation, is it simpler than ours?
In general, I would reluctantly compare this without concrete information. Nevertheless, Germany is often referred to as a model in certain respects, although it is certainly not possible to say that Germany is better in everything.
Is this perhaps because the examiners are better there?
Certainly not, but as I said: I don’t particularly like such comparisons.
It should be noted that those countries which are a role model for us are also struggling with problems.
Otherwise, they wouldn’t have any cooperation with our authority, would they?
As far as Czech legislation is concerned, what was the significance of the amendment to the Procurement Act, do you already feel any impact?
Considering that this novella is only in force for a short time, it has not yet had an impact on our trials.
Since our inspections take place retrospectively, you have to expect a certain delay here.
So far, we are more likely to feel impact in the preparatory phase of future checks - so we have introduced the new "Recognistioning risks of unequiency" and examined and analysed almost 14,000 public contracts with this - the changes will of course have had an impact, as the thresholds change, the conditions for certain types of tenders have been modified, among others.
Do you see the passage of this law rather positively, or is the system burdened with further bureaucracy?
I believe that this law is certainly a step in the right direction and hope that this assumption will be confirmed.
However, the problem may arise that the rules become "stricter" and can no longer be adhered to.
Even under the previous rules, the persons subjected to an examination are accountable to the institution (for example, in the case of an operational regional policy programme, the Regional Council) that any non-compliance with the Procurement Act amounts to a violation of budgetary discipline.
But does it make sense to make the law even stricter?
In my opinion, not in this way.
The system must exclude those who want to attack and abuse the system, and not punish those who have committed a form error that does not affect actual decision-making.
Such a system merely puts bureaucracy under even greater pressure.
Where's the way out?
Let’s say first of all which path isn’t expedient.
The prime minister recently said that the ODS wouldn't bother entrepreneurs with controls on the so-called "black system" - what exactly does that mean?
Is the black system banned or permitted?
After all, a law must have its validity, and if something is prohibited by law, then the head of the government cannot simply come and obstruct the control office, whose duty is the control of observance of the law.
He can say, "We are changing the law and allowing the matter," but he can't say we're pretending to be nothing.
The Procurement Act contains relatively strict rules on the formal execution of certain processes - and this is also correct for the protection of public competition.
On the other hand, however, it is also tragic if the bidder with the best offer is excluded solely due to formal errors.
The law will never be perfect, but it must be applied properly - and that is what I think we lack.
On the roads there is occasional ice smoothness, but they are mostly dry.
On the roads of the Czech Republic there is partly sleek snow and ice smoothness.
However, the roads are mostly navigable, sometimes increased caution is required.
Karlovy Vary
In the district of Karlovy Vary, the roads were easily navigable this morning, occasionally there was sleezing snow and ice.
Temperatures dropped to -5 to -10 °C, during the day it's supposed to get a bit warmer.
In the region it is no longer snowing, and at the lower altitudes only a thin blanket of snow remains.
On the ridges of the Ore Mountains, however there is about 30 cm of snow.
According to the district road guard, there was limited visibility due to fog.
The R6 expressway and the traffic routes of the first road class in the region are currently unrestricted.
However, attention is needed, for example, to bridges on which the lanes are sometimes icy and smooth.
All traffic routes of the second and third road classes, including the mountain roads, are also passable.
On these roads, however, iced or extended scraps of snow remain in sections.
Increased attention is therefore required, especially in the higher elevations.
Pardubice and Hradec Králové
On some roads of Eastern Bohemia there is a danger because of ice smoothness, at higher altitudes and in the mountains on the roads there is sometimes still an extended layer of snow, informs the road and highway management on its websites.
The road masters warn drivers of ice smoothing, which can occur especially at higher altitudes in the Pardubice district.
Ice smoothness can occur in particular on traffic routes of the second and third street classes in the districts of Lanškroun, Ústí nad Orlicí, Polička, Svitavy and Vysoké Mýto.
Roads I/43 and I/34 at Svitavy are chemically treated.
Snow lies especially on the roads in the Giant Mountains and the Orlické hory mountains.
An extended layer of snow lies at high altitudes on the roads of Rychnov nad Kněžnou and Trutnov.
In Eastern Bohemia today it is mostly sunny or serene, and it remains largely rain-free.
Temperatures range between -3 and +1 °C, there is a weak wind.
Pilsen district
The roads in the district of Pilsen are easily navigable today, but there is increased local attention, and the driving style is adapted to the weather.
This is frosty in the morning, with temperatures between 3 and 9 degrees below freezing.
Since it snowed before and the temperatures fell afterwards, it can lead to ice smoothness on the roads.
In addition, fog is to be expected locally, although the visibility will improve in the course of the day.
This information comes from the district road masters.
The D5 motorway is virtually unrestricted navigable, but increased attention is recommended between the 80th and 131st kilometers.
The carriageways of the first class of road are mostly dry, but frozen.
In the south of the districts of Pilsen and Tachov, however, it can lead to sleet ice in sections.
The roads of the second and third road classes are humid, and therefore the reference to possible icy sections also applies here.
Drivers should be particularly careful mainly on the less frequented roads in the Böhmerwald area.
Olomouc district
Today drivers who are heading towards the altitudes in the Olomouc district have to reckon with slush.
After the use of scattered vehicles, slush remains on the pass Červenohorské sedlo and on the road towards Videlský kříž.
Nocturnal snow drifts forced the road masteries to use, in the district of Šumperk fell about 3 cm of fresh snow, according to the road administration.
In the other areas of the district, the roadways are usually unrestrictedly navigable.
"In Šumperk's district, there are still scraps of snow in the high altitudes.
On the pass Červenohorské sedlo, the drivers have to reckon towards Jeseník with slush," an employee of the Šumperk road administration told the agency ČTK today.
Their colleagues in Jeseník district also had to come out at night, according to their information, the roads are free and wet up to the altitudes after chemical treatment.
In Olomouc district, the roads are unrestrictedly passable, but in Šternberk district drivers have to drive cautiously in forest sections, as the lanes are still damp here.
Ústí nad Labem district, Liberec district
In Northern Bohemia, the road masteries report several poorly navigable road sections.
In addition to several places with snow or ice smoothness, the mountain road from Telnice to Knínice in the district Ústí n.K. is closed, according to the traffic police.
Temperatures remain below zero even at lower altitudes, so that snow and ice will stick to the roads. From the plains, especially southeast of the Bohemian Low Mountains, on the other hand, no problems are reported, and the roads there are usually dry.
So far, no traffic disruptions have been reported.
Ice smoothness is reported in particular from the periphery of Štětí.
According to meteorologists, there were ideal conditions for this at night - during the day rain and melting snow, cloudless skies and frost at night.
Poor traffic conditions are reported from the main routes as well as from road I/13 on the border of Ústí n.L. and Liberec districts.
The closure of the road between Telnice and Knínice was necessary due to branches pushed from the snow load to the carriageway.
Šimon Ornest: At our concerts we want to gather positive energy
What do you think that in less than a month the doomsday should take place?
This is one of those disaster reports that we humans like to jump on.
With our band The Tap Tap, we’re making fun of it and saying we’re the only band in the world that can gather enough positive energy to stop or prevent doom.
In December you will also realize a unique project with three concerts against the end of the world.
Can you introduce the project to our readers?
This is a nationwide benefit event that we have been planning for two years.
For this purpose, we want to use the marketing potential of the Mayan calendar, the end of which is predicted at 11:10 a.m.
The day before that, i.e. on 20 December, three concerts will take place simultaneously in Prague, Brno and Ostrava from 21:00.
The end of the concerts is supposed to be around the time when the Mayan calendar ends on the Pacific island of Kiribati, which is in a time zone around 12 hours ahead.
Whom did this idea come from?
Originally from me, then we designed the idea together with our graphic designer Honza Augusta.
Not only do we want to gather enough positive energy to prevent the end of the world, but we would also like to think together with the public about the state in which we once hand over this world to our children.
On the occasion of the end of the Mayan calendar, we have also developed a unique collection of shoes, T-shirts, bags and keychains against doomsday, with the purchase through www.e-tap.cz people can also support us.
Together with other performers, the band The Tap has also recorded the so-called hymn against the end of the world with the title "Doomsdaise fails".
The hymn is already very popular on YouTube today. Will it also resound at the benefit concerts?
Of course, at the very end, provided that everything works out and the end of the world does not take place...
The hymn is to be sung by all performers at all three concerts at the same time.
In addition, the hymns together are broadcast live by the Czech television in a direct circuit.
The text of the hymn comes from Tomáš Hanák, who also plays the main character of Jesus in the video clip, Xindl X also sings with...
How did you come to work with both of them?
We also cooperate with other personalities of the Czech cultural scene, which is related to the fact that we realize many benefit events and concerts...
We strive to involve these people intensively in our projects.
It has been shown that most are interested in collaborating with us and having fun.
What purpose does the proceeds of the concerts against the end of the world benefit?
For the establishment of the barrier-free educational center Studeo, where we have been working for six years together with the association Tap at the Jedlička Institute.
The students of the Jedlička Institute are regularly supervised by lecturers to help them with various leisure activities.
Since the students themselves do not have the means to visit the lecturers, we want to help them in this way.
As part of the completion of the Jedlička Institute, a separate building is to be built, into which we then move with this project.
At each concert, several groups and performers perform.
According to what criteria have you chosen them?
We wanted to put together a program that appeals to all generations including children.
In Prague, for example, Chinaski, Support Lesbien, Illustratosphere with Dan Bárta, The Tap Tap, Marián Bango and Jiří Suchý perform.
You can find out everything else on the website www.kpks.cz.
Are similar "large-scale events" also planned for the future?
In May we perform for the first time at the Prague Spring, and also for this concert we prepare an interesting program with interesting guests.
Next year we would also like to perform at the Czech House in New York, and I personally - if we are already in the USA - would also like to associate this with appearances in Washington and Chicago.
Your foreign plans aren’t exactly small, so far you’ve performed in Madrid, Brussels, London or Moscow.
However, The Tap Tap is a group made up of people with disabilities.
How do you manage these journeys logistically and organizationally?
It's not as bad as it seems at first glance.
Five of our members are wheelchair users, i.e. the wheelchairs have to be transported in the luggage compartment. In addition, of course, we have to carry many instrument cases and other luggage...
Nevertheless, it has always worked without any problems, ČSA and British Airways have always been very well prepared for us that I have sometimes already wondered.
Even in Moscow, where we were recently, there were no problems.
Through your trips abroad, you have a comparison of how it looks in the individual countries with accessibility, how to deal with disabled people there, etc.
How have you experienced these things so far?
After Madrid, Luxembourg, London and other cities where everything works better than with us, we witnessed in Russia that everything is still in its beginnings.
Opposite Prague, Moscow is absolutely non-disabled, where it is still unusual for someone in a wheelchair alone in the city centre.
It is not even a matter of course there that you leave a wheelchair user, for example, in the elevator.
Luckily, clubs are also being created there that try to point out the problems of people with disabilities.
And in what respect do we have to catch up with the more developed countries?
There are still a lot of things where we're hanging afterwards...
However, it should also be noted that an improvement in the situation always depends on the efforts of those affected.
In London and Madrid, it is absolutely customary for people with severe disabilities to move independently in public spaces, go to the museum, use toilets, etc.
However, it is not exactly customary for a larger group of disabled people to be actively involved in social events there, in which we are a little ahead of the West with the band The Tap Tap!
Respection in public and accessibility is one thing, but it's only when significant athletes, artists, actors, politicians or lawyers come from our ranks will things really start to change.
So far, these are only isolated cases of particularly strong-willed personalities.
The Tap is very popular at the moment, but if we go back a few years, what motivated you to start a band in 1998?
At that time I started as a supervisor at the Jedlička Institute, where I encountered many young people who were interested in dealing intensively.
And since I’m a musician myself and play saxophone, among other things, I started a music club together with a colleague.
Well, and over time, as our moderator, Láďa Angelovič, used to say, this is a bit out of control (laughs).
But the band only became known in the last few years, or am I wrong?
It’s true that working with well-known singers has helped us, and we’ve also started actively promoting ourselves.
We have become aware that if you do something and this cannot be seen on the outside, one’s own performance does not exist in a certain sense.
Thanks to EU funding, we can also afford good editors, high-quality devices and the like.
Was your goal to get this far with The Tap Tap?
From the very beginning, I felt the potential that you can approach things differently.
In show business today, one often copies the other.
This is logical in its own way, since everything new is viewed with caution and is only accepted after a long time.
There are few really unique things, and I dare say that Tap Tap belongs to it.
The first impression that the viewer usually has is, of course, pity; this is only natural...
But this pity is absolutely superfluous, because people with disabilities aren’t solitary and abandoned beings that we must regret.
They are people who can live and develop a full-fledged life, provided that they have the necessary resources.
I always say that if people with disabilities achieve something, this is not only progress for themselves, but for the whole of society.
Was it also helpful to this success that you're a strict boss, as you hear from a lot of people?
If you want to do something well, you are not allowed to compromise in many things and you also have to demand a certain discipline.
However, in my opinion this is completely normal.
Some come to us with romantic ideas, and when they realize that they have rehearsal twice a week, have to complete preparatory phases and spend a lot of time on concert tours, then the enthusiasm of many quickly elapses.
But that's how it works everywhere, in any band that wants to work seriously and achieve something.
Currently, The Tap Tap has twenty members.
How many of these have been there from the beginning, i.e. since 1998?
Only one: Láďa Angelovič.
We're an open group, people come and go, you can't prevent that.
Anyone who has the will and the desire to participate is always welcome with us.
Takes place on the day before the predicted doomsday, Thursday 20.12.2012 at 9 p.m.
Venues are: Incheba-Arena/Prague, Fléda/Brno and Plynojem/Ostrava; 12 Czech bands and other musicians
All three venues will finally be connected via live circuit and a joint interpretation of the anthem "Doomsday falls out" of the band The Tap Tap.
The aim of the concert is to raise the resources for equipping a multi-functional disability-friendly educational centre under the name STUDEO at the Jedlička-Institut in Prague.
Admission costs 400 CZK, children up to 12 years free of charge, tickets are available in advance at Bohemiaticket.
Poland and space.
Last week, Poland was accepted as the 20th member of this space agency at a Council of Ministers meeting of the member countries of the European Space Agency, making Poland the second member country from the former Eastern Bloc (after the Czech Republic, which became an ESA member on 12 November 2008).
Poland began a close cooperation with ESA in 1994 and participated in several ESA projects in the following years.
Poland's path into space, of course, began much earlier.
Even before the Second World War, Polish cosmos enthusiasts dealt with space travel, although they did not always understand.
I recall, for example, the lecture by A. Šternfeld on December 6, 1933 at the Warsaw Observatory, in which the author presented thoughts from his pioneering work "Entrance to Cosmonautics".
However, the ideas of the young engineer (born 1905) left the listeners cold, and after years Šternfeld still remembered that only Dr. Jan Gadomski had shown interest in his work.
Šternfeld won the Prix REP deer donated by Robert Esnault-Pelterie and André Louis Hirsch in 1934 for "Entrance to Cosmonautics".
Dr. Jan Gadomski (1899 - 1966) later became an important advocate of astronomy and aerospace.
In Polish journals he published hundreds of articles and wrote several books on these scientific disciplines.
Gadomski’s globally recognized contribution to the development of spacemologists was honored, among other things, by the fact that one of the craters was named after him.
Already in 1925, a railway draisine was built in Poland, for which a rocket propulsion was planned.
Unfortunately, the author of the draft and the details of this project remained unknown.
It is not even clear whether the rocket should be used to launch or brake the drain.
The information on this comes exclusively from the contemporary press.
In 1933 the Polish infantry began to deal with rocket-powered projectiles.
The research was carried out by the institute Komórka Techniki Uzbrojenia in cooperation with Prof. Mieczyslaw Wolfke and Prof. Gustaw Mokrzyckiegi.
It can be seen from the documents handed down that the research had penetrated to the stage of practical tests.
Of course, the research was interrupted by the invasion of the German Wehrmacht.
In 1978, a photo-electric hunting rocket appeared in the trade press, written by Rohoziński, and the following year the work "Rakieta - torpeda powietrzna i rakietobomba lotnicza" by the author Leliwa-Krzywobblocky appeared.
Both projects dealt with the military use of rocket propulsion.
Shortly before the war, all projects for the military use of missile technology were coordinated by the Temporary Scientific Advisory Committee (Tymczasowy Komitet Doradczo-Naukowy).
The committee was established in 1937, but after two years of activity its work was terminated by the beginning of the war.
Further articles on space travel appeared after the war thanks to the work of the Polish Space Society (Polskie Towarzystwo Astronautyczne).
The first mention of this society takes place in 1954 in the November issue of Problemy, in which four extensive articles are dedicated to spaciality.
Prof. M. Subotowicz’s article contains the proposal for the establishment of a space company.
At that time, there were already the first satellite technology projects, and it was foreseeable that space exploration would have a future.
Already in its beginnings in 1956, the Polish space company PTA made an effort to join the International Federation of Space, founded in 1951, and in fact the PTA became an ordinary member as early as the autumn of 1956.
The following year, the first PTA chairman, Kazimierz Zarankiewicz (1902-1959), became Deputy Chairman of the International Federation of Space.
He held this office until his death in 1959.
Since 1956, the PTA has been instrumental in the development of the highly successful meteorological rocket (Rakieta Meteorologiczna), the first Polish rocket for scientific research.
The first type, the RM-1, was completed in 19597, the first start took place on October 10, 1958.
The rocket with a maximum altitude of 1800 m was about 80 cm long and weighed not quite 5 kg.
Later, an improved version, the RM-1A, was built, and in 1959 flight tests began in the Błędów desert with the two-stage RM-2 rocket.
The rocket was 1.4 m long and weighed about 11.5 kg.
Another stage of development should already be able to carry out real scientific work - the RM-34 rocket should reach an altitude of up to 14.5 km and be used for the observation of altitude winds.
In 1962, however, further development was discontinued.
Successor to the first RM-type RM missiles was the meteor 1 missile developed between 1962 and 1965.
The rocket was designed as a two-stage rocket, its total length was 510 cm and the launch weight was 32.5 kg.
Of this rocket, three types were developed (Meteor-1A, -1B and -1C), which differed from each other in the size of the transport space for scientific instruments.
The Meteor-1A rocket had a transport space with a capacity of 0.4 litres, Meteor-1B over 0.34 liters and Meteor-1C over 0.62 liters.
The maximum altitude was 37 km for all three types.
In the years 1965 to 1968, the Meteor 2 rocket was developed at the aviation institute, whose first flight tests began in October 1970.
Meteor-2 had a take-off weight of 380 kg and was able to carry a payload of 10 kg at an altitude of about 60 km.
After that, the Meteor-2H and Meteor-3 versions were built.
Also worth noting is Poland's accession to the COSPAR (Committee for Space Research) in 1960, which was followed two years later by the establishment of a national COSPAR committee.
Poland also participated in space exploration with Soviet satellites as part of the Intercosmos program, and in 1978 Polish pilot Miroslaw Hermaszewski became the second intercosmonaut after Czech Vladimír Remek.
Repealling the law on charitable work is not a solution.
Last week, the Constitutional Court repealed the law on charitable work.
This decision provoked a lively discussion among the public.
It’s certainly interesting to look at the problem from a broader perspective.
The liberal-oriented economic systems in the EU and in the globalized world are based on the principle of unregulated economic competition.
In the end, this means that the individual economies are in a permanent conflict with each other.
The reason for this is the principle of free commerce and the free, unrestricted movement of private capital, which goes hand in hand with rampant financial speculation.
Due to the large differences in the value of labor (wages), there are price tensions.
This term is understood to mean a state in which the manufacturer tries to exist in economic competition by importing favourable goods "underbidding" the competition in order to seize a larger market share and to increase its profit.
On a large scale, this means for most companies to move their production abroad or to shop cheaply there, or to give up their business. The result is high unemployment in countries with comparatively high labor costs.
Since private capital bears no social responsibility and thus no responsibility for the unemployment caused by it, government spending necessarily rises.
This situation is further reinforced by the fact that traders are absolutely unwilling to pay their taxes and thereby to compensate the economic and social damage caused by them as a whole.
This situation is widely known, so that concrete statistics are superfluous at this point.
The reckless practice of private capital creates a situation in the individual economies in which governments are forced to compete with each other in order to artificially reduce the social level of their own population and thus lure foreign capital into the country.
In other words, governments are supplying their own population to private capital without regard to the social consequences.
This is expressed in particular in amendments to existing laws.
The aim of these measures is to force one's own population to accept the dictates of private capital, especially in the wage sector.
This is done, on the one hand, through economic compulsion with long-term unemployment and, on the other hand, through the curtailment of workers' rights.
The result is growing poverty, as well as ever-increasing differences between rich and
In Germany, there have been public food dispensaries for poors who are no longer able to feed through their own work.
The number of those affected is already in the millions.
In the course of increasing the competitiveness of the German economy, there are regular cases in which workers receive such a low wage that the state has to increase their income up to the subsistence level.
Such a scandal has also been uncovered in the case of aid workers employed in the German Bundestag.
The austerity measures of virtually all EU southern states will undoubtedly amount to the same state in which people are forced to emigrate (as was the case in the 19th century) under the pressure of a catastrophic crash of their social state, and in doing so hope that private investment capital will eventually flow into the country.
This naturally raises the question of where this capital comes from?
If it comes from another EU country, only poverty migrates from one country to another; or the capital is completely absent, because in China, India, Brazil, Turkey, Morocco or Egypt or in Africa at all, employees work for a fraction of European wages.
This affects the whole of Latin America.
Liberal theories and media assert to the point that the state must not participate with capital in its own economy, and that planned economy leads to economic catastrophe.
Private capital insists narrowly on its position that the state must not intervene in the economy in a steering manner.
Then, however, one has to ask oneself whether private capital does not influence or even direct politics and thus the entire state in its own selfish sense.
Here's the clear answer: Yes.
The proof of this is the existence of a virtually omnipotent lobby that is ubiquitous in all states.
The result is a desperate situation in the area of corruption, of mutual favouritism, a legislation in which virtually everything is punitive but nothing punishable.
In Germany, the situation is such that for lack of finances, a state ministry commissions private legal companies closely intertwined with industry with draft laws.
These bills are then passed in the Bundestag.
All violence then no longer proceeds from the people, as proclaimed by the Western constitutions, but from strong financial groups that prefer their own interests.
Clearly, liberal democracies are quickly getting into a signity, as Appian describes in his work on the crisis of the Roman Republic in the time of Caesar and Pompey: "The state has long been in disintegration, and the offices have been filled with violence.
Against bribes, by unlawful sleeping of advantages or with stone and sword.
Corruption proliferated unrestrainedly, and the people went to the election with votes that have already been bought ... "People with character did not apply for offices, so that as a result of this decline the state had no consul for eight months" ... "One really began to talk about the fact that the only medicine in this difficult situation was a sole rule, and that a strong man had to be elected permanently."
As it turns out, today's society, as in antiquity, is based on the ruthless preference of one's own advantage without paying attention to social interests.
By its very nature, private capital is not able to recognize and enforce the advantage for the whole of society.
The result, today as then, is an unprecedented decadence of the elites, who do not strive in the slightest for deeper reforms.
We must therefore seek the cause for the advent of fascism and communism in the ruthless liberalization of economic systems in the 19th and 20th centuries.
From today's state of affairs, the demise of these regimes in favour of liberal democracies can be seen as a certain pause in which the next round is already looming.
Particularly catastrophic is the fact that today's elites are in complete ignorance with the possible losses of hundreds of thousands of lives in the future, the humanitarian and social disasters of which we are already witnesses, as well as the crimes against humanity as we know from older and modern history.
Repealing the law on charitable work is not a solution here, at least not a permanent one.
Under the pressure of global and European economic competition, the Czech Republic will be forced to continue to look for ways to push down the social level of the population.
This trend is therefore system-related.
The only solution is targeted political-social reforms that increase the state's capital participation in the economy, increase citizens' influence on the government and weaken the monopoly position of private capital in society in favor of the state.
Israel: laboratory of chaos.
"... that nothing arises out of violence, or could ever arise," sings Sting in the song Fragile, whose most important verse in the chorus reads: "...so that we may not forget how fragile we are".
"If my sons didn't want war, there wouldn't be one," says the dying Gutele Schnaper, wife of Mayer Amschel Rothschild, in 1849
After the last wave of violence between Israel and the Gaza Strip, as always, there has been an innumerability of reactions.
Some stand behind Israel and argue their right to self-defense while calling the Palestinians terrorists; others, in turn, support the Palestinians, argue with the racism of the Israeli state with the genocide perpetrated on the Palestinian Arabs and describe the state of Israel as terrorist.
I do not want to write at this point about who is culprit and who is the victim in these recurring waves of killing; ultimately, today's inhabitants of Israel, including self-government areas, were born into the current political situation and have not witnessed the beginning of the violence itself.
It’s about giving readers a look behind-the-scenes, looking at whom the 95-years of tension (calculated from the Balfour declaration in November 1917) in the small patch of land in the Middle East above all.
My thoughts are partly based on accessible historical facts, others are derived from these and emerge from my own understanding of who or which group of people significantly influences the events of modern history.
The history of humanity is first and foremost a history of the struggle for power.
In each epoch we can make out an Alexander the Great or a Napoleon.
What is less apparent is the question of whether it was really always these personalities themselves who determined their steps autonomously, or whether there was someone behind their throne who steered their actions to a predetermined goal.
We have to admit to ourselves that we are living in a time in which the wealth of the world is concentrated in the hands of fewer individuals, and that this concentration of money and the resulting power did not arise within the span of a generation.
Among these astronomically rich families, one stands out that could be described as the head of the Kraken (I don't know if there's anyone standing above them, but I don't rule this out) - the Rothschilds.
There's not much written about them.
Understandably.
The first press agency (Reuters) was already bought by the Rothschilds in the 1990s in order to prevent the connection of their name with highly criminal acts, in the background of which the consolidation of their power, the multiplication of their assets or both.
The Rothschilds are involved in almost all central banks of the world, and against the countries in which they are not participating in the central banks, they wage wars or prepare for them (before the raid on Afghanistan there were 7 countries, after the raid on Iraq there were 5 countries, after the overthrow of Ghaddafi, only four remained, with Russia in the meantime subordinating its central bank to the Russian government).
Whoever trieded to oppose this family had to pay for it with their lives.
Abraham Lincoln declined to renew the status of Rothschild's Bank of America as a central bank, began issuing his own banknotes during the Civil War, and was assassinateed in 1865 in a theater.
J.F. Kennedy began issuing his own banknotes and wanted to abolish the Fed - he was assassinated in 1963; lawmaker Louis McFadden was poisoned in 1936 after seeking to sue the FeD for triggering the Great Depression in 1929.
