The movie Argo: When it's at its worst, call Hollywood
In November 19979, a mob occupieding Islamist students occupied the U.S. embassy in Tehran and took 52 diplomats hostage.
For their release, they demanded the extradition of the ousted Shah Mohammad Reza Pahlavi, who had fleded to the U.S. after the revolution, who for decades had upheld the patronage of his regime.
For the U.S. government, there was no possible way out of this situation - it couldn't afford to throw the Shah overboard, as this would have massively damaged the trust of its allies in other countries.
At the same time, she was unable to enforce the release of the hostages in Iran, where the revolution resulted in the establishment of a theocratic regime.
It was a major blow to the reputation of the United States, and the subsequent fiasco in the violent attempt at hostage liberation only made things worse.
The imprisoned diplomats were finally released after 444 days after negotiations were brokered by the Algerian government.
The fate of the hostages evoked a wave of solidarity as well as anti-Iranian sentiments at home.
The debacle in Iran contributed significantly to Jimmy Carter's defeat against Ronald Reagan in the 1980 presidential election.
The film Argo by director and actor Ben Affleck describes an episode of this story, which gave the USA a small partial victory.
Immediately before filling the embassy, six embassy employees managed to escape.
This ended after numerous complications at the Canadian ambassador's residence.
The CIA, in cooperation with the Canadian authorities, managed to bring the refugees out of Iran; in doing so, an extravagant story helped them to obscure their true identities - they traveled disguised with Canadian passports disguised as members of a film that allegedly visited shooting locations in the country for a science-fiction film.
Combination of Genres
The plan developed by expert for "exfiltration," Tony Mendez, also necessitated Hollywood's collaboration.
In order for the camouflage to become credible, articles about this film project appeared in trade journals, press conferences were helded, and the fictitious production company had a real office.
The details of this operation have long been kept secret. The film relies on Tony Mendez's memories.
Affleck's film is a special combination of several movie genres.
On the one hand, it is a realistic political thriller, but at the same time a so-called "Caper Movie", i.e. the story of a successful hussar piece such as the film Ocean's Eleven.
As a result, the mood levels alternate in the film - on the one hand the documentary scenes from Tehran (iconic contemporary photographs are shown in the title sequence, and there are no major differences in the film version of the same situations either).
On the other hand, the loose passages from Hollywood, which come ironically and slightly exaggerated.
And then there's the scenes from CIA headquarters and other institutions - men in suits at the meeting table, on the official hallways, on the phone...
Ben Affleck has achieved a remarkable reboot of his career with this film.
A ridiculed actor has become a respectable director, and even as an actor, he is no longer the target of ironic remarks.
Argo is his thirth feature film after the crime novel No Children's Game (Gone Baby Gone, 2007) and the Thriller City Without Mercy (2010).
At the same time, it's Affleck's first film, which doesn't set in the director's native Boston.
The sense of the atmosphere of the place was one of the features that set this director's previous films apart from Hollywood's standard.
However, Affleck also proves this sense in Argo, where Tehran is "played" by Canada.
The best scenes of the film take place in the streets and show the reconstruction of events that have really taken place - the entrance sequence of the storming of the message is very impressive in its clarity, and nevertheless conveys the feeling of confusion and unpredictability that occurs when the story moves somewhere.
Affleck and his team also have a similar effect on fictional scenes (the false film staff at a bazaar in Tehran).
Too much action in too many places
The director also had to deal with the fact that the story doesn’t actually offer many exciting scenes in the cinematic sense.
However, he works quite successfully with the one, sometimes helpingling reality - which does not always seem so elegant (the scenes in which the imminent crisis at the airport in Tehran is avertented by a telephone call in America, or even the chase on the runway, seems somewhat artificial).
Argo's weakness is its all-too-wide dispersion, which is due to the need to show too much action in too many places.
Alan Arkin and John Goodman are very charming in their roles as Hollywood employees, but their characters deserve more space, and they are far from alone in the film.
Affleck's film thus loses some traction. It is a film that can be pursued with a certain interest and in doing so the design and the retro-style reminiscent of a thriller from the 70th.
Enthusiasm, however, it is difficult.
However, as a reminiscent of a piece of history in a certain way to this day and as proof of how exaggerated the conceptions of the omnipotential and omniscentive secret services are, it is sufficient.
Policies for inflating balloons, bananas and circus
The server www.bankovnipoplatky.com, which annually launches a survey on the most absurd bank fees, has decided this year to organize the competition "Most Absurd regulation or idea from the EU workshop".
"On this image, the plan recently put forward by the EU to introduce a women's quota of 40% in the management of large European companies brought us," Patrik Nacher, who takes care of the organization of this survey, told the Právo newspaper.
Among the nominated absurdities, for example, is the judgment of the European Court of Justice on the unification of insurance tariffs for men and women.
Until then, women enjoyed an advantage in life insurance because they objectively pose a lower risk for the insurance companies.
“Further incredible incursions from the EU workshop can be nominated by the end of this year.
The actual vote will then take place until the end of February 2013," Nacher said.
Controversial EU regulations include, among other things, the binding admixture of bioethanol with fuels, which in its consequence is nature-damaging, the prohibition of reliable mercury thermometers only because they contain a relatively small amount of this hazardous material, or also the directive on the size of chicken cages, due to which eggs became much more expensive this year.
In the past, there was also criticism of the factual prohibition of the use of the term "butter spread" or the ban on sales of conventional light bulbs.
Bananas must be 14 cm long
Under the pressure of this or that lobby from trade and industry, whose demands are usually represented by one or more member states in Brussels (for example, the Czech Republic is currently trying to enforce the demands of Czech banks with veto threat).
Lobbying interests were also behind that bananas of the highest trade class in the EU must be at least 14 cm long and must not have a "curvature anomaly".
The European Commission defended itself by arguing that it merely harmonises the inconsistent national standards that would hinder trade.
The standards regarding fruit and vegetables have already been softened by the EU against the resistance of some countries by pointing out that the previous directives would lead to a waste of food.
One candidate for victory in this poll may be the EU regulation from last year, which, according to balloons, must be labeleded that children under the age of eight are not allowed to inflate the balloon without the supervision of an adult.
The EU referred to an American study, which according to balloons is the leading cause of children's suffocation.
A similar restriction already applies to children up to 14 years of age when using fairgrounds.
Curious incursions are also available at home
Relatively absurd is also a directive that affects the European officials themselves - anyone who occupies an official function in the EU is not allowed to use the designation Macedonia because of the sensitivity of the issue to Greece and must say FYROM (Former Yugoslav Republic of Macedonia) instead.
In cooperation with the Association of Liberal Economists Laissez Faire, the server Bankovnípoplatky.com also nominated the EU regulation on the extent of food supplies in the territory of a member country in addition to the above-mentioned absurdities.
The EU thus determined the maximum volume of food supplies that was allowed to be located in the territory of the Czech Republic on the day of our accession to the EU.
For example, the Czech Republic then exceeded the fixed volume for canned mushrooms, for which a hefty fine threatened us.
The organizers of the survey were also impressed by the idea that certain countries should receive money for that they don't have a sea, or by the idea of giving grants for grants.
Of course, these ideas did not come from Brussels, but from Prague.
“We’re disadvantaged because we don’t have a sea.
We will apply for reimbursement from the EU in the autumn of 2004, the then Minister of Agriculture Jaroslav Palas (ČSSD).
As an argument, he argued that the grain harvest had been very high and that the state camps were overcrowded as a result of the so-called intervention purchases.
Since the Czech Republic has another route to seaports, the EU should pay hundreds of millions of euros to us, according to Palas.
The European Commission finally met the Czech Republic to such an extent that it organised an invitation to tender for the purchase of cereals from countries that do not have access to the sea.
Funding for applications for funding was offered to the municipalities by the Ministry of Regional Development under the direction of Minister Pavel Němec (US-DEU), in concrete terms it should be funds for the setting up of grant applications to Brussels.
EU: Bizarre regulations are the exception
Regulations in the member countries are easily the target of criticism, the EU's efforts to regulate and thus also for the effectiveness and development of the entire European Union, according to some experts.
According to these experts, a more serious problem is the EU funding for projects that have nothing in common with the deepening of European integration, but which have been enforced by individual member countries in the budget negotiations.
Emotions in this country are also evoked by the fact that, like other member countries in Brussels, the Czech Republic has to fight for the right to use specific designations of traditional products and does not always win this battle.
After a six-year battle with Germany and Austria, the Czech Republic was able to defend the designation "Olomoucké tvarůžky", the Inländerrum (Tuzemský rum), whose tradition goes back to the 19th century, our manufacturers had to rename "Tuzemák".
The designation rum may only be used for products from sugar cane distillate, not for sugar beet products.
In the official list of products registered in the EU, the Karlovy Vary Zwieback, carp from Pohořelice and Třeboň, or the Saazer hops now also appear in the company of the world-famous cheese varieties Feta and Gorgonzola, the Lübeck marzipan or the Parma hops.
With an EU brand, the Pardubic gingerbread or the Hořicer rollers can boast.
"People want me to save the republic, but I'm just an amateur," Okamura claims.
Senator, how do you come up with the idea of running for president?
It's not about being a senator or president.
If in our country everything were to work without problems, I wouldn't run anywhere.
But I can no longer see how the republic has been plundered for 20 years, how thieves roam freely, while people are raising taxes and retirement age.
I didn't have any ambition to become a politician.
However, when I see something I don’t like, I’m trying to suggest a solution to change it.
And since I'm already over 40, and independent and non-partisan, I have no other way of influencing things other than running for the office of a senator or the president.
You made it to the Senate and made your way into the castle.
Isn't that why you're turning your back on your constituents?
I've said to myself all along that I'm going to take up the fight for the castle after the outcome of the Senate elections.
Later, I clarified this and said that I will run for the office of the president, provided I am elected senator.
However, my goal is not the function. The function is an instrument with which I can realize my visions.
For this, I need as much influence as possible and the strongest possible mandate.
However, the difficulty is not only that we, as a people, scold in the pub and in front of the TV, butting down anyone who wants to make a difference.
In addition, there are the media, which wants to rob you of honor, and confuse the freedom of the word to lie with freedom.
About me, for example, it was claimed that I would bribe journalists or that I was an adviser to Jiří Paroubek.
Let’s talk about your visions.
The struggle for the castle has taken up with the thesis of the material and criminal liability of politicians as well as with retrospective asset declarations over 20 million crowns.
To do this, you need to change laws.
As president, you don't have competences in this regard, and only the Senate as a whole can bring in legislative proposals.
How are you going to solve this problem?
As a simple citizen, I campaigned to ensure that the travel guide remains a free trade, and this could be enforced.
The problem is small-spirited politics - if someone from the right or from the left comes with a good proposal, it is deliberately rejected, and that only stops the population.
As an independent non-partisan, I have a far greater potential to gain support from all parties represented in parliament.
My advantage is that without drawer thinking and party dogmas from any party, I can take what is best for our country.
Do you see yourself as a person in politics rather on the right or on the left?
From a Czech point of view, it seems to me that people see me rather on the left.
For me, though, this right-left thinking is completely unimportant.
It's important that we move forward.
It's not about whether someone belongs to the left or to the right camp; I want to connect people.
I always publicly support every good solution approach, even if it comes from the KSČM or the ODS, and I will also turn against bad ideas.
You don't like it if someone calls you a populist.
But don't confirm what you've just said?
If you create a business plan in a company, then you also have an ideal goal and a vision.
And that's what you're approaching.
This can be called populism because of me, but all the proposals I have spoken about are already working in the world, or they have been discussed by important experts.
But without support in parliament, you only have keywords.
And you won't get far in politics in the long term.
Or do you think that, if you only talk to people long enough, you will be able to enforce material and criminal liability, for example?
I don't have any other option.
Convincing politicians, journalists and the public and trying to bring them to our side to enforce these goals.
As president, it wouldn't be a problem for me to urge the leaders of the parliamentary parties live on television to enshrine material and criminal liability for politicians, civil servants, judges and prosecutors by law.
And those targeted might have to say why they reject this.
A strong personality pointing to grievances can put pressure on the political scene.
Take only once the president's direct election, which was a merit of public pressure.
I say very clearly that I'm an amateur; I'm neither a genius nor an intellectual.
I'm trying to find allies for my visions.
I've only just started with politics and I'm trying to find the support of the majority for my programme.
I'm always trying to judge things myself; and if it doesn't work out, I'll go back into the private sector in six years.
This sounds a little like Okamura should save the Czech Republic.
I'm not a savior.
I know that I can't do anything alone; that's why I've talked to several well-known personalities about whether they don't want to run for the Senate.
For example, I spoke to Radim Jančura, who declined due to overwork.
In addition, I supported the investigative journalist Jana Lorencová, who uncovered the scam in the trade of light fuel oil.
My candidacy began with the requirement that people are deeply unhappy, but now I'm not sure anymore.
Sixty percent of eligible voters didn't go to the elections at all, and those who have left have mostly elected representatives of the establishment.
In the Senate, we are only two independents.
People have chosen such a Senate composition that changes will be very difficult to enforce.
Nevertheless, I will fight for my visions, e.g. for the direct election of the mayors and district captains.
Are you thinking about founding your own party?
I haven't thought about that yet, because I don't have time to check the past of every potential party member, and I don't even have the money for it.
I don't even have any money for the president's campaign yet; there are only CZK 20,000 in my transparent account.
Don't have any money?
You're talking about financial statements, what would yours look like?
I estimate my private assets at about CZK 60 million.
In Prague, I have a plot with a value of 25 million, an apartment for about 10 million, another for 8 million, an art collection worth about 10 million, as well as an Aston Martin for 3.5 million and a Škodu Superb, which is worth about a million. And then I still have a few million in the account.
The Aston Martin, by the way, was a children's dream - I've always liked James Bond driving in the car, being gallant to women and at the same time fighting evil.
You're driving an Aston Martin, you've got a fortune of $60 million and don't have money for your campaign?
They say that you want to change the republic, but you don’t want to invest your own money.
This isn't exactly persuasive.
I don’t have $15 million for the campaign.
You want me going on debt?
I've already put 2.5 million into the campaign.
The fact that I don't have sponsors shows that my program probably doesn't arouse too much interest.
And I'm not obligated to contest the campaign out of my own resources.
The cost of the campaign is basically covered by my salary as a senator.
But I couldn't live on the senator's salary alone; for example, I couldn't fund my son the English school, which costs 30,000 a month.
If I just wanted to make money, I wouldn't run for anything.
Do you continue to work as an entrepreneur to have your livelihoods?
Didn't ye say that you're going to shut down these activities?
But that's not related to the amount of salary.
As promised, I have partially scaled back my activities.
For example, my deputy will become managing director of the travel agency in spring.
People want to see me as a Samaritan who saves the republic.
But I also have to make a living from something.
Which monthly income were you used to as an entrepreneur?
About 200,000 to 400,000, and that continues to happen.
However, as president, I'd discontinue my ventures entirely.
The full conversation can be found in the Saturday edition of Právo.
The MVRDV architects prove that the real adventures are not only in the mind - with the example of Spijkenisse and the recently erected Bücherberg - 2 photos
"I find the building funny, it looks futuristic, and finally there's something interesting to see again," says Lisette Verhaig, a passer-by on the side of the road.
And Stefan Spermon, IT technician in a nearby large-scale company, says: "Yes, it's beautiful, the house."
But I wonder what we still need a library for today.
They all have internet, iPad and e-books.
No one of these old-style libraries is still voluntarily going to one of these old-style libraries today, right?
Spijkenisse, a sleeping city in need of barely in need of sight at the gates of Rotterdam, is a strange record holder.
The 80,000-inhabitant community has the lowest education rate in the entire Netherlands.
In order to counteract this stupid circumstance, it was decided a few years ago to contribute to general education and to recreate the seven fictitious bridges, which are depicted on the euro notes, as pretty brushed reinforced concrete miniatures.
The success of the educational offensive was limited.
And so the city fathers realized that there was only one way to master the statistics: A library had to come here!
Winy Maas from the Rotterdam architectural firm MVRDV, master of foolhardy bar charts and producer of witty, yes often cynical buildings, took the matter with the usual serenity and appeared for competition hearing in 2003 with five books under his arm and a grin on his face.
And while the jury was still looking puzzled and shrugged with the armpits, the naughty Maas stacked his exquisite souvenirs in size to a pyramid and ended his actionistically substantiated speech with the words: "Dear urban community!"
So that's him, my suggestion for the book mountain of Spijkenisse, for the so-called Boekenberg!
Nine years later, the €30-million mountain has poured up.
It is part of a revitalization project, which also includes underground parking, supermarket, post office and a few adjoining residential and terraced houses with a total of 50 apartments.
At the beginning of November, the Bücherberg was awardeed the second "Best Library of NL 2012".
In addition, the project is nominated for the Dutch National Wood Award 2012.
This is the faceless small-town restore, which until now had nothing more to offer than a post-modern pedestrian zone and a stunned ugly town hall, behind whose white façades one would be inclined to suspect a dairy factory to enrich a piece of contemporary architecture.
Above all, Spijkenisse now has the first public cultural building in the history of its existence.
The long road to the book
The first impression: Büchereldorado under cheese bell.
Although there is a lift that leads in the middle of the mountain massif, the true joys of space and reading open up when climbing the topography within walking distance.
The glazed interior is bright and clear, the burned clinker floor and the elegant street lights speak unequivocably the language of a public town square.
The urban environment is perfect.
You are already looking for a park bench, dog and football-playing boys and girls.
Books, books, books everywhere.
"Normally, the bookshelves stand along the façade, and in the middle there is a large, dark room that is mostly uncomfortable and impersonal," says Winy Maas.
We turned the classic room configuration upside down and put the reading area from the inside to the outside.
The interior of the Bücherberg is cleverly used: in the middle are offices, internet library, chess club, environmental center and building technology headquarters.
A special pleasure are the black bookshelves, which are sometimes wall coverings, sometimes parapets, sometimes a stair railings.
Optics, haptics and smell are foreign.
Even diehard architects and civil engineers shake their heads with unknown building materials.
"We wanted to work here with recycled materials," explains Joop Trouborst, project manager at Spijkenisse's municipality, at the Standard's request.
And so one day we came across a suitable waste product from agriculture in a Friesland farm.
For many years, a millimeter-thin plastic fleece has been used as a root substrate in the Netherlands in greenhouses and in fields.
It's cheap and time-efficient.
The thin textile lasts one or two seasons, after which it is ripe for the bulky waste.
For the library, the fleece - for the first time in these amounts - was pressed into four-centimeter-thick plates.
Under heat and pressure, the so-called Landbouwplastic (KLP) turns into a dark, homogenous, resilient material that smells a bit of new cars and a bit of sports shoe.
After 105 steps you have arrived at the summit.
At the end of the almost 500-meter-long hike you will not only be rewarded with a fantastic view of the city, but also with Dutch croquettes and potted ficus trees.
They provide atmosphere, but above all for the right humidity in the Literary Mountains.
donations for the new soul
"You wouldn't believe it, but despite the many glass surfaces, this house is a showcase project in terms of ecology," says Trouborst.
Heated and refrigerated with geothermatic heat.
Although the Bücherberg is under a glass fall, the sun shines only briefly inside even on sunny days.
The wide wood glue binders, which stand transversely to the glass façade, serve as shading and intercept much of the sunlight.
The indoor climate is very pleasant.
The rest is done by fully automatic roller blinds.
Stefan Spermon, the initial skeptic from the IT industry, has already ventured into the new library.
Lisette Verhaig was already there.
And also the TCM teacher Cynthia Bogarde, who even describes Boekenberg as Spijkenisse's "long overdue soul".
The reason: At the opening a few weeks ago, every resident was invited to donate a book from his personal collection.
For the time being, the optical gaps in the not yet fully stocked library are to be filled - currently 70,000 pieces.
The concept has risen.
The shelves are filled to the top.
"Nothing is worse than a half-empty library," says architect Winy Maas.
“Thanks to our invitation, every resident now has a certain relationship with this new house.
Everyone knows that his book is part of this building.
And if it's only to the ornament.
MVRDV has thus succeeded in the supreme discipline, which is called identity foundation in technical jargon.
Spijkenisse has made literary history.
As young and uneducated as it may be.
This is finally a starting point for identity.
Szabo: "Germans have to play a bigger role"
Germany abstained in the vote on the inclusion of Palestine.
According to Stephen Szabo, an expert on U.S.-European relations, Berlin is thus balancing on a narrow diplomatic degree.
Deutsche Welle: Earlier this week, Germany had initially signaled that it would vote against the request for an observer status of Palestinians at the United Nations.
Then Berlin abstained.
Why?
Stephen Szabo: Germany doesn't support what the Israelis did in Gaza.
However, Germany must now be careful due to its special relationship with Israel.
But at the same time, I don't believe it doesn't support the American position either.
Germany wanted to demonstrate its independence - without, however, being too critical of Israel.
During the uprising in Libya in March 2011, Germany also abstained when it came to setting up a no-fly zone.
This was finally enforced by NATO.
Is it difficult for Germany to take a clear position on important international issues?
Yes, that's true.
This is because it is changing its foreign policy, away from a policy that has been managed, so to speak by the USA, towards a German foreign policy.
Complicatingly, Europeans do not have a coherent and unified policy.
Thus, the Germans find themselves between the fronts.
They are expected to play a more independent role, but they are not used to it.
I think they're still approaching their role, but they're on their way to a "more normal" foreign policy.
A foreign policy, such as France or Great Britain.
What does a "normal" foreign policy mean specifically from a German point of view?
It demonstrates the willingness to take positions on national interests that are independent of those of the United States or European partners.
I believe that German foreign policy is motivated by economic policy, i.e. exports and its relations with certain regions, such as Russia, China or the Middle East.
Germany's economic interests are, to a certain extent, different from those of the other great powers and that is why Germany must protect its interests.
Have these economic interests had an influence on attitudes in the Middle East conflict and voting in the UN?
On the one hand, Germany has large sales markets in the Middle East, especially in the Gulf states.
Therefore, it must be careful not to confront the public, but also the elites, in the Arab countries.
That definitely matters.
But I wouldn't want to attach too much weight either, that's not a completely one-sided relationship.
But it definitely plays an important role in German considerations.
Has Germany damaged its relations with the U.S. by abstainning from making important decisions, for example in the vote on Palestine?
I believe that in Europe and even in the USA there is a great understanding of the German position.
That's why I don't think this was such a dramatic rupture as on the Libyan issue.
Maybe Germany will even gain some respect with it.
After all, it signals that it is taken seriously as an international player and that his interests must be considered.
There are different opinions in Europe about the Palestinian initiative.
The U.S. has clearly advocated veto.
Is there any disagreement between the U.S. and many European nations?
Due to U.S. Domestic politics, these differences have always existed.
I believe that the Obama administration secretly has a lot of understanding of the European position.
But because of the political situation here, the government cannot, of course, represent this position publicly.
I believe that the actual disagreement isn’t as great as they always appear.
If you look at relations between Obama and Prime Minister Netanyahu, Obama isn’t that excited about Netanyahu’s policies.
Is it difficult for Germany to reconcile its close relations with Israel and the USA on the one hand, and the position of the most important partners in the EU on the other?
I think that's what makes it so difficult for the Germans.
It would, of course, be a bit easier for the Germans if there were a coherent and unified European policy, which is not currently the case.
So they can't be part of a broader line and instead they need to push things forward from their own position.
That’s what they’re doing with the euro.
I believe that in the future Germany will play a leading role in pushing Europe towards a European position.
Of course, this is not easy for Germany, already because of the relationship with Israel.
This has always been a sensitive issue.
But I believe it's clear to the Germans that they need to play a more independent role.
Does Germany see itself in the role as an important international player - does Germany even want to take on a leading role?
Or is Germany still having a hard time taking a leading role?
Germany is not yet used to it, this is still unpleasant and, for obvious reasons, the country still finds it difficult to play a bigger role.
For example, if we look at the euro crisis, every time Germany assumes a bigger role, various anti-German sentiments become free.
That doesn't make it easy for Germans.
This is actually the old problem: you don't want to be surrounded by hostile countries.
From this point of view, Germany is in a much more difficult position than the United States.
It has to be susceptible to various neighbors and opinions and that's not easy.
Now the influence of the USA in European politics is continuously diminishing, but the EU does not fill this vacuum at the moment, who else will stay there?
The Germans will simply have to play a bigger role.
Even if you don't like it, it's uncomfortable for them and make them even more unpopular - c'est la vie!
Stephen Szabo is deputy director of the Transatlantic Academy in Washington, an institute in which academics and political experts from Europe and North America work together on the challenges of the transatlantic community.
Szabo is also a member of the German Marshall Fund, where he specializes in German politics, U.S. foreign policy and transatlantic relations.
"Brand protection" in China: When Puma and Armani suddenly become Chinese
Armani is a world-renowned brand, Polo Ralph Lauren too.
But what is Armani Polo?
This conceals an officially registered designation in China, which, however, has nothing to do with the original companies.
Nevertheless, it enjoys protection as long as the actual namesakes do not complain.
And even then it's not clear if they're right.
"It's becoming increasingly difficult for foreigners to protect their trademarks in China," says Thomas Pattloch, a lawyer at Taylor Wessing, who specializes in property rights violations in the Far East.
Every week a new case lands on my desk.
The imitators already have a few additional letters so that they can register their trademarks.
So Gucci simply becomes Lu-Gucci, instead of Prada you enter Prada-Kny.
German companies are also legally copied in this way, such as the sporting goods manufacturer Puma.
Pattloch opens a folder with registrations of the Trademark Office in Beijing.
On September 14, 2010, a Chinese company had the Zegna DF Puma trademark protected there, an art name, which was also used by the fashion house Ermenegildo Zegna.
It is known that the Chinese are world champions in copying and injured intellectual property.
There are multi-storey department stores in the big cities that almost exclusively sell counterfeits.
Pattloch's cases, however, are differingly: On behalf of his clients, he argues that Chinese companies are officially given the right to use a designation that is already protected elsewhere.
The Chinese call this "Bang Mingpai," a passenger brand.
The word aligns with "Bang Dakuan."
This refers to women fishing for rich men.
The Chinese authorities aren't aware of any guilt
"That's business-damaging, you have to fight it," Pattloch urges.
"The brand is diluted, the selection disappears, the image damage is enormous."
The financial failures and litigation costs of the affected industries go into the millions, especially for expensive flagship products.
According to market research firm CLSA, China is the third-largest market for luxury items and the fastest-growinning market with a volume of €15 billion per year.
However, deletions of dubious entries in the trademark register are difficult to reach and go into the money.
Up to nine years, the procedure can drag on, with an uncertain outcome.
Pattloch reports cases in which the court dismissed lawsuits because, after the long duration, the contested name had become a "market reality".
If the plaintiff is unlucky, he would even have to pay the plagiarist money because he violated its brand in China, Pattloch says.
Sometimes the law of the Jungle prevails here.
Famous cases also affect graphic elements.
In 2009, Daimler lost a legal dispute with the construction equipment manufacturer Sany, the company that recently took over the German concrete pump builder Putzmeister.
To this day, the Chinese are therefore allowed to use an emblem that is similar to the Mercedes star.
Volvo buyer Geely originally used a blue-and-white logo reminiscent of BMW; the dispute was settled, Geely had to change it.
The fashion maker Lacoste lost in China against imitators from Hong Kong and Singapore, who made the famous crocodile look in the other direction.
The Chinese authorities aren't aware of any guilt.
The CTMO brand office in Beijing admits that that there were bottlenecks there in 2010 due to insufficient staffing and equipment.
Last year, however, they returned from the "emergency situation in the workflow to normality".
Thus, the stock of unprocessed opposition proceedings had fallen by 22 percent.
Almost 57,000 such cases have been completed, 75 percent more than in the previous year.
Nevertheless, 81,500 appeals are still waiting in the office.
Tacting against it costs a lot
As so often in China, the numbers are impressive.
Last year, CTMO received more than 1.4 million applications for trademark protection, almost a third more than in 2010.
This is a record and means that for the tenth time in a row, China is leading the list of new registrations around the world, the agency said.
This also applies to the inventory of all valid trademarks, a total of 5.5 million pieces.
In 2011, 1.8 billion yuan in fees were taken.
Simply, this means that each registration costs on average 1280 yuan or 160 euros.
Tacking against this costs a multiple, as the case of the German family-owned company Freudenberg shows.
For more than seven years, the company has been campaigning a Chinese plagiarist.
Although the Germans were able to ensure that its illegal fabrication of imitated motor vehicle parts was excavated.
However, the imitator continued to secure the Chinese rights to the Freudenberg brand.
You have missed this yourself, because family names could not be protected in Germany, says Hanno Wentzler, the chairman of the management board of Freudenberg Chemical Specialities in Munich.
The CTMO brand office also dismissed Munich’s objection.
In the next two instances Freudenberg got right, but the opponent continues to fight to this day.
You have to pay hellish attention
Now the matter is pending before the Supreme Court.
Wentzler is confident of bringing the matter to a good conclusion and praises the professionalism of the courts.
But he also says: "The procedure is extremely elaborate and costs us a lot of time, money and nerves."
The internal costs could hardly be quantified, the company archive had even had to look through one hundred-year-old files to provide evidence.
Five years agove, Freudenberg offered a "high six-figure euro amount" in vain to the other side for settlement.
"That's where you can see how much that's worth to us," Wentzler says.
The dangers in the Far East even threaten to spill back into Europe.
Namely, when impersonators secure unprotected trademarks there.
For example, a Chinese manufacturer in Germany wanted to register Freudenberg for shoes and leather.
The group has long since abandoned this business area, but it was still able to avert registration.
"You have to pay hell of attention," Wentzler says.
Both he and lawyer Pattloch advise German companies to be very careful when dealing with China.
It is not enough to rely on international trademark rights; rather, the foreigners should "register everything that is somehow worth protecting, even in China," as Wentzler says.
Otherwise, this can become much more expensive than registration.
In fact: If Freudenberg is subject in the last act of his trademark drama, then you would probably have to pay royalties for your own name to the other side, explains Wentzler.
Or we're being forced out of the market in the segment in question.
World Aidstag: stomping, singing, helping
In Heidelberg, the choir Imbongi rehearses - and AIDS orphans in Swaziland are happy about it.
The story of a connection that overcomes far more than 8733 kilometres.
First there's the ramming: cowboy boots, basketball shoes, women's pumps and men's slippers look for the beat on the parquet floor, find it quickly.
One-two-three-four.
Only then do the voices of the singers swell slowly - alto, bass, tenor and soprano - press, beguile and persecute each other.
And Fiete Hopf, the 29-year-old conductor, almost lifts it out of his fabric shoes when he brings them together with his supple wild gestures.
It's Monday evening, in the music room of the Institute of Medical Psychology in Heidelberg the choir Imbongi rehearses a new song.
Fifty singers, aged between 23 and 69, from the human geneticist to Hausmann have arrived.
"Om'Obani" is not a simple piece, each choral voice has a different text and this also in a very foreign language: Zulu, which is spoken by eleven million people in South Africa, Botswana, Lesotho, Malawi, Mozambique and and in parts of Swaziland.
Help for self-help
Worldwide, there are around 34 million HIV-infected people, Unaids estimates, the United Nations program to fight AIDS.
23.5 million people live in southern Africa.
There are 245000 AIDS orphans in Swaziland.
More than 40 percent of the population are HIV-positive.
For 180 AIDS orphans in the village of Esitjeni, Voices for Africa has found godparents in Germany.
70 of them attend secondary school.
For 15 or 20 euros a month you can accept a sponsorship.
It guarantees the child school fees, school uniforms and a hot meal daily in the Gogo Center.
Imbongi means storyteller on Zulu.
The Bantu language can speak fluently in this room, but singing.
For almost ten years, the choir has been practicing songs in this foreign soft language and occasionally brings them back to where they originally came from: to the south of Africa.
Because 8 733 kilometers away from Heidelberg, in the northwest of the Kingdom of Swaziland, there is the village of Esitjeni, which depends on the vocal power of the German choir.
Forty percent are infected
About 2000 people live there, some still in simple clay and straw huts, much of which are children.
More than 300 of them no longer have parents because they succumbed to the HI virus.
Esitjeni shows on a small scale what all Swaziland suffers from: the highest HIV infection rate, lowest life expectancy worldwide, according to Unicef.
Circumcisions of the male foreskin, which have been shown to reduce the risk of contagion by half, are scarcely accepted by the population.
More than forty percent of people in Swaziland carry the immunodeficiency virus, dying at mid-thirties is not uncommon.
On a joint trip to Africa in the spring of 2005, the choir discovered the village, but above all, the Imbongis saw many children in the streets, who not only lacked parental care, but simply everything: food, clothing, education.
Without graduating from high school, you have no chance in a poor country.
Initially, it was the private commitment of individuals to send a child to school for a few euros a year and allow him a hot meal a day.
But already a year later, the choir founded the association "Voices for Africa", which since then almost professionally takes care of the AIDS orphans in Esitjeni.
Facts about sexually transmitted infections
What are the most important sexually transmitted diseases?
Bacterial STIs include syphilis, chlamydia, and tripper (gonorrhea).
Common viral STIs include HIV, human pappilomaviruses, herpes genitalis or hepatitis.
Felt lices and scabies belong to the parasitic STI.
Who are the main affected groups?
Syphilis and trippers occur mainly in men who have sex with men.
The Robert Koch Institute assumes that at least four out of five syphilis cases reported in Germany will be transmitted through sexual contact between men.
In heterosexual adults, chlamydia infections, trichomonades, candidiasis (fungus), tripper, and humane pappilomavirus are common sexually transmitted diseases.
The proliferation of HIV is comparatively low among heterosexual adults in this country - nevertheless, around 20 percent of new HIV infections account for this group.
Chlamydia infections occur much more frequently among adolescents than in other populations.
According to European surveys, three-quarters of all infections affect young people between the ages of 15 and 25.
In addition, human pappilomaviruses are common among adolescents.
How did the number of infections develop?
Not all sexually transmitted diseases are reportable.
The number of syphilis infections has more than doubled, according to the Robert Koch Institute, from 1697 illnesses in 2001 to 3698 diseases in 2011.
The number of new HIV infections has declined since 2007.
In 2011, there were around 2700 cases.
This is about one-tenth less than the year before.
What are the symptoms pointing to a venereal disease?
The infectious diseases can cause ulcers in the genital area, complaints during urination, outflow, lower abdominal pain as well as blisters or warts.
However, they often do not cause pain or other symptoms and therefore go unnoticed.
How can you protect yourself?
Condoms can mitigate the risk of contagion, but do not provide one hundred percent protection.
In some cases, pathogens of sexually transmitted diseases can also be passed on through lubrication infections and close physical contact.
Experts therefore recommend regular check-ups, especially for frequently changing sex partners.
Diagnosed early, most STIs can be cured and late consequences avoided.
Through sponsorships, donations and last but not least the fees that the choir sings throughout Germany, a lot comes together.
"In total, we have already sent around €200 000 to Esitjeni," says Annette Lennartz, the club's chairwoman.
In the village itself, Zodwa Dlamini, a self-confident and assertive woman, manages the funds from Germany.
She also takes care that the orphans are well accommodated, for example with one of the grandmothers.
The Gogos, as the old women on Zulu are the mainstays of the village.
Some of them have taken up to 14 orphans with them, giving them a roof over their head and ensuring that the children in their uniforms show up on time for school lessons every day.
If you have no one left, you will come to the shelter for Khanyisile, a single woman who is paid by the association as well as the two chefs, who cook for more than 200 hungry children every day.
"Voices for Africa" has also launched a sewing school, built two chicken houses and organized together with the American health organization PSI that many in the village have had an HIV test.
That's not self-evident, because as obvious as the disease is throughout the country, it's most likely to be silent.
A King With 14 Wives
"AIDS is an absolute taboo," says Annette Lennartz, "because it's related to sexuality."
Actually strange in a country where the king officially holds 14 wives.
The last absolute monarch of Black Africa, King Mswati III, is known for his excessive lifestyle.
Polygamy rather than democracy.
Among other things, it is also due to this state-sanctioned form of life that the HI virus has spread so quickly in recent decades.
But also the many migrant workers who carry the virus across the country.
Free condoms exist on every corner, says Annette Lennartz, "but they are hardly used.
The culture is a different - flesh to flesh."
To promote the cultural exchange, the Imbongi Choir travels together every two or three years through the south of Africa and sings songs full of wistfulness, fighting spirit, confidence and black self-confidence that many still know at the southern tip of the black continent from times of apartheid.
A bus full of whites singing songs in a black language - so much recognition not only brings mood and joy, but has also braved some grimmy border soldiers a few tears.
The trip always also leads to Esitjeni, where the singers visit their godchildren.
Even if you can hardly find the small village on maps, in the valley of the Ezulweni River it is more than known.
"Go to Esitjeni, there's the light," the people there say.
And if you return the 8733-kilometre airline to Heidelberg, to the stomping singers in the rehearsal room on Bergheimer Straße, you can also see it shining there.
Messenger: NASA discovers ice on Mercury
The Messenger probe has found evidence that there is ice on the planet Mercury.
The ice cover should possibly be up to 20 meters thick.
The U.S. space agency NASA has demonstrated the existence of ice on planet Mercury.
Although it is closest to the sun, there is also frozen water there, according to three studies published on Thursday in the journal "Science".
The probe Messenger has found evidence that there is an ice cover in the region of the planet, which is permanently in the shaded region of the planet.
This should be at least 30 centimeters and possibly even up to 20 meters thick.
The water presumably came from comets or possibly asteroids smashed on Mercury.
However, no one associates the discovery of ice with the existence of life on the planet, said Sean Solomon, the chief scientist of the Messenger probe.
The temperature on Mercury can reach up to 426 degrees Celsius.
However, the finds could help to clarify how water and other building blocks of life have reached other areas of the solar system.
Unnoticed by Earth citizens, probes, telescopes and small robots like Phoenix are in the process of exploring the depths of space.
From time to time, they send images to Earth: small peepholes into infinite expanses.
The image comes from a camera developed by German researchers from the Max Planck Institute.
The eight planets of our solar system, as well as the dwarf planet Ceres.
Like Pluto, which orbits around the sun behind Neptune, Ceres is not a "planet" according to the International Astronomical Union's new term definition of 2006.
This image of an infrared imagement of the Spitzer telescope shows a "family portrait" of uncounteded star generations: the oldest stars can be recognized as blue dots, it is harder to identify the pink "newborns" in the star circle hall.
This star-forming region - unromantically called W5 by scientists - has been discovered by the Spitzer telescope in the constellation Cassiopeia, at a distance of 6500 light-years.
This dazzling embers of a dying star has taken up NASA's Spitzer telescope.
The doughnut-shaped ring is made of material that the star hurled out in the dying process.
In the huge Trifid Nebula, 5400 light-years from Earth, new stars emerge from gas and dust.
NASA's Spitzer telescope shot this photo of the galactic birthplace.
The Pleiades star cluster, also called "The Seven Sisters," can be seen with the naked eye at night.
With the telescope, however, the colors come out better.
Like a red age, the Helix Nebula looks towards the viewer in this infrared photo.
It is located 700 light-years away in Aquarius constellation.
Its resemblance to an earthly continent earned this fog the title
A combination of normal and infrared photo ensures the spectacular colour scheme.
In its full beauty, this baby star could only be absorbed through the infrared detectors of the Spitzer telescope.
Saturn and its rings: How these were created is one of the great mysteries of astronomy.
Possibly they are the remnants of a Saturn moon that disappeared without a trace 4.5 billion years ago.
One of the Hubble telescope's largest and sharpest footage: The Whirlpool Galaxy
Depending on the colour scheme, shots of spiral galaxies become true works of art.
The image published by the European Southern Observatory shows the Trifid Nebule, several thousand light-years away, in the constellation Sagittarius.
The name Trifid comes from the Latin word trifudus (three-parted, three-cleaved), since dark dust strips divide the glowing heart of the star birthplace.
In the constellation of snake bearers, astronomers have photographed the harbingers of a cosmic carambolage: 400 million light-years away from Earth, the cores of two merging galaxies race towards each other and will inevitably collide with each other.
This star birth recorded the Hubble telescope in the spiral galaxy M83.
If technical shortcuts do not like, it may call it by its nickname Südliches Feuerrad.
The photo of the Hubble space telescope shows an excerpt from the Iris Nebula in the constellation Kepheus.
The nebula, 1400 light-years away, consists of dust granules that are ten to a hundred times smaller than ordinary house dust.
This image has been merged from optical and X-ray images of various telescopes.
It shows a ring of black holes, 430 million light-years from Earth.
This group of galaxies, called Arp 273, took up the Hubble space telescope for NASA.
Scientists call UGC 1810 the larger spiral galaxy.
In this star nebula is the brightest groupe of young stars of our Milky Way.
The star cradle always produces new youngsters.
Also, this star-cloud, connected with the Rosette Nebula, constantly produces new baby stars - 5000 light-years away from Earth.
In this brightly radiant galaxy with a small black hole, there is still no dust but only gas.
Researchers suspect that it originated only shortly after the Big Bang, when hydrogen was predominantly found in the universe.
Our insights into space: the most important telescopes
The telescope is said to have been invented by Hans Lipperhey in 1608 - even before Galileo Galilei used it for stargazing a year.
Since then, the mirrors of the optical telescopes have become ever larger - and the insights they provide deeper and deeper.
For 30 years, from 1947 to 1975, the Hale telescope in the Palomar Observatory near San Diego was the world’s largest telescope.
The mirror, here in the picture, had an average of five meters.
The Large Binocular Telescope is located in Arizona, USA.
It allows glimpses into space just over two mirrors, each with a diameter of 8.4 meters.
The inner workings of Gran Telescopio Canarias on the Canary Island of La Palma is huge - the mirror alone comes at 10.4 meters in diameter.
The mirror of the Southern African Large Telescope in South Africa is segmented - to save costs.
Nevertheless, it reaches a diameter of about eleven meters.
Disadvantage of the favorable design: The telescope is lashed at its height angle - and is therefore limited in its mobility.
The Hobby Eberly telescope in Texas is also fixed at the angle of altitude.
Its peculiarity: the high lighting power.
Despite comparatively small mirror diameters, this extends to those of the world’s largest reflecting telescopes.
With the help of a radio telescope in Arecibo (Puerto Rico), researchers listen to space after extraterrestrial signals.
The radio telescope has a diameter of 305 meters.
Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence (SETI) allows any computer owner to help by providing computing power.
View of the Observatory of the European Observatory (ESO) in the Chilean Andes.
This is where the Very Large Telescope - which lives up to its name.
With a total of four mirrors, the telescope can also illuminate the medium infrared spectrum.
The European Extremely Large Telescope is also planned at the ESO Chilean Observatory.
Its main mirror is said to cover a whopping 42 meters - and be assembled from almost 1000 mirror elements.
However, pictures are to be expected in 2018 at the earliest.
Until 2007, the two Keck telescopes on the Hawaiian volcano Mauna Kea were considered the largest in the world.
They offer two mirrors with an average of ten meters each.
The Keck telescopes are part of the Mauna-Kea observatory, which next to them with the Subaru telescope and the IRTTF.
A new, huge telescope is also to be created on the Mauna Kea - with a mirror average of thirty metres.
Here it can be admired on an illustration.
The most important impressions from space, however, are provided by the Hubble Space Telescope.
Since April 24, 1990, it already provides images of distant worlds.
Since March 2009, the Kepler space telescope has been searching for extrasolar planets – primarily those that are habitable.
On February 2, 2011, NASA announced that 1235 planet candidates have been identified since the mission began.
In the picture, the last launch preparations are documented at the Kepler space telescope.
The James Webb Space Telescope (JWST) will not be launched into space aboard an Ariane5 rocket at the earliest in 2018.
The primary mirror of the space infrared telescope has a diameter of 6.5 meters.
One of the telescope's tasks: it's supposed to search for light from the first stars and galaxies after the Big Bang.
Scientists assume that ice also exists at the south pole of Mercury.
However, there is no reliable data for this, as Messenger circles far closer to the North Pole around the planet.
Radar measurements have suggested for decades that there is ice on Mercury.
Due to the messenger probe launched in 2004 - the first to revolve around Mercury - the scientists now have certainty.
Drinking butter every day - and being 168 years old
In the south of Azerbaijan, many people reach a biblically old age.
There’s even a museum of longevity.
A search for traces in the country where 97-year-olds are still comparatively young.
In the south of Azerbaijan, many people reach a biblically old age.
There’s even a museum of longevity.
A search for traces in the country where 97-year-olds are still comparatively young.
The drive through the Talysh Mountains is wildly romantic.
The minibus rumbles across the winding road, past densely wooded hills, rippling rivers and simple farmhouses.
Everything is green and juicy, you might think you are in the Black Forest.
However, this is the deepest sourth of Azerbaijan, the border with Iran is only a few kilometers away.
Here lives the Caucasian ethnic group of the Talyschen, of which not much is known, except that it speaks perfectly Persian as well as Azerbaijani and is very durable.
Terminus is Lerik.
The small town flaunts with too bulky architecture from Soviet times, which does not want to fit into the picturesque mountain landscape.
Tourists from Europe hardly come here, too arduous is the approach from Azerbaijan's capital Baku.
For the 323 kilometers you need eight hours, because the road is only one-lane.
The fabulous wealth that the land owes to its oil in the Caspian Sea has not yet arrived here in the province.
Yet Pilata Fatulayeva (48) is convinced that Lerik has what it takes to become a tourist attraction.
"Baku became famous in May with the Eurovision song festival - and next year we have a festival of the oldest people in the world here," Fatulayeva says.
She's the museum's director of longevity, arguably the one in the world.
Here's the vita of about eight dozen Talyschen from the area, which is older than 100. Fatulayeva points to a black-and-white photo.
This one's my grandfather, he was 120 years old.
At the age of 136 years still a child conceived
But controversial star of the museum is Şirali Müslümov, who is said to have turned 168 years old.
However, there is no birth certificate.
And in view of the oldest documented person, who turned 122 years old, Müslümov's age nevertheless seems extremely doubtful.
"In 1805 he was born here in the region, he died in 1973," Fatulayeva explains.
The man had been married three times and had 23 children, at the age of 136 he is said to have fathered a daughter.
So Şirali Mueslümov miscalculated for a few decades at his age?
But Rembrandt Scholz, age researcher at the Max Planck Institute in Rostock, has also heard about the elderly in Central Asia.
"Remarkably many high-aged people there are also in some areas of China, in Japan or in the Hunza Valley in Pakistan," says Scholz, "even in Sardinia live extremely many very old men."
However, due to the lack of documents, there was no scientific proof of age, especially since civil status registers were missing.
Every day beaking molten butter
However, the fact is that the people of the region around Lerik strikingly often reach a biblical age.
There are currently 20 people older than 100 years.
So why are there so many elderly people living here in the south?
The Azerbaijani travel guide Farid Mugimzadeh explains this with the special genes of the Talyschen.
Museum director Fatulayeva, on the other hand, thinks it's because of the diet.
But the fact that the high-calorie diet of the Talyschen is supposed to be so healthy, who love meat, bread and above all dairy products and many of which drink a glass of molten butter every day, does not seem plausible from a nutritional point of view.
Or is it the original way of life that keeps people young? Rubaba Mirzayeva resides in Cengemiran, a tiny settlement not far from the town of Lerik.
At the age of 97, she is still comparatively young for these latitudes.
Mirzayeva, who claims to have 143 descendants, lives in a simple wooden house typical of the entire Caucasus region.
She sits on the floor with a butter barrel that rolls her tirelessly.
Eight people live here under one roof, including one of Mirzayeva's sons and a daughter, both have long been grandparents.
Two toddlers are also jumping around.
In the kitchen, guests are prepared tea, which is served in the typical Armadu glasses.
Mirzayeva's white teeth stand perfectly in row and link, under her headscarf she hides long, dark blonde braids that her son proudly reveals before us.
I've always washed my hair with milk, so they've never run out and kept their color.
"Shampoo I've never used either," Mirzayeva says.
Monthly retirement is enough to live
She's always eaten only what came from her own farm, tomatoes, potatoes, peas.
Throughout my life, I haven't bought groceries in the supermarket once.
Then she tells of her husband, who was in the army.
Worst was in the period after World War II.
However, everything got better when "beloved father" Heydar Aliyew took the helm.
The propaganda sounds strange from an old woman's mouth.
Yet the cult driven around the nation's father figure who ruled his country like a dictator knows hardly any borders in Azerbaijan.
Until 2003 he was in power, later his son Ilham took the helm.
After all, there is no hardship among Azerbaijan's elderly.
230 manat (about the same sum in euros), Mirzayeva receives monthly as a pension, for local conditions an amount from which one can live well.
And perhaps Mirzayeva's long-greying son is right: "The old enjoy respect in our culture."
They live in the midst of the extended family, are loved, cared for and are happy.
If that's not a reason to stay alive for as long as possible
The word "human rights" is missing from the constitution
The revolution has returned to Cairo.
Rival rallies in Cairo reveal the country's deep division.
The future constitution with Sharia law as a base is fiercely disputed.
The Egyptian president didn't spar with pathos.
We have to make the transition.
"And that he succeeds is my responsibility, before the people and before God," he said on state television.
His speech was addressed to the whole people, but above all to the Christian Copts, the liberals, enlightened Muslims and seculars.
Because all of them, so far hopelessly divided in a confused opposition, are afraid.
Fear of a state of God on the Nile of graces of the mighty Muslim Brotherhood.
According to Mohammed Morsi almost apologetically, he had temporarily curtailed the competences of the Constitutional Court and expanded his competences "to save the revolution."
Yet what the 61-year-old engineer, who holds a doctorate from the American University of Southern California, really wants to save, the Egyptians are not quite clear about.
Should the judiciary be disempowered?
In fact, the 234 articles whipped through by the Islamist-dominated 100-member Constituent Assembly in a nocturnal, 15-hour parforce ride are partly worrying.
As in previous constitutions, the draft states that jurisprudence is based on "principles of Islamic law".
But what does "principles" mean?
This was and is a matter of interpretation and it is to be feared that the Islamists will use the spongy formulation and the accompanying legal scope for a stricter interpretation of Shariah.
This suggests at least a newly added article: in all matters concerning Sharia, Al-Ashar University must be consulted, the country’s most important Islamic institution with great radiancence in all Sunni Islam.
This doesn't have to, but it can mean that the clergy oversees the legislation, which would de facto result in the incapacitation of the judiciary.
Much in the draft constitution is a matter of interpretation
Also problematic: The civilian military jurisdiction is to be maintained further.
These courts served during Mubarak's domination for the suppression of opposition activists.
After the dictator's overthrow, up to 11,000 civilians were in military custody.
The state should also, according to the draft, “protect the true essence of the Egyptian family” “and promote their morals and their values”.
This is formulated legally so blurred that with the help of this article, state institutions could even control the contents of film art and literature.
In plain English, this means nothing but censorship.
By the way, no article explicitly stipulates equality between men and women.
For this, another forbides insulting or slandering the Prophet Muhammad and his messengers.
But what an insult is and how it should be punished remains unclear.
Equally dubious, the wording suggests that the "insulting of people" is forbidden.
Is there already a caricature of the president or a joke at the expense of a lawyer?
A matter of interpretation, such as so much in the draft that Morsi is available for signature and, according to his words "very soon," will be presented to the Egyptians for the referendum.
“The revolution has returned.”
The opposition has been forming for weeks against the superiority of Islamists.
Tens of thousands gathered in unfamiliar consequency on Friday night in Cairo's Tahrir Square and vowed to bring down the Charter even before it can enter into effect.
"The revolution has returned and we will win," said Hamdin Sabbahi, third-placer of the presidential election.
Nobel Peace Prize laureate and ex-head of International Atomic Energy Agency Mohamed El-Baradei declared that the draft constitution belonged "on the rubbish mountain of history."
Via short news service Twitter, he accused the supporters oF Morsi to lead a "coup against democracy".
"If he declares the referendum, we will go to his palace and overthrow him," opposition activist Jasser Said said.
"We aren't tired yet, the blood of our brothers hasn't been atoned yet," the Egyptian media quoted opposition politician Chaled Ali as saying.
And several judgees announced they did not want to oversee the referendum, making it invalid.
"The Qur'an is our Constitution"
For its part, the well-organized Muslim Brotherhood blewed to the counter-demonstration, but as a precaution not in Tahrir Square, but after a mass prayer on the other bank of the Nile in front of Cairo University.
Many veiled women and adherents of the Salafists participated and shouted, "The people demand the application of God's law."
They demanded of Morsi: "Clean the land!" and asserted: "The Qur'an is our constitution."
A struggle for sovereignty over the symbolic Tahrir Square, on which it all began, would probably have conjured up civil war-like conditions.
Morsi's supporters obviously didn't want to risk that.
The Muslim Brotherhood said that both the opponents of the draft constitution and the proponents had expressed themselves loudly and clearly.
Now is the time to let the people decide at the ballot box in which direction the country should go.
A majority in favour of the Islamists' design is considered safe.
“The word human rights doesn’t even occur”
Hafez Abu Saeda is angry about this forced constitutional process, which should actually have gone until February and involve all societal forces.
The 48-year-old human rights lawyer and chairman of Egypt's Organization for Human Rights (EOHR) defended the Muslim Brotherhood when they were in jail under Mubarak or stood trial.
Not because he would share their worldview, but because human rights are indivisible to him.
For this, he was beaten, convicted, imprisoned.
"And now the word human rights is not even in the new constitution," he lamented in conversation with "Welt am Sonntag".
Morsi's expansion of power to all three powers in the state had the lawyer resigned.
These measures are glaring violations of democratic rules of the game and will lead Egypt into a new dictatorship.
"Instead of strengthening civil society, the president is de facto overriding them," Saeda laments.
But without civil society organizations, a democracy cannot work.
Saeda feels left alone, even by the international community, which considers the ideological directional struggle on the Nile with a mixture of curiosity and suspense.
That might take revenge.
A protester on the Tahrir warns: "You're wearing a monster that you can't control anymore."
Rakfisk from Norway: Is this the world's most foul-smelling fish?
Norway's five million inhabitants enjoy one of the highest standards of living - not only in Europe, but around the world.
Could the country's secret to success have anything to do with the local appetite for pretty foul-smelling fish?
Take a selection of overripe cheeses.
You place them in the middle of a pile of dirty, wet sportswear.
And leave them there for a week.
For example, you have to imagine the nose-deafening smell of Rakfisk, one of Norway's great delicacies.
I'm located in the small town of Fagernes, about three hours from Oslo.
There's snow here, spectacular landscapes - and that stubborn smell in the air that just doesn't want to disappear.
Rakfisk is trout sprinkled with salt, which - depending on how stinking you would like to have your fish - is fermented in water for up to a year.
When the darkness comes across the country in Norway and the weather gets colder, Norwegians flock to a festival here in Fagernes, which is dedicated to this, well, really very captivating specialty.
"You eat it raw and then rinse it down with a glass of aquavit," says Havard Halvarsen, who actually works at the local firefight station, but as a so-called "Rakfisk-General" also hosts the festival.
Around us, people consume small fish cubes and consume large amounts of alcohol.
"Cruple people like the aquavit more than the rakfisk," Havard says.
The spirit can displace the odor.
I'll taste a few bites.
If you avoid bringing the rakfisk close to the nose, it'sn't bad - like sushi, for example, which has a rather long bus ride behind it.
Rakfisk is the product of a very different, far poorer Norwegian era, when there were no cooling possibilities and fish was soaked in airtight saltwater barrels in autumn.
In the deepest winter, he was then taken out after long fermentation and consumed - undoubtedly under undempered senses with the help of alcohol.
Even a generation ago, thousands of Norwegians in search of work were forced to leave their country and mostly emigrated to the United States.
Today, the population is growing rapidly - more than 13% are immigrants, attracted by a thriving labour market, high wages and comprehensive healthcare.
Even from Sweden, the old rival that was far richer than Norway not so long ago, immigrants are flocking.
Rakfisk is seen as something important - as an essential, albeit strict-smelling part of Norwegian history.
It’s one of the more expensive dishes you can buy here.
But actually everything here is expensive - a small glass of beer or a sandwich costs a proud 10 euros.
Norway doesn't often make it into the world's press, and most people don't seem to bother that.
Here it's still hated to call Anders Breivik by name - that racist right-wing extremist who shot 77 men, women and children last year.
Instead, one refers only to the "July 22 incident."
Norwegians struggle to accept that someone in their peace-loving country is capable of such brutal murders.
Since the early 1970s, the growth of one of the world’s largest oil and gas industries has been mainly responsible for today’s wealth of Norway.
"But oil isn't the only reason we're doing so well," says Anna, our waitress who distributes round trays with ripe rakfisk and embodies the Nordic ideal with her long blonde hair and stabbing blue eyes.
We are, as they say, prudent people.
Their English, just like that of most people here, is spotless.
We'ren't very suggestive, we don't like boasting.
Norway has been very cautious with its wealth of oils - a large part of the industry's yields are invested in a special fund for the benefit of future generations.
When in the years up to the Great Depression, everyone threw money around them that they didn’t own at all, Norway kept its purse firmly closed.
"As long as we can ski in the winter and go hiking in the summer, we're happy," Anna says.
"And eating rakfisk, of course," she adds with carefree laughter.
I'm standing in the sneering for a different dish - I've had enough of Rakfisk.
Such an elk burger is definitely something different. The juicy meat's mouthing me.
But in the evening the stink fish is again on the menu.
The hotel where I'm staying is one of several venues organizing a rakfisk dinner where guests can tune for the best - or perhaps the smoke most challenging - fish.
There's a live TV circuit to a fly-clothed showmaster surrounded by plates with rakfisk.
The spectacle resembles the Eurovision Song Contest.
"Which score do you have up there in the mountains for the best fish, Thor-Jürgen?"
“Here are our points, Havard.”
You clap and laugh.
A man falls from his chair, possibly overwhelmed by the aquavit.
But maybe it's the severe haze of the whole fish.
Mexico's Enrique Pena Nieto faces difficult start
As Mexico's future president, Enrique Pena Nieto, prepares to take office, Will Grant of the BBC analyzes the challenges facing him and the mixed feelings of his people.
Currently, the traffic in Mexico City is particularly bad.
Even on better days, this is an always clogged city, but on Monday a ring was erected, which cordons off several important traffic routes to the capital and causes chaos on the roads.
The goal, however, wasn't disturbing commuters on the way to work, but preventing protesters from reaching parliament.
On Saturday, Mexico's new President Enrique Pena Nieto will receive the presidential bandage and take the leadership of the nation.
He faces a complex task.
Under Felipe Calderon's outgoing government, Mexico has been able to prove itself economically, but the country is in the grip of a drug war that has already cost 60,000 lives over the past six years.
"My administration is strongly committed to reducing the violence," Pena Nieto told U.S. President Barack Obama earlier this week at the Oval Office.
I will propose a new security strategy with which we can achieve this goal.
Before going on an equal footing with the U.S. president, Pena Nieto served as the governor of his native Mexico.
In this densely populated, sprawling part of the country that surrounds the capital, opinions about the new head of state are divided.
A direct man
For example, in the remote small town of Valle del Bravo you like to remember him.
Residents of the holiday resort attribute to him the development of tourism and the construction of infrastructure.
To reach the city, you can drive on one of the highways that Pena Nieto had built and which represents a great improvement over the brittle, bumpy roads that once existed here.
Plaques with its name also hang outside a modern sports center and an impressive interactive museum on climate change.
"We are expectinging from him real and lasting changes," according to his friend and political ally Gabriel Olvera Hernandez, who works as a congressman for Pena Nieto's party, the PRI.
In particular, as far as security and the economy are concerned, we hope for an interesting and real change that our country so desperately needs.
After 81 years in power, the PRI was replaced by Vicente Fox in 2000.
Congressman Olvera admits that after 12 years outside Los Pino's presidential palace, the party has high expectations of Enrique Pena Nieto.
He rejects the opposition’s accusations that the new president lacks substance.
He is a very direct man, very committed and has big plans for the country.
He is an excellent statesman, but above all he is also someone who can listen.
But on the other side of the state, not many people share this view about their former governor.
In Nezahualcoyotl, also known as Ciudad Neza, the contrast with the cobbled streets of Valle del Bravo could not be greater.
The place is hidden under highway overpasses and in many ways represents a suburb of Mexico City itself.
And the problems in the community are of a crucial urban nature.
Earlier this year, the military was involved to fight the drug gands operating in the neighborhoods, and violence against women is a particularly acute problem.
On a patch of fallow land near a major landfill, the bodies of dozens of murdered women have been left behind in the past two years alone.
During Governor Pena Nieto’s tenure, more than 1,000 women have been murdered in the state of Mexico – a statistic that surpasses even that of Ciudad Juarez, which is notorious for its violence and the murders of innocent women.
Criticians Pena Nietos are of the opinion that he at least did not adequately address the problem of women's murders during his tenure.
But in the worst case, his government closed its eyes to the problem.
In a concrete building, typical of the run-down neighborhood, Irinea Buendia struggles with the tears as she shows me photos of her late daughter Mariana Luna.
According to the official version of events, Mariana committed suicide in 2010.
However, her family believes that she was murdered by her partner.
"When I arrived at her home, it seemed that her body had been washed," recalls Senora Buendia.
There were signs that she was beaten, and the mortuary rigidity had already deployed.
As her mother tells her story, a picture of Mariana looks down at us from the wall. It's attached next to a cross that carries a single word: justice.
But this, according to the family, has been denied her until now.
The authorities have treated me as if I was a talker, a troublemaker, a narrower.
They want their judgment to be simply accepted and shut up.
"But that's not possible when there have been so many formal errors and omissions," she says.
When President Pena Nieto receives the presidential bandage on Saturday, he also assumes a huge responsibility.
Tens of thousands of Mexican families have been afflicted by violent crimes over the past six years, and the new president has promised to take special care of this problem during his tenure.
"I hope as president he's the way he was as governor," says PRI congressman Olvera in Valle del Bravo.
Yet that's exactly what the families of the victims in Ciudad Neza are most afraid of.
Prosecutors claim Bradley Manning didn't complain about mistreatment
Prosecutors contradict Bradley Manning's accusations of being mistreated in prison
The hearing deals with Manning's time at military prison in Quantico, Virginia
The defense seeks a dismissal of the indictment based on harsh conditions of detention
U.S. Army Privateer Is Accused of Stealing Thousands of Confidential Documents
Prosecutors on Friday attempted to determine that Army private Bradley Manning - accused of the biggest revelation of confidential information in U.S. history - missed several occasions to complain about the mistreating he allegedly suffered in military custody.
During a cross-examination of Manning during a pre-trial hearing in Ft. Meade, Maryland, Maj. Ashden Fein explained that documents on weekly visits made by prison officers during Manning's nine-month detention in Quantico, Virginia, had no complaints about his treatment.
A day before the cross-examination, which was held as a result of a request by the defense to dismiss the indictment - Manning had already been adequately punished by the trial period of imprisonity - Manning had testified that he had considered suicide in prison.
U.S. Army intelligence analyst, who was arrested in June 2010, is accused of stealing thousands of confidential documents while serving in Iraq.
The material was then published by WikiLeaks on the internet.
WikiLeaks has never confirmed Manning as a source of information.
At Friday's hearing, Fein went through with Manning the forms that officials had filled out after their meetings with Manning during his detention at Quantico military prison, where he had been detained from July 2010 to April 2011 under heightened detention conditions.
Officers asked Manning questions and wrote down his answers.
When Fein questioned him about the forms on Friday, Manning admitted that he had judged the treatment by his guards as "excellent" and the treatment by the facility as "very professional" overall.
The forms, according to Fein, contained no complaints about mistreatment, even though officers had questioned Manning directly about his treatment.
Manning replied that he had orally expressed his concerns and the officials had discussed them with him and indicated that they had dealt with them. However, they had not been logged.
"They wrote (after discussinging the concerns) "no problems," and that didn't necessarily mean I didn't say anything at all," according to Manning.
The judge, Colonel Denise Lind, also asked Manning why he didn't complain about his treatment when he met in January 2011 with a committee that was supposed to investigate suicidal thoughts mentioned by him on a form a few months earlier.
Manning responded that his intention during that meeting was to downgrade his observational status for "prevention of injuries."
The military testified that it had assigned him this restrictive status - which is one level under the threat of suicide - to his and the protection of others.
"I wanted to convince employees that I was doing well, and to get rid of the observational statud for the prevention of injury (...) in order to be able to achieve a higher quality of life in my view," Manning said.
Manning testified Thursday for his arrest and relocation to Kuwait, where he was detained for nearly two months before being transferred to the Quantico naval base prison in July 2010.
He said that in Kuwait he had considered the suicide and had once fallen unconscious due to the heat.
To not be allowed to know what is happening to him or in the outside world had been stressful.
"My world shrank to Camp Arafjon, onto this cage combined," Manning said Thursday.
I thought I'd died in that cage.
When he was transferred to Quantico, Manning had spent the majority of his days in a small cell - at least 21 hours and often more than 23 hours - and without company.
He was only allowed a mattress, a blanket, flip-flops, clothes and his glasses.
Manning said he tried to keep moving, because during the day it had been forbidden to sleep or even lie down.
He always had to sleep with light that entered his cell from the outside.
If he had turned around at night and guards could no longer see his face, he was woken up so that he could turn back.
In January 2011, Manning's lawyer formally objected to Manning's treatment.
In April 2011, Manning was transferred to Fort Leavenworth's, Kansas military prison.
On Friday, Judge Manning also questioned an allegation he had made in his testimony on Thursday - so he had to sleep one night naked in his cell in Quantico and stand naked in front of guards and other prisoners at a morning count.
Manning had testified that he had never had the opportunity to envelop himself with his blanket during this count.
When questionered on Friday, Manning stated that he had concluded from an order of his guard that he was supposed to drop the blanket with which he could have covered. However, he admitted that no one had asked him to do so.
On Thursday, Manning had testified that he was forced to sleep nude the previous night. He would have previously tried to show an official that he poses no danger to himself.
Manning had told the officer that he could have used the waistband of his underpants or his flip-flops to hurt himself, but did not.
On that night, Manning testified, his underpants, flip-flops and glasses were removed from his cell.
His lawyers hope that the judge will at least consider his imprisonment experiences and drastically reduce his imprisonment should he be convicted at his trial in the military court, which is scheduled for the beginning of next year.
The defense has indicated that Manning will plead guilty to lesser offenses, while other charges deemed to be too extreme are to be challenged.
The continuation of the hearing is scheduled for this weekend. A justification of detention conditions is expected from the prosecutor's office.
The Pentagon is sticking to its position that Manning has been detained under the provisions that apply to all detainees detaineed under the highest security precautions in Quantico.
Charges against Manning include favoring the enemy, unlawful publication of intelligence on the Internet, transmitting national defense information, and stealing public property or documents.
If he is found guilty on all charges, he faces a life sentence.
My Mexican-American identity crisis.
Many people are forced to leave Mexico due to the lack of opportunities
Mexicans tend to accuse the emigrators - they remind them of hard times, he says
Navarrette believes Mexican Americans are caught between two worlds
On a recent trip to Mexico City, I had just crossed the hall and had arrived at passport control when perplexity spread within me.
Signs referred to two queues: one for "Mexicanos" ("Mexicans"), another for "Extranjeros" ("foreigners").
I stood there for a few seconds, unsure which I was supposed to go.
Grew up in central California and I've been called "Mexican" all my life.
An ethnic abbreviation - just as my friends in Boston call themselves "Iren" or my friends in New York "Italians."
Later, I opted for "Mexican-American."
But this was Mexico.
And in my grandfather's home, there was no need for abbreviations or hyphens.
I was just American.
I speak Spanish, at least good enough to assert myself in this language at both ends of an interview.
But I don't have the vocabulary of a native speaker, and I don't get rid of my American accent either.
So I took my American passport and queued up for Extranjeros.
I was thinking about that moment this week when future Mexican President Enrique Pena Nieto visited the White House to meet with President Obama.
On the agenda were all those issues that one expects when the minds of these two countries meet: immigration, drugs and trade.
Pena Nieto was also very interested in talking about the growth of the Mexican economy. She is one reason that Mexicans these days are equally likely to stay in Mexico instead of emigrating to the United States.
He wants to ally himself with the United States and Canada and create a North American trading bloc modeled by the European Union.
And Pena Nieto vowed to continue Mexico's war against the drug cartels, even if he did not give specifics in this regard.
Mexico's relationship with the United States is complicated and full of resentment.
Most Americans presumably never waste a single thought that the United States invaded Mexico in 1848 and forced the leadership of the country, at gunpoint, to leave them more than half their territory.
But for Mexicans who think in centuries rather than minutes, memories of this event can't be overlooked.
So as soon as a U.S. government official draws even the mildest criticism of Mexico, one hears - in the Mexican press and among the country's elites - complaints about how Americans are harassing the sovereignty of their neighboring country.
And Montezuma's children embark on the war trail.
And yet Mexico's biggest challenge, in truth, lies in the relationship with the more than 35 million Mexican Americans living in the United States.
Do you want to talk about resentment?
There's more than enough.
Mexico has winners and losers. People for whom the country offers opportunities, and others for whom it does not.
The only reason why so many people of Mexican design live in cities like Los Angeles, Las Vegas, Phoenix, Denver, or San Antonio is that at one point in our pedigree, a person - perhaps a parent or grandparent - didn't get a chance in Mexico and emigrated north.
And in most cases, this person corresponded to a profile - dark-skinned, uneducated, from a poor village, etc.
We are the descendants of these men, and our faithfulness applies to them.
Not Mexico.
And even though we may now be living the American dream, attending good schools and receiving good jobs, we must never lose sight that it's the American dream we're living - not the Mexican one.
Our identity may sometimes be blurred, but as far as our loyalty is concerned, this is not the case.
This clearly applies to the United States.
What's more, we're aware that many members of Mexico's leadership don't like us.
This is based on reciprocity.
They see us as a memorial of a humiliating defeat and look down on us because we are not Mexican enough.
Our Spanish will never be good enough, our ties to Mexico will never be strong enough.
In their opinion, our existence is a symbol of failure.
If our families hadn't failed in Mexico, they wouldn't have left.
And then we wouldn't see ourselves trapped behind the silk curtain - with a pleasant life in the United States, but somehow lost.
My wife, who was born in Guadalajara and immigrated legally to the United States as a child, reminds me that there is tensions between Mexicans and Mexicans because Mexicans have a clearer idea of who they are - and Mexican Americans resent it.
While she is a U.S. citizen, she sees herself as part of two countries.
Yet many Mexican Americans I know have the feeling of not belonging to either country.
We love the Mexican band Los Tigres del Norte, but also Bruce Springsteen.
One enjoys the best of both worlds, but none of them are rooted.
We are seen as Americans in Mexico.
And in the United States, we're seen as Mexicans.
As I should learn during my trip, there's something that makes this relationship even more complicated: some Mexican leaders and parts of the education bourgeoisie want to resume their ties to the diaspora.
They want to use Mexican Americans as provisional "ambassadors" for Mexico who represent their interests in the United States.
We are supposed to tell our American colleagues about how great a visit to this country is and put pressure on the political leadership to intensify their ties with Mexico.
Well.
That won't happen.
Too many hurt feelings.
And given the unjust distribution of income, unbridled corruption and drug violence, many of us aren’t so sure if it’s really a great country.
I'm afraid you're gonna have to go on your own, Amigos.
It's fair.
If at least some Mexicans aren’t ready to forgive the United States for dealing with Mexico a century and a half ago, they must also accept that some Mexican Americans hold a resentment of how their family members were treated not so long agove.
Hmmm.
Mayble we're "Mexican" than I thought.
Old skirmishes, new Middle East
The ceasefire between Israel and Hamas could turn out to be an unexpected cornerstone for peace
Can there ever lasting peace between Arabs and Jews in the Middle East?
The latest bloody events seem to stifle such hope in the bud.
In the midst of the usual admissions of guilt, countless buildings have been laid in ruins, more than 140 Palestinians, most of them civilians, and six Israelis killed - and for the first time, rockets have smashed from Gaza near the Israeli metropolis of Tel Aviv and the holy city of Jerusalem.
Yet even though Israelis and Palestinians seem trapped in their historical conflict, the Middle East is changing around them.
The Arab Spring has created a new, uncertain situation, and whether they want it or not - Palestinians and Israelis have become part of this turmoil.
Maybe this will only lead to more bloodshed.
Yet there are reasons to believe that the deadly stalemate could finally come to an end.
A war that is neither lost nor won
At first glance, optimism seems anything but appropriate given the current situation.
Even if the ceasefire agreed on November 21, this week's fighting has empowered the hardened on both sides.
The leaders of Hamas, the Islamist movement that controls the Gaza Strip since 2007, will claim to have pushed the Israelis back, even though his group itself has suffered huge losses.
Although several Hamas leaders are killed and Gaza's 1.7 million inhabitants are cramped into one of the most desolate and densely populated corners of the planet, Israel has so far failed to destroy Hamas.
In fact, Hamas is gaininging in the West Bank, which is controlled by the rival, moderate Palestinian group Fatah.
And Hamas leaders could come to the conclusion that they have time on their side.
Just as Islamists have gained influence in the Arab world, Hamas has been able to draw powerful and rich friends to their side.
Turkey, a resurgenting, regional power once considered Israel's closest Muslim ally, has taken sides for Hamas - just like Qatar, one of the richest and most dynamic Gulf states.
Cheering Hamas supporters believe an Islamist crescent is forming around Israel - from Lebanon in the north, where militant Hezbollah has power, via Syria, where rebels of an increasingly Islamist imprintination could be facing Bashar Assad's fall, to Jordan, where Hamas allies threaten the king.
And in particular, the rise of the Muslim Brotherhood under President Muhammad Morsi in Egypt - by far the most populous and most important Arab country - on the southern flank of Israel has changed the balance of the region sustainably.
Hosni Mubarak, the secular despot who ruled Egypt for 30 years until his 2011 overthrow, had little time left for Hamas.
In contrast, the Brotherhood is sister-in-law with Hamas and its leaders are more dependent on people's views.
In future diplomatic negotiations, Hamas could prove to be an actor who cannot be pushed out even by Israel and the United States.
The Israeli hardliners, on the other hand, allow an opposite conclusion to be drawn.
Militarily speaking, Hamas is no longer a danger.
Israel's missile defense system, "Iron Dome," has proved its worth and many of Hamas rockets have been destroyed.
At least for a while, Israel will be able to sleep more soundly.
In diplomatic terms, the United States steadfastly adheres to its position. Many European countries have blamed Hamas for the latest escalations of violence.
Israel has done well in recent years, particularly under Prime Minister Binyamin Netanyahu, who has largely ignored the peace process.
Although rockets from the Gaza Strip have killed about 30 Israelis since 2004, the country has remained relatively free of suicide bombers - not least thanks to the barrier facilities that run into the West Bank and thus into the main part of a possible Palestinian state. These protect the Jewish settlements, which have been declared contrary to international law, but are constantly being expanded.
Netanyahu stands up well. His Likud party has teamed up with an even more combative group in the run-up to the January 22 elections under Avigdor Lieberman.
Why even these deceitful Palestinians coddle and give them their own state?
If they were indeed in control of the West Bank, wouldn't they fire rockets just like their colleagues in the Gaza Strip?
No, it's better to keep them in check behind the wall and punish, should they dare to raise their heads.
Maybe the hardliners will be able to prevail, but the Arab Spring could put a dash through the bill.
Even if the Islamists, who are now in power in Egypt and in other countries, have little left for Israel, they will initially focus on the difficulties in their own country.
Israel's defense budget is larger than that of its four Arab neighbors combined.
A war with the local superpower will hardly help the new Arab governments to repair their economies.
That pragmatic President Morsi teamed up with Barack Obama to reach a ceasefire could be an auspicious sign - and perhaps symbolize the beginning of a new era.
Israelis should also think longer-term.
While the rest of the Arab world is taking tentative steps towards democratization, Palestinians will continue to be deprived of their right of self-determination - a powder keg that will undoubtedly one day explode in the Israeli-occupied territories, just as a bus exploded in Tel Aviv this week.
Repression is already undermining democracy in the Jewish state, and with the growth of the Arab population, this situation will only intensify.
Bloody missions to repatriate Hamas in the Gaza Strip will increasingly take their diplomatic toll over the years.
Both sides need an impetus from the outside
The correct answer is that is represented by the more reasonable representatives of both sides, the majority of the rest of the world and by this newspaper: two states where Israel is ceding territories for security purposes.
The - for the near-future small - hope is that the ceasefire grants outsiders more influence to enforce this solution.
Egypt, together with Turkey and Karta, must now stop arms shipments in the Gaza Strip and is better positioned than ever before to convince Hamas of the idea of a Jewish state based on the 1967 borders, complemented by territorial exchanges and a common Jerusalem.
Outsiding Arab nations should also persuade Hamas and Fatah to reach an agreement.
This would do more for the creation of a Palestinian state than with the forthcoming application for UN observer status.
Obama, too, needs to do his part to persuade Israel to negotiate.
During his first tenure, he missed the notion of his own peace plan.
Far away in the White House, it seems as if he is also averse to involvement.
But that’s an unfortunate, short-sighted perspective.
The United States has an essential interest in a stable Middle East.
This includes a peace agreement between Israel and the Palestinians.
Australian laws for uniform cigarette packaging come into force
Health warnings and diseased body parts on neutral green packs that are the same for all tobacco brands
The world’s world’s first laws for uniform cigarette and tobacco packaging have come into force in Australia, replacing brand logos and colours with neutral, olive green lids, which are also printed with drastic images showing sick body parts and infants and children suffering from cigarette smoke.
Apart from the different health warnings and pictures from next Saturday, there is the only difference between the packs in the brand names, which are reproduced in identical, small font.
In doing so, Australia introduces the world’s strictest regulations for tobacco packaging.
The Australian federal government wants to dissuade young people from smoking by eliminating the glamorous picture of tobacco use.
In doing so, the government assumes studies that have shown that people who haven't yet started smoking at 26 are 999% likely to stop starting after that.
"Even among children of young ages, you can see that they understand the message that tobacco corporations are trying to promote with their brand image," says Federal Health Minister Tanya Plibersek. She quotes, for example, that have shown that children associate the crown of a logo with the idea of a princess.
Although Australia has one of the world's lowest smoking quotas and the changes will have little effect on the profits of multinational corporations, other countries are already considering similar steps.
The tobacco lobby fought hard against the laws.
It was said that the laws would favor the black market trade, which would lead to cheaper and more easily accessible cigarettes.
"The laws will lead to serious, unintended consequences," says Scott McIntyre of British American Tobacco Australia.
Chinese and Indonesian forgerers will offer far larger volumes of these products on the streets of Australia.
Others, on the other hand, say the laws have boosted their business.
Sandra Ha of Zico Import Pty Ltd, a small family business, notes that demand for cigarette cases and silicone lids to cover the unbearable packaging has risen enormously since the corporations British American Tobacco, Britain's Imperial Tobacco, Philip Morris and Japan Tobacco failed two months ago at Australia's supreme court with their contestation of laws.
Zico has solded up to 6,000 units to wholesale stores and is waiting for new holdings.
It's good business for us.
According to experts, the snack is in the popularity of social media with exactly the population that the plan is addressed.
A number of Australian laws have banned tobacco companies from TV advertising, forbiding them from acting as sports sponsors and forcing most traders to stop laying out cigarettes in stores. Now tobacco advertising has switched to the internet.
The Australian government has banned internet advertising by local businesses and sites, but has no influence on international sites.
"Anyone who markets tobacco and has only a small area left to promote their products can't get around the internet," says Becky Freeman, a health care researcher at the University of Sydney.
On social media such as YouTube, Twitter and Facebook, Freeman has noticed an increase in testimonials on brands written by "quite ordinary people."
One has to ask oneself the question: is this just a private person who finds such great pleasure in Marlboro cigarettes that she even records a video for it - or does a marketing agency have to do with it?
British American Tobacco Australia says the industry, rather than marketing, is focused on dealing with the new laws.
It has even gone so far as to pay Ukraine, Honduras and the Dominican Republic to contest the new regulations - those countries are complaining to the World Trade Organization about unjust trade restrictions, although none of the countries engage in greater trade with Australia.
A WTO decision is expecteded for mid-2013.
According to Plibersek, the government has held talks with other countries considering similar packaging laws.
Canada was the first country to impose photographic warnings in 2001.
Meanwhile, these have been extended to more than 40 countries, including Brazil, Turkey and Ukraine.
Tougher laws are being considered in Britain, New Zealand, South Africa and India.
Many Australian smokers are defiant.
The pictures don't affect me.
I'll just ignore them.
"You just take a dump and put away the box," Victor El Hage says while buying a pack of cigarettes, in which a photo with a mouth tumour is printed.
Honestly, there's only one reason I'd stop, and that's my little girl.
James Yu, operator of tobacco trade "King of the Pack" in downtown Sydney, says the unified packaging has complicated replenishing of its shelves.
"It used to take me an hour to sort in a delivery, now I need four," says Yu.
"The government should've just banned cigarettes completely - then we'd have said okay, that's it, we're shutting down," he adds visibly angrily.
In a constantly electrified world, it's nice to be bored
This Thanksgiving I unexpectedly spent five hours at the airport when our plane dropped out due to mechanical problems and we had to wait for another plane.
So I've had plenty of time to worry about a few.
I don't want to lie.
Waiting half a day at the airport for a flight is quite annoying - even when books, magazines and iPhones (and of course tax-free shopping) are available.
But more and more academics and child development experts are citing boredom as something positive.
If it's after them, it's okay for us - and our kids - to be bored occasionally.
With this, the brain is forced to get lost in interesting trains of thought, which possibly promotes creativity.
And because most of us these days are almost constantly staring at one or the other screen, the benefits of boredom no longer benefit us.
Should we welcome boredom with open arms?
Yeah.
And no.
I'll come back to that again.
Like many people, I have always assumed that boredom is a relatively top-to-the-term phenomenon that has developed with increasing leisure time.
Yet Peter Toohey, professor of Greek and Roman history at the University of Calgary in Canada and author of "Boredom: A Lively History" (Yale University Press, 2011) doesn't see it that way.
"In fact, boredom has a very long history," he says.
Pompeii's house walls adorn Latin graffiti over boredom that dates back to the first century.
And then there's the question of how we actually define boredom.
According to John D. Eastwood, an associate professor of psychology at the University of York in Ontario, Canada, the problem is that boredom has been defined and discussed in many ways.
After a long reading of research literature and the presentation of the idea in front of a focus group of about 100 people, Professor Eastwood and his colleagues came to the conclusion that boredom can be defined as the experience of "wanting to pursue a satisfying activity but not being able to do so".
Boredom differs from apathy in that the person is not busy, but wants to do so.
With apathy, however, there is no urge to do anything.
The key experience of boredom, he says, consists in a "disturbance of the attention process, coupled with a bad mood and the feeling that time passes only slowly."
Boredom can almost sound like depression.
But Professor Eastwood believes that although these two states may be related, bored individuals see the problem in their environment or the world, while depressed individuals see the problem within themselves.
We sometimes think we're boring, even if we're just struggling to concentrate.
In their study "The Unengaged Mind: Defining Boredom in Terms of Attention," for example: "The Idle Mind: The Definition of Boredom from the Aspect of Attention," which appeared in the journal "Perspectives on Psychological Science" in September, Professor Eastwood and his colleagues point to an earlier experiment in which participants listened to a volume on which a person read a magazine article.
Some groups heard a noisy television program from the neighboring room, which had nothing to do with the contents. Others listened to it at a low volume, so it was barely perceptible, while the third group didn't hear these noises at all.
The people who heard the barely audiblevable television said they felt more boredom than the other two groups - they had trouble concentrating, couldn't find any reason for them and attributed their difficulties to boredom.
Tryinging to focusing on a difficult or strenuous task can lead to boredom, says Mark J. Fenske, associate professor of neuroscience at the University of Guelph in Ontario and one of the study's authors.
If, on the other hand, one performs a monotonous activity - "just like the search for faulty products on a factory conveyor belt, music can distract and help not to feel bored".
In fact, he says, we now know that fidgeting and scribbling, often interpreted as signs of boredom, can even fight them by keeping people more physically observant.
"Studies have shown that children who are allowed to limp around learn more and retain more information than those who are forced to sit still," according to Professor Fenske.
We all get bored at some point - my delayed flight, a booming loudspeaker, a particularly tiring movie.
However, some individuals get bored more easily than others.
To measure these differences, explorers developed a "scale for boredom vulnerability" in the 1980s.
On the scale there are statements like "A lot of things I have to do, repeat themselves and are monotonous" and "I have so many interests that I don't have enough time for everything".
Using such scales, researchers have found that boys become boring more often than girls, according to Stephen Vodanovich, professor of psychology at the University of West Florida - especially in terms of the need for more and a greater variety of external stimuli.
But in general, teenagers are a pretty strained bunch.
In 1991, Reed Larson, professor of human and community development at the University of Illinois, conducted an experiment in which he contacted nearly 400 teenagers and their parents seven to eight times a day with a pager.
He found that 32% of adolescents at school were bored when doing homework, while 23% reported being bored when they weren't at school.
However, only 3% of parents said they felt bored.
Professor Larson says today, 21 years later, that he does not know whether the percentages have risen or decreased in terms of boredom.
However, he is certain that "young people are perceived as particularly boring", mainly because children and teenagers do not have a great influence on their daily routine.
So back to my original question: Is boredom healthy?
Sometimes not, because, in extreme cases, it causes people taking extreme physical risks and falling into gambling or drugs to alleviate them - that's what studies have shown.
On the other hand, many philosophers and writers examine the relationship between boredom and creativity. Professor Vodanovich, who has been researching the topic for more than two decades.
"With boredom, the brain wants to tell you that you should do something different," said Gary Marcus, professor of psychology at the University of New York.
But the brain doesn't always know what you're supposed to do best.
If you’re bored and you use that energy to play guitar and cook, you’re going to be happy.
But television can cause feelings of happiness in the short term, but these evaporate again in the long term.
If you give your bored child an iPad, you can dissuade it from boredom, but it hasn’t learned to conversating or controlling yourself,” says Professor Fenske.
And he adds: "Self-control is transferred from one situation to another".
The child not only learns how to conversate, but also receives more self-control in other areas.
I don't think we're supposed to celebrate boredom.
We shouldn't see them too critically either.
Instead, our goal should be to make us feel comfortable away from the constant activity and technology sprinkling.
Professor Eastwood agrees.
"It sounds like we're going to have to be bored more often. But boredom is the excruciating, unceasing desire to do something meaningful," he says.
He believes that, in truth, people are only looking for a way to switch off and relax.
“In an environment where we are constantly overstimulated, it is difficult to engage once the noise is silent.”
There's no clear guideline in Colorado for the new marijuana law
19-year-old community college student and footballer Anthony Orozco is on trial in southeastern Colorado for a misdemeanor that will soon be legal in this state: the possession of a few chunks of marijuana and a whistle with which he smoked them.
According to Orozco, he and some friends were traveling by car in Lamar on a September day when they were stopped.
After the police officer found marijuana in the car, Orozco was summoned for possession of drugs and drug paraphernalia - cavalier offenses, each provided with $100 in penalties - and was given a court date.
"We are being treated like criminals," Orozco said.
But is he also one?
In the uncertain weeks after the state's decision to legalize small amounts of marijuana for its own use, the answer to hundreds of minor drug offences depends less on the law than on the location.
Hundreds of regulatory offenses revolving around marijuana are already being dropped here and in the state of Washington, which approved similar measures.
The police authorities are no longer pursuing adults over the age of possession to a small extent, which will be permitted by law as soon as the new laws come into effect in the next few weeks.
But prosecutors in Colorado's more conservative counties have announced they're prosecuting existing marijuana offences and are still citing people in court.
At the same time, several cities, from the suburbs of Denvern to the Western Mountains, are voting on preventing new, state-run licensed marijuana stores from opening in their communities.
"This situation continues to evolve so quickly that I don't even know what's going to happen next," says Daniel J. Oates, police chief in Aurora, located east of Denver.
And regulators in the state of Washington are also scratching their heads.
They are awaiting guidance to establish a licensing system for the production, manufacture, distribution and sale of the drug - and in doing she have a deadline of 1 December 2013 in mind.
In Washington, it is believed that in any case Colorado is ahead of most states in terms of marijuana regulation, where the drug was legalized first for medical purposes and then for self-use.
"Colorado has a more regulated market. The state therefore lends itself well for guidance," says Brian E. Smith, spokesman for the regulator for liquors in Washington state.
But Smith also admits that no place or system can do more than serve in the sense of a rough guide.
"We don't have a real precedent that we can follow," he adds.
The Washington law, called I-502, comes into force on December 6 and thus also forms the endpoint of a grey area in which the state licensing system doesn't exist, while ownership is legalized.
And unpleasant practical issues must be clarified during this time - such as how the state requirement of "reasonable access" to licensed marijuana is to be agreed with the prohibition of cannabis shops within 300 meters of schools, parks, playgrounds and childcare facilities.
"Nowhere will it be harder to find a location for a licensed cannabis business than in urban areas, particularly in the Seattle metropolitan area," says Ben Livingston, spokesman for the "Center for Legal Cannabis," a recently formed research group.
On November 21, Chief Oates sent an e-mail explaining that the prosecutor would no longer prosecute minor marijuana offences in people over the age of 21, and that the police "as of now" will not seek criminal charges for those offenses.
Chief Oates added that the police will continue to enforce urban guidance to regulate growers of medical marijuana and drug traffickers would continue to be pursued.
In Weld County, northern Colorado, District Attorney Ken Buck takes a stricter view.
After the vote, he stated that his agency would continue to prosecute cases of marijuana possessions, mainly to persuade consumers to deprivate.
Currently, 119 people have to answer for the possession of about 50 grams or less marijuana in court, although many have also been charged for other offences.
"Our authority has a duty to prosecute violations that were criminal offences at the time," Buck said in a statement.
Reactions are ambiguous even in places like rural Mesa County, where voters declined the Marijuana Initiative.
Police in Grand Junction, the county's largest city, no longer indicating adults for possession of smaller quantities.
District Prosecutor Pete Hautzinger supported this decision, but also decided not to drop all pending possession procedures.
"I don't think I'm wasting my time trying to enforce the law until it changes," he says.
Although 55% of Colorado voters supported the measure, it was clear from the very beginning that it would not be easy to reconcile the personal consumption of marijuana with the complexes of government and the legal system.
And the contradictory responses throughout the state raised a profound dispute among local officials with regard to the great green experiment.
District prosecutors are a "cultural barrier," says Sean McAllister, a lawyer from Denver, who represents defendants in junction for marijuana offences and acts locally as a spokesperson for the nation for the national marijuana law reform organization.
"They've spent such a large part of their lives prosecuting people that they still can't accept legalization.
As the first states to treat smaller amounts of marijuana such as alcohol, Colorado and Washington are traded as national model cases for the legalization of drugs.
While advocates and state officials are preparing for an age of legal sales, they are also nervously awaiting instructions from the federal government, which intends to continue to treat the sale and cultivation of marijuana at the federal level as a criminal offence.
Advocates of the legalization movement hope that the Justice Department will yield.
Despite several sensational arrests of consumers and sellers of medical marijuana, the federal government has largely allowed the sale of medical marijuana in Colorado, Washington and 16 other states.
Kevin A. Sabet, former drug policy adviser to the Obama administration, believes that while criminal investigators are unlikely to kick doors to seize smaller amounts of the drugs, they are presumable to condone legal, state-regulated marijuana deals under the new legislation.
Several cities in Colorado aren’t even waiting for federal authorities to act.
Even before Election Day, some local municipal administrations approved moratoriums on new marijuana stores, although these could only open in about a year.
Last week Montrose, a city in the West, passed a six-month ban that presumably will be passed next week.
"We don't want to get into a position where we're licensing someone and then facing a big problem at the federal level," according to Bob Nicholson.
Our community voted against this change in the law.
We're guided by what the community has chosen and not what the state has chosen.
There are many questions still open.
Petronella Wyatt: I was bullied from Oxford because I'm Tory
not only university students of today are attacked for their views
It had always been my biggest dream to get a seat at Oxford University.
Both my father and older brother had attended the university that I once considered to be the world's greatest stamp of knowledge, a modern Greek symposium that fostered the great pillars of civilization - free thinking and tolerance.
However, within two weeks of moving into Worscester College at the end of the eighties - where I had planned to study history - I had packed my suitcases again and thus brought about the first scandal of my life.
My father collapsed in tears.
My friends were confused.
In the Evening Standard it was said that I had left because I would have had a problem that fellow students were having sex in the neighbouring room.
Writer A N Wilson mischievously explained that I had left university because I was forced to drink from damaged cups.
But the truth was less amusing.
I ran away.
Yes, ran, because I suffered from systematic bullying and threats.
Not because of my rather unusual name, or because I had come from a private school.
I had only been harassed for one reason - a reason that seemed all the more narrow-minded and gruesome in this alleged cradle of enlightenment: My now deceased father Woodrow Wyatt had been an important adviser to Margaret Thatcher and I proponent of the Conservative Party.
Why do I mention this right now?
Well, according to current reports, a new generation of students of moderate rights are suffering from similar persecution.
The organized and rising hatred of Tory students at Oxford, meanwhile, has developed such dimensions that a group of them last week demanded equality protection that benefits gays, disabled people and ethnic minorities.
Conservative members of Corpus Christi College's Junior Common Room (JCR) say they are "often actively isolated, personalized and declared undesirable because of their political views."
They are calling for a place on their college's equality committee to ensure that their opinions can be expressed freely.
Their situation was aggravated by a recently aired documentary about political students on BBC Two, "Wonderland: Young, Bright and on the Right," which Tories portrayed as an eccentric and neo-Nazis.
Among other things, graduate Joe Cooke, former president of Oxford University's Conservative student association, who was driving in a Rolls-Royce and flaunted with a silver-coloured suit and silver-studded walking stick.
At other universities, conservative students say they are considered "scapegoats for introducing higher tuitions."
20-year-old Luke Black, vice-president of Nottingham's Conservative Student Association, said in a Sunday newspaper: "There is a growing left-wing tendency at universities.
People think we're like the Bullingdon Club without even getting to know each other."
Samuel Roberts, 21 and history student in Corpus Christi, who initiated the request for greater protection, believes such a climate is "unpleasable." 19-year-old Stephanie Cherill, president-designate of Oxford's Conservative student association, says she has observed a growing rejection among JCR members towards students of moderate rights.
"This poses a danger to the intellectual discourse and to members' well-being," she says.
In my first few weeks at Oxford, I belonged to such a minority.
As an incredibly shy 18-year-old I had arrived in September 1986.
Hatred of the Conservative Party had peaked.
In the previous year, the university had voted not to give Margaret Thatcher - a former student - an honorary title due to cuts in higher education.
The atmosphere would have frightened Stalinists.
In the first days of the first semester week, during which new students get to know each other and the tutors, I received a taste of the future events.
I had the experience that the tutors not only tolerated the mockery of Tory students, but even participated with joy.
Political issues such as the miners' strike, privatization and the government's rejection of sanctions against South Africa's apartheid regime found their way into the wood-panelled rooms of the tutorial.
At my first of these events, I was supposed to translate French texts from the 18th-century into English. I hadn't been prepared for what was to come.
"Miss Wyatt," said tutor Harry Pitt (now deceased), "please translate the first paragraph."
I stumbled at it.
Pitt was a little man with a face as if made of cake dough, and quite hateful.
"Do Thatcher supporters refuse to learn French, or are they just stupid?" he asked me.
The other students giggled.
In my eyes, tears arose.
"I suggest that they take a few French lessons in your free time - so if you'ren't too busy with your social activities," Pitt growled at me.
I returned to my room with my head hanging.
That evening, I was sitting in college alone at dinner when someone tapped me lightly on the shoulder.
It was a second-year English student named James who introduced himself as a member of the Conservative student association.
"I know who you are," he said kindly.
Unfortunately, it's what it's like.
Anyone who's suspected of being Tory gets harassed.
It's already bad enough for me, but they know your father, Margaret Thatcher, that's why it's going to be worse for you.
Most Tory freshmen pretend to be supporters of the Labour Party.
At a local pub, I later tried to pretend.
I insisted that I didn't agree on all points with Mrs Thatcher.
This trick should prove unsuccessful.
A beginner in philosophy, politics and economics, who, ironically, had attended Eton College, considered conservative, said to me: "You are the daughter of a fascist pig".
You're polluted.
Other students shared his opinion.
I've been perverse and dirty.
"How do Tories have sex?" one asked me.
They're beating each other, aren't they
I felt the way homosexuals must have felt in front of the more liberal legislation of the United States.
Would I ever be able to leave a normal life at Oxford University?
Wouldn't I be able to meet like-minded people until after dark?
Should I have to turn to the Labour Party and suppress my natural inclinations?
The three years that lay before me stretched before my spiritual eye into a purgatory of ostracization and isolation.
The only tutor who openly revealed himself as Tory was Norman Stone, professor of contemporary history who was based in my college.
He was hated not only for being a Conservative, but also because he had advised Thatcher on foreign policy and wrote speeches for them.
He's rarely been there.
He hated the place, found it provincial and petty and rejected it for his Marxist-determinist historical perspective.
In 1997 he accepted a professorship at the University of Bilkent in Ankara, Turkey.
"You won't be happy here," he told me.
I began commuting between Oxford and my parents' house and found refuge among more open-minded friends and family members in the city.
I told my father that I hated Oxford and why I did it.
He seemed disbelieving.
During his time in the forties, all political views were accepted.
"But it's the best place in the world," he said.
They'd never do something like that, not in my city of dreaming spires.
Even my communist friends always displayed impeccable manners.
His wet eyes clouded.
Give it a chance.
I'm sure they're just teasing you.
It'd break my heart if you left.
Exhausted by my frequent travels to London, my emotional resistance weakened.
One of my male friends, also Tory supporters, had yielded to the pressure and renounced his convictions.
During a tutory the following week in which another history tutor had seriously claimed that I was "an enemy of the people," I decided to do the same.
Although I secretly melted with shame, I admitted that my parents had subjected me to a "brainwashment," calling them "old jerks."
The relief lasted only briefly.
It was my father who slapped the last nail into the coffin of my Oxford career.
At that time, he wrote two columns a week in the Murdoch press.
My door was locked.
I hided in my room, and after five minutes, my bunnies gave up.
When they left, I packed a suitcase and took the first train to London.
I've never returned.
You're welcome to call me a whiny lippy tail.
But no 18-year-old should ever suffer from such intimidation and viciousness in an educational institution.
And even more tragically, it was Oxford - a university that not only spawned 14 Tory prime ministers, but to this day hides behind an undeserved reputation of equality and freedom of thought.
"Valentino prefers elegance to celebrity"
On the occasion of the exhibition "Valentino - masters of couture", which opened this week in London, ABC speaks with Naty Abascal, Fiona Thyssen-Bornemisza and other famous customers of the Italian fashion maker.
Somerset House, the former residence of Isabel I of England, is the only place in the British capital worthy of hosting an exhibition about Valentino Garavani.
This is acknowledged by the designer at the opening of "Valentino - Master of Couture," a grandiose retrospective for which more than 130 dresses of haute couture have been put together, created by its "maison" over the past 50 years.
"I love this palace," he says with his distinctive Italian accent.
This exhibition is the conclusion of a story whose only protagonist is "il signore" Garavani, which could not have been written without his distinguished clientele.
Valentino has always felt fascination for the stuffy and distant world of the nobility.
In the first hall of this exhibition, which will still be on display until 3 March, there are a variety of private letters and photos with the signature of the finest aristocracy, starting from Princess Salimah Aga Khan, to the Princess Marie-Chantal of Greece and Margarita of England to Lord Snowdon.
Valentino depicts these personal memories as trophies of his social ascension from modest fashion maker in the town of Voghera in northern Italy to the idol of the international jet set.
Being in love with the nobility doesn't have to be detrimental.
"At least they don't kick out their butts on your magnificent carpet like some beautiful celebrities from pop music do," says Baroness Fiona Thyssen-Bornemisza.
In the 60s and 70s the two lived in the Alps and were good friends.
Valentino is a spectacular, generous and elegant host.
"We were all happy when we were invited to his house in Gstaad," explains the ex-wife of "Heini" Thyssen, close friends with forgotten beauties such as Marella Agnelli or Eugenie Niarchos.
Valentino has always preferred the elegance of celebrity.
And yet he's a star.
Valeria Mazza, in a dress by Valentino
The Argentinian model Valeria Mazza also doesn't forget the charisma of the fashion maker.
Many years agove after a fashion show at Piazza di Spagna in Rome, we went to his home for dinner.
We were about 20 people, including Sharon Stone and John Kennedy Jr.
At every detail of his apartment, the decoration, the food and the music one recognized and felt his influence.
"All those invited felt important and loved," recalls the "top model," which began her career at the week of haute couture in 1995 in Paris.
"He never goes out of fashion, because his designs are works of art," she concludes.
Fashion show of origin
Garavani's life is not a story of obsessiency, rather of mutual loves.
He likes people with good parenting and the highest titles, and they love him.
One of the galleries in Somerset House has been transformed into a glorious and 60-metre-long catwalk that proposes a roll swap: the user occupies and walks along the catwalk to admire a dreamlike "audience," wearing masterpieces by Valentino, such as the dress Jackie Kennedy chose for her wedding to Aristóteles Onassis, the costumes that Monica Vitti wore.
Names such as that of Sibilla de Luxemburgo, Gloria von Thurn und Taxis, Mette-Marit of Norway, Rosario of Bulgaria or Sofía of Habsburg radiate in this audience of fashion puppets.
Naty Abascal and the fashion maker, 2006
Many of these customers say the first Valentino is like first love, "unforgettable."
I can still remember perfectly.
It was pants, a shirt, a vest type "Gillette" and a jacket from the autumn/winter 1971-1972 collection.
"It's been a gift from him," says Naty Abascal, one of the designer's muses.
"I prefer him to everyone else because of his great love for the women and because he emphasizes our beauty," the ex-Duchess of Feria adds.
I really like the colors he uses; they have a lot of light and underline the face.
Proportions are perfect.
The princess and opinion leader at fashion Patricia della Giovampaola d'Arenberg can't forget for the first time she wore a Valentino.
When I lived in Italy as a teenager, I dreamed of finally having the age and opportunity to wear one of his evening dresses...
At the end of the 1990s it finally happened.
I bought my first Valentino dress for a feast in my cousin's castle, Prince Edouard de Ligne.
It was a red dress with a flying skirt and a draped top with a cleavage that lives up to its name.
"It was a dream come true," says Rodrigo d'Arenberg's widow.
"Valentino doesn't depend on fashion, its obsession is timeless," explains this Italian aristocrat, who lives between Parí's, New York and Buenos Aires.
Princess D'Arenberg keeps her evening dresses "with absolute care" "because a dress is not only a dress but also the memories associated with it."
The "King" of Fashion
Marie-Chantal Miller's wedding dress for her wedding to Pablo de Grecia in 1995.
Four months of work and 25 "ragazze" (as the fashion maker called his seamstresses) were necessary to make this ivory-colored silk dress with pearls, twelve different embroidery and a four-and-a-half-meter-long tail.
According to journalist Suzy Menkes, the top authority of the trade press, this dress was a milestone in haute couture at the end of the 20th century, "the return to the top-of-the-level customers."
Married for years with the "savoir-être" of the elite, Valentino is now her best specimen.
Cavaliere di Gran Croce (the highest award in Italy), Cavaliere del Lavoro, Commandeur de L'Ordre des Arts et des Lettres, awarded the Legion of Honour, cumulates Garavani as many honors as any of the husbands of his customers.
"I've always noticed his refinement, his tranquility, his well-groomed and perfect appearance," D'Arenberg admits.
I last saw him at a gala dinner at the Museum de Orsay a month ago.
He sat at the table of Countess Jacqueline de Ribes, a very good friend of mine.
"He was spotless; time leaves no traces on him."
And that's what a princess says...
The world's most difficult profession: Kawah Ijen's human working animals
For four euros, the bearers of the Indonesian volcano put their lives and health at risk when carrying 70 kilos of sulphur over the steep, rocky paths.
There are people for whom their work is hell, and others work literally in hell.
This is the case with Anto Wijaya, one of the 400 miners who earn their lives by recovering sulphur from Kawah Ijen volcano in the east of the Indonesian island Java.
To do this, he has to descend to the bottom of his crater every day, where the sulphur-containing gas flowing out of the earth solidifies in contact with the air.
After he breaks out large sulphur stones, which can weigh up to 70 kilos in total, he transports them in two bamboo baskets on his shoulders over the sloping paths of stone.
While it's only 250 meters to the top of the volcano that rises to a height of 2386 meters, the exhausted carriers take more than 40 minutes to ascend at the tortoise pace, having to maintain balance and carefully set their steps so that they don't slip and crash.
They know that every step can cost their lives, as happened to a French tourist who crashed years ago on the steep slopes of Kawah Ijen.
Kawah Ijen's miners earn €5 for every kilo of sulphur they mine.
Arriving at the top, they make their way between the tourists, whom they photograph like circus monkeys and walk with the tirifyingly heavy baskets three kilometers to the scale, which a mining company has set up a little further down at an altitude of 1850 meters
The societ is PT Candi Ngrimbi, which has been exploiting the deposits in the volcano and (never better said) since 1960, to whom it pays 662 Indonesian rupees (5 Eurocents) for every kilo of sulphur.
She then sells this to the petrochemical industry for every 10,000 rupees (83 euro cents), because this mineral is widely used in everyday life and is used to produce matches, fireworks, cosmetics, dynamite and even for bleaching sugar.
"Since we normally carry 70 kg, we receive about 46,000 rupees (3.8 euros) for each route," Anto explains to us, who usually does three routes a day.
For each track he needs three hours, after which he is completely done, but at the end of the day he has 138,000 rupees (11.5 euros) together.
What looks like a miserable reward for such an almost inhumanous effort, however, is threefold of what he would earn with fieldwork.
"The daily wage of the miners here, where 15,000 rupees (1.2 euros) per day are paid during the coffee harvest and the average monthly income is two million rupees (167 euros), is very high," explains the carrier, who previously worked as a bricklayer on the tourist island of Bali.
There he earned 75,000 rupees (6.2 euros) a day, and the work wasn't that hard, but Anto has returned with his family to Banyuwangi, a village nearby, for a reason that's as important as the sulphur in Indonesia: "I've married a girl from Bali, where they're Hindus, and I've taken them to Java to convert them to Islam."
Anto has asthma, breathes with difficulty, coughs frequently and his eyes are irritated because of the toxic gases.
27-year-old Anto has been jeopardising his life at Kawah Ijen for three years, whose sulphur has already left his first traces behind him, despite covering his face with a mask and special glasses.
Anto has asthma, breathes with difficulty, coughs frequently and its eyes are irritated because of the poisonous gases from the volcano.
This is the price he has to pay for reaching his dream.
"I'm going to work here for another two years because I want to open a shop or learn Spanish or French," he promises in a more than acceptable English.
This sympathetic and intelligent, but punished by life young man could work as a tourist guide, waiter or at the reception of a hotel, but instead he does the work of a carrier.
He shares a dirty wooden hut with other wearers and stands up at two o'clock every morning, because the sulphur also pours out at night, where its characteristic yellow color shines blurily in the darkness.
The shadows challenging, he climbs into the crater in the light of a small headlamp on his helmet, which he bought from his own money.
Approximately 400 straps load the baskets with sulphur onto their shoulders on the crater floor.
Despite its large profits, the mining company has not mechanized sulphur mining in order to save costs, and does not provide any equipment to the carriers, who work on their own account and are paid by weight.
They also receive no share of the 30,000 rupees (2.5 euros) surcharge per camera that the guardians of this natural park, together with the entrance fee of 15,000 rupees (1.2 euros), demand from the tourists who come to photograph the volcano and the human carrier.
"This work is for the animals and not for humans," protests Madrusin, a stocky, 42-year-old carrier who has been working at Kawah Ijen for three decades since leaving school.
He is able to carry up to 110 kg and assures he will continue "as long as possible" because he needs the money to educate his three children between the ages of 10 and 18.
I won't retire, I'm going to die here because the volcano has been my whole life.
Even if the sulphur burns the throat and the eyes itch, when the wind suddenly turns and pinches the miners in the thick columns rising from the crater, the men are so hard that no one complains about serious illnesses ... of course except for the usual breathing problems, osteoarthritis, pain in the knees and blisters on the shoulders that have deformed from the weight of the baskets.
With the basket on his back, Unainik is only able to carry 50 kg at the age of 53.
Every day, he and his companions erupt 15 tons of sulphur from the volcano, which are transported by three trucks via a goat's path between the shrubbery to the camp in Tamansari at 18 kilometres away.
"I'm not going to retire, I'm going to die here because the volcano has been my whole life," Unainik explains, opening his mouth wide, in which some teeth are missing.
Of his five children, the eldest at the age of 30 also works as sulphur carriers.
Time passes, but poverty persists generation after generation in one of the toughest professions in the world: that of those who work as a human duty at Kawah Ijen.
Singapore Need Babies to Save the Economy
The Singaporeans place the blame for having no children, on the career, the stress and the cost of the real estate and the children's education.
“The population of Singapore needs to grow.”
I am a patriotic husband, you are my patriotic wife: let us fulfill our civic duty and create life!
It seems incredible that the verses are part of an advertisement for minzbons, but still - or precisely because of it - they became a viral video on YouTube in Singapore earlier this year.
The sentences are part of a rapper song thating local sayings such as "pushing a "bao" (loaf) into the oven to make fun of Singapore's low birth rate.
The advertising company BBH, which shot the video, maintains the hope that the commercial achieves in a funny way to draw attention to the problem.
Their creative director Douglas Hamilton says he wanted to use the power of music to get people to fulfill their "national duty."
This is something just for the internet, that’s why we had to make it funny.
This is the biggest problem in this country.
When it comes to procreating, we are the worst in the world, so we realized that it was an issue we had to take care of.
We knew that the government had tried a lot, such as marketing perfume with pheromones or the organization of speed-dating evenings (quick flirts).
Possible that many of these ideas were creative, but they did not necessarily lead to success.
Therefore, we thought: Why don't we do something as creative as possible to tackle this problem, such as composing rap?
1.2 children
Those who don't take this issue lightly are the Singapore government.
Every year, she spends $1,300 on policies to encourage citizens to have more children.
A government package for married couples and parents grants up to $15,000 per child, extends maternity leave and distributes tax benefits.
But everything has had little success so far.
Singapore is a rich, high-tech city-state in Southeast Asia, also known for the conservatism of its rulers and its strict social controls.
According to its national population authority, the birth rate is currently 1.2 children per woman.
The last time that rate was above 2 - which is also known as subsidization rate - was in 1976.
So why don't Singaporeans have more children?
Tan Wei Ming, director of marriage and family policy at the national population authority, says that this was due to "better education" and "greater career opportunities."
“This has allowed people to have substantially more options in terms of life goals and prioritizing besides getting married and forming a family,” she explains.
These changes in social circumstances have contributed to the higher number of single persons and the delay of marriage and births, the result of which is the reduction of the birth rate in Singapore.
Meanwhile, an immigration policy aimed at a drastic increase in immigrations to counteract the reduction of the population led to resentment among the local population.
In Singapore there are websites where barely concealed xenophobia is flourishing against many new immigrants, especially against the Chinese, who are criticized for keeping wages low and not integrating.
The rise in immigration is also seen as one of one of one of the reasons the ruling party in Singapore last year achieved its worst election result since independence.
Since the elections, an attempt has been made to correct the problem with higher contributions and levies for foreign workers.
Unexpected Consequences
While the falling birth rate has the well-known impact on a nation's economic growth, tax revenues, health care costs and immigration policies, the example of Singapore also shows unexpected consequences.
The government is trying to ensure that not so many small apartments are built.
Thus, it is already having an impact on the housing market.
The Urban Development Authority has begun to control the number of small apartments known as "shoe boxes" that are allowed to be built in certain areas of the city.
These apartments have a living area of 46 square meters and show a great sales success.
However, there is a concern that they can promote the lifestyle of the single person, and that they deter the construction companies from building large housing for families.
But Lim Yew Soon, director-general of real estate firm EL Developers, says his "shoeboxes" can be sold much faster than larger housing units.
They are so popular, and the units are sold out by days and even weeks faster than the larger housing units.
As a result, they are very much better for our cash flow.
However, he admits that the new standards give developers clearer orientations, which were previously placed in the way if they wanted to build too many small residential units within a project.
Too much stress
Singapore is a city state.
Even if these new rules could pave the way towards an increase in the national birth rate, it is clear from the talks with the Singaporeans working in the financial district that they won't have a major impact.
"People are severely stressed, the apartments and also education are expensive, which is why it's pushed out by many," says one young manager.
The kids can get others.
"It's important for me to dispose of my own money and time," says another, about twenty-year-old-year-old.
Men and women cite their careers, the stress and the cost of property and parenting as reasons that they can't have children.
That's why Singaporeans have the final word when it comes to having children - the government can still do so much.
The offline private offline is private online
Privacy
According to the dictionary, this is the property private or "that area of private life, where one is entitled to protection against any kind of interference".
What's private for someone under the age of 16?
How is this definition applied to his everyday life and social networks?
Does he understand the dangers he exposes himself to when he publicizes situations on the Internet that he wouldn’t share in real life?
El Periódico interviewed five youngsters aged between 10 and 15, who are frequently on the net.
In four of the cases, they linked the term to "something very own" on a personal level and "to the password and the user" when they applied it to the social networks.
"I wouldn't disclose my deepest secrets in a post," says 10-year-old Jorge while tryingly explaining the importance of "private" in websites such as Facebook, Twitter, Hotmail and Windows Live Messenger, where he has had accounts for two years.
"These are very confidential secrets, my mother is allowed to know them, but not the whole world," he confirms.
On FB I upload beautiful pictures or pictures of games.
I'm passing my time with acquaintances too.
"I wouldn't share a photo that's not of me, or of someone who's taking the fool," he says.
The boy is aware that it is wrong to post obscene photos, of nudes, of crimes, or to write offensive or forcible comments.
Jorge assures to know 35 friends he has in FB and nine followers on Twitter.
Most of them are relatives.
Including the mother, who has the password to one of the accounts.
I signed up to Twitter to express myself and write interesting tweets.
"I don't know if anyone answers me, I'm just uploading them," he adds.
"The social networks are entertaining, I can quickly talk to relatives in the distance or with friends," he states.
Asked, he replies that he would never accept an invitation from an unknown person.
Even if someone recommended him a stranger, he would ignore it.
The 14-year-old José's case is different.
The teenager has user accounts in Hotmail, Facebook, My Space and Ask. In Ask, he admits not to know 20 of the people he added to his friends list.
"That doesn't scare me because we have something in common, for example music," he states.
According to the boy, no one hinted or asked him for his address or phone number.
"If someone afflicts me or asks them of me, I'll just remove them from my user account," he admits.
José reached out to Ask after reading a referral on Twitter.
The boy is not unknown to experiences that are now known as cyberbullying.
The acquaintance of a girlfriend was blackmailed on a social network.
They threatened him and demanded money.
"He's never known who it was," he assures.
According to José, the victim did not close the user account.
“He only did it privately.”
After that, he explains a series of steps to configure a user account securely.
In contrast to Jorge, this boy would upload photos of acquaintances in uncomfortable or embarrassing situations.
Yes, I'd do if he's unsympathetic to me or I want to wipe one out of him.
"But I know this is cyberbullying," he explains.
Key questions
Marielos Porras, teacher for English with a university degree in education and learning, thinks that in ordering to orient the children and young people, it must be understood that it is the purpose of the social networks.
"The Internet arose as a medium for searching for information; however, with these pages, the rules of the game have changed," she says.
Porras explains that the academist Marc Prensky, holder of a master's title in pedagogy at the University of Yale and author of the work "Digital Natives and Immigrants", shaped these terms to explain the phenomenon.
The digital natives are them, the children and adolescents who grew up with this technology.
"We are the digital immigrants they are supposed to teach when we still learn," he says.
He explains that it is a complex issue "because we require them to have a defined criterion about what may be disseminated, published or said at an age where maturity is insufficient."
"They're also supposed to be picky when it's most important to them to be popular to have thousands of friends without thinking about the consequences," he adds.
According to the expert, the most effective way to teach the children and adolescents is what privacy is, asking them questions that makes them think.
"Telling them don't don't do it doesn't work," he says.
Porras then mentions a few possibilities: there are things you wouldn’t tell a stranger. Then why are you doing it on the Internet?
Or would you like being posted a photo of you, like the one you published by a friend?
Do you know what the others publish about you?
Did you ask the other people for permission beforehand if you've labeled photos of celebrations?
And another question: Do everyone need to know what you're doing at every moment?
Another point is to make them understand that they need to trade online as they do offline.
They're the same rules.
"Someone outside the internet acts with respect, morality and other principles, and the same must apply to social networks," he explains.
Surveillance
Estuardo Guardia, university professor, elementary school teacher and educational consultant, comments that it is essential for parents to carefully read the guidelines of the social networks.
When they understand all sections, they have solid foundations to talk to their children about the importance of opening a user account on the internet.
"For example, the age, or what you are allowed to share or publish," he explains.
According to Guardia, the children need to be reminded of the lesson "Thou shalt not speak to strangers."
Completion of the Unasur Summit without publication of the Lima Declaration
The 6th Summit of Presidents of the Union of South American Nations (Unasur) ended today in Peru without the publication of the Lima Declaration, which was previously announced and should theoretically be signed by the seven rulers present.
Efe repeatedly tried to gain access to the signed document at the Fourth Ordinary Session of the Heads of State and Government of Unasur. Sources at the State and Presidential Chancellery initially announced that they would hand it over after the Examination, but subsequently declared that it would be published on the Peruvian government's website at some time.
For questions about the text, they stated that the content was outlined by the President of Peru, Ollanta Humala, during brief statements to the press.
Journalists' access to information about the summit was limited at any time.
During the day, only the picture of the presidential meeting without sound was transmitted in the press room with the message "private session, limited audio".
The little information circulating among the journalists was received by the press officers of some of the Unasur governments present at the meeting, but not by the Peruvian press secretary.
The only document circulated during the day was the list of participating presidents, causing unequaciation among the hundreds of journalists from various national and international medias who called for more information.
After that, the presidential office of Peru sent an e-mail to the media with the "financial declaration" of the summit meeting, but it was Humala's declaration and not the official document at the conclusion of the summit.
Last October, Peru hosted the Third Summit South America - Arab States (ASPA), and on this occasion the so-called Lima statement, which had been announced beforehand, despite a strong request by the press.
On the official ASPA website you can convince yourself that the document was published last Tuesday.
At both international meetings, the Peruvian authorities emphasized that the information systems were in place for journalists, but they limited access to information to a great extent.
The summit also ended with the joint commitment of Chile and Peru to adopt a ruling by the Court of Justice of The Hague that resolves a border conflict between the two countries.
The presidents of Peru, Ollanta Humala, and Chile, Sebastián Piñera, met during the regional meeting and confirmed that they would accept the decision of the IGH, which begins in The Hague on Monday, in the process that Lima initiated against Santiago to listen to the pleas of both sides.
"We will follow and execute the verdict on the differences that we are currently presenting to this international court," Humala said, along with his Chilean counterpart.
"Chile was, is and will continue to be a country that adheres to international laws, seeks peaceful solutions for disputes, concludes agreements and submits to the international courts," Piñera adds, greeting Humala with a handshake in front of the flags of both countries.
The confirmation of both presidents that they would submit to the ICJ came after Colombia, following a decision on the maritime borders with Nicaragua, which it considered a grave mistake, brought an action against the Bogotá agreement, in which it agreed to submit to the rulings of this international court.
Present at the summit were the presidents of Brazil, Dilma Rouseff, Venezuela, Hugo Chávez, of Bolivia, Evo Morales, and Argentina, Cristina Kirchner.
Paraguay, which has been barred from Unasur since 2011 following the deposition of its President Fernando Lugo, did not take part in the meeting.
Host President Ollanta Humala opened the talks in the morning and ended the summit shortly after noon in Lima.
The head of state read the final document, in which it announced that 16 resolutions were taken and the lines of action for 31 projects between the South American countries amounted to a total investment of $17 billion.
On the decisions made, he said that the countries of Unasur "will take significant steps towards the goal of South American citizenship, for which we extend the decisions on residence rights."
He said measures would be taken to "improve cooperation in the fight against insecurity in cities and against organised, cross-border crime, as well as measures to improve access to medicines and the Internet at low prices in all areas of South America and to act jointly and efficiently in the event of natural disasters."
"With the crisis in Europe, "economic consolidation (in Latin America) must not take on triumphant traits, but is intended to expand our production performance and show a better future to our peoples," Humala added.
"We have decided to prioritize a number of 31 emblematic projects designed to improve spatial connections in South America and especially in rural and border areas... and thus unite our countries and open up new economic forms," the Peruvian president said in a ready statement.
Among these projects would also be five Peruvian, which would be located on the transverse axes of its territory from the coast to Brazil, and of which two are supposed to ensure a better connection with Ecuador, although he gave no further details.
Likewise, the final document referred to the political situation in Paraguay.
"We hope that the electoral process in this country serves for its resumption in the Union of South American Nations," from which it is currently excluded.
The necess for Latin America to remain an integrated region for prosperity and peace and with good neighbourly relations was another aspect that was emphasized at the summit.
With this in mind, the president of Colombia, Juan Manuel Santos, said before the start of his participation in the regional meeting that he was expecting a meeting in Mexico on Saturday with his counterpart Daniel Ortega in order to treat the maritime conflict respectfully following an ICJ ruling challenged by Bogotá.
"Maybe I'm going to meet tomorrow (Saturday) with President Daniel Ortega," Santos said.
"We will review all these avenues - they are not exclusive, and the agreement with Nicaragua also makes talks with Nicaragua necessary," he underlined.
"I hope to be able to tell President Ortega that we will deal with this matter as civilizedly and respectfully as possible," Santos added.
Santos and Ortega would meet in Mexico on Saturday, where they intend to be present at the takeover of the country's new president, Enrique Peña Nieto.
As part of the summit, the bloc's defence ministers also met in the run-up to adopt the action plan for 2013 to strengthen the dialogue and consensus on defending the region.
Argentina, Bolivia, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, Brazil, Uruguay, Venezuela, Chile, Guyana, Surinam and Paraguay are members at UNASUR, even if the latter country is currently excluded.
Peru has the chairmanship of the regional bloc on time.
"South America must learn in the integration of citizens," says Rafael Correa
Ecuador's president, Rafael Correa, confirmed today that creating a common citizenship is a goal, with "South America actually being able to learn something from Europe."
Correa, who attendeed the 6th summit of the presidents of the Union of South American Nations (Unasur), which took place in Lima, explained to state television that Europeans "killed each other in World War II and in other clashes and "are practically a fatherland today."
With this in mind, he defended the project of creating a South American citizenship that advances the member states of Unasur.
"The free movement of citizens and workers in all South American countries must be achieved, as is already the case in the Andean community; however, there are still reactionary sectors seeking a return to the past," he warned.
The President of Ecuador also endorsed the reshaping of the Organization of American States (OEAs) under the stipulation of diminishing the influence of Anglo-Saxon States and taking into account those who have signed the San José Convention on Human Rights.
Those who teach never commit themselves to anything, and in returnation, we the South Americans always sign everything.
"It is incomprehensible that the Inter-American Commission on Human Rights is based in Washington and is funded by the United States," he declared in reference to the political asylum that Ecuador has granted the founder of WikiLeaks, Julian Assange.
Correa assured that he did not regret this decision because he did not betray his principles, but fulfilled his "deep democratic values and views on human rights".
He added that that at the time there had been "founded assumptions that Assange would be extradited to a third country and that his proceedings would not comply."
He also criticized the Swedish judiciary, which had demanded that he be questioned on his territory for an alleged sexual offence, while "Swedish jurisprudency allows this by means of a video conference that could have been carried out via the embassy of Ecuador in London."
Correa explained that there was a risk that Assange's health and mental state would be compromised.
"Since he found himself in our embassy, I haven't spoken to him, but the ambassador has informed me that he has a slight problem with the lungs, but not a serious one," Ecuador's head of state said.
However, there is a risk that he would suffer physical and psychic damage because he was trapped in a confined space and could not perform physical exercises outdoors.
"That would affect everyone's health," he added.
Correa stated that that the solution to the asylum granted to Assange since the month of June at Ecuador's embassy in London was the granting of a leisure with which he could travel to Ecuador and that this would be in the hands of Britain, Swede and Europe's legal authorities. He underlined that there had been talks with London to find a solution for the imprisoned founder of WikiLeaks.
We do not deal with human rights, we do not use this word for this case, but there have been constant discussions.
"The solution to this problem lies in the hands of Britain, Sweden and Europe's legal authorities because Assange's lawyer, Baltazar Garzón, conducts a number of proceedings at various European authorities," he explained.
And "when Britain grants this charter tomorrow, in my opinion, this is done."
And if Sweden interrogated Mr Assange at Ecuador's embassy in London or via Skype, as allowed by Swedish jurisprudence and done in other cases, the problem was settled.
Correa took the opportunity to confirm as a defender of press freedom and said that what he did not tolerate is "the mediocrity, the evil intention and the lie that invalidates freedom of expression."
"The greatest enemies of press freedom are not the evil and perverse politicians, but bad journalists who strive and blackmail for profit," he said.
With this in mind, he congratulated himself that Ecuador would no longer be dominated by such journalists "nor by the bankers or hegemonic and skewer-bourgeois countries," and anticipated that in a re-election he "will deepen the revolution to continue on the path in this right direction."
Correa also supported the decision to maintain the veto against Paraguay in Unasur at least until his next elections, arguing that the organism must "remain to remain tough and must not allow any opportunism on and no coup d'état disguised as a legally disguised," becausing "the legitimacy of democracy in Paraguay."
The president of Ecuador also considers the wish of his Colombian counterpart, Juan Manuel Santos, now and after the ruling of the International Court of Justice of The Hague, which favored the maritime sovereignty of Nicaragua to negotiate with Nicaragua the maritime borders between the two countries, "absolutely justified."
At present, this judgment will not be implemented.
It is a problem between a South and a Central American state.
Confliences are unavoidable, but they must be overcome in the pursuit of a common path.
They need to be treated comprehensively so that you can overcome them and look forward.
He also relied on a good conclusion of the disputes over maritime borders between Peru and Chile before the same Court of Justice and said that "it is right for Latin America to invoke international instances if both countries undertake to accept the judgment, even if it is very harsh."
Referring to the possibility that he faces himself as a candidate for the next presidential election in Ecuador in order to achieve his third mandate in a row, he stated that he sees this possibility "with much optimism and joy, although it is also very difficult."
Correa assured that if he lost the elections in February 2013, he would retire from political life.
Make never interested me personally, but under such unjustable circumstances as in Ecuador this socio-economic poverty can only be corrected by political power.
"My political movement had the belief that it was me who guaranteed this likely victory, that's why I have to meet that responsibility," he said.
If I were to win, it would be my last period as president, after which I withdraw from public life.
This also applies if we lose.
"That's decided," he explained.
Correa also referred to the treatment of the health problems of the President of Venezuela, Hugo Chávez.
I've just spoken to Venezuela's vice president, Nicolás Maduro. He told me that he had traveled there for a planned, routine treatment, and that he would be expected to be able to travel back to Cuba after the election campaign.
"It in no case does not mean a relapse in President Chávez's state of health," he commented.
The Ecuadorian leader took part today in Lima at the 6th Summit of Leaders of the Union of South American Nations (Unasur), which ended with the call for higher regional integration to ensure sustainable progress, equality and security.
Cause of dying because of AIDS today is too late detection
Fabrizio was 21 years old when he was confirmed the result of the investigation: HIV-positive.
"It was as if a bomb struck," he says of the moment he was given the diagnosis, which the doctor "tried to mitigate without discernible success."
He hid it from his family.
He decided to take care of his illness alone and began to find out about it; and with such commitment that he was just celebrating his 43rd birthday.
He is undoubtedly one of the oldest patients of the Hospital Civil de Guadalajara (HCG), in which he came after several fights in 1994.
Fabrizio has been living with the human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) for 22 years, which was difficult to imaginable at the beginning of the 90s, when there were many doubts, few treatment options and more stigmats.
At the time, even the director of an IMSS clinic avoided saying goodbye "because it led to an impasse."
At that time AIDS was synonymous with death.
Today it is possible to survive this syndrome even with quality of life.
However, many people still come to the clinic who know nothing about their disease and in which the virus has already caused damage and has exhausted their immune system, and who are victims of opportunistic infections.
31 years after the appearance of AIDS in the world, i.e. after the appearance of the first reported cases, "the great success today means that the survival of a patient taking up the treatment in time and the survival of the general population are exactly the same," explained Jaime Andrade Villanueva, the head of the HCG's HIV unit, Jaime Andrade Villanueva explaining that this information had been supported in April this year.
Andrade Villanueva, Infectiational Diseases and HIV/AIDS, commented that since 2000, the scientists concluded that AIDS was not a death sentence, while the years of survival and quality of life depended on the degree of impairment of people’s immune system at the beginning of the treatment, and that those would have greater chances that aren’t drug-addicted: up to €300 at a count of 200 CD4.
Put simply, the explanations above mean that those who receive the diagnosis HIV-positive at the age of 25, in this sense "can easily turn 75 years old as long as he is under control," this professional explained.
In order to classify this progress correctly, it must be recalled that the life expectancy of Mexicans today is an average of 76 years.
Even if mortality has decreased significantly in recent years (in Mexico, the number of people dying from AIDS has decreased from 6,678 cases in 2007 to 4,862 in 2011 (annual report of ONUSIDA), it is nevertheless true that 60 percent of patients recorded in the national database have died.
In Jalisco alone, 255 people died in 2011, and there are already 187 deaths by May this year; however, it is assured that there has been universal access to antiretroviral medicines since 2005.
- So why are people still dying from it?
- In my opinion, the problem isn't access to treatment.
This is my opinion and that's what's in our hospital.
At least in the past 12 years, we haven't had any supply problems with the medication. The problem is that the individuals come in far advanced condition because they don't know their state of infection, i.e. in very late phases of the disease.
"Nine out of ten patients come to us if they already have an accompanying infection. In order to be able to influence global mortality, earlier diagnoses are required, which is why massive detection tests must be offered to all persons who require this."
This proposal is agreed by the experts and officials of the Consejo Estatal de Prevención del Sida (Coesida) (State Committee on AIDS Avoidance in Jalisco) and also the patients like Fabrizio, who had his test carried out at a private laboratory, for the only reason that it had made a friend of him who, despite his young age, was already in the AIDS phase.
It's all different if you know you've got AIDS.
Some think they're going to die and don't want to hear anything.
"If I have to die already, it'd rather burst three times," one says in my case.
The change was towards improvement, I eat well, do sports and take the medication.
Today his parents only know that he had cancer.
I'm living a normal life, like everyone else.
"I'm working, doing a lot, have an active but responsible sex life, I'm paying attention to my health and that of the other person," enumerates Fabrizio, who agreed to share his intimate details with MILENIO JALISCO in order to animate with his testimony to those who are scared as part of Worldwide AIDS Day.
They should take the test if they belong to the high-risk group. The sooner they know if they are HIV-positive, the better, and if they already know the diagnosis, they are supposed to learn how you can live responsibly like all people.
His message is a summary of the fight against AIDS in 2012.
Condoms behind the counter
There are big holes between the health programs and ordinary citizens, explains Ricardo Salazar, journalist from Guadalajara, who has embraced the cause of HIV.
And the most important thing is prevention.
In the designated places "the distribution of condoms was actually increased; in the past they were handed over individually, now they give us packages with a hundred pieces, which is already good, but there are still people who do not have access to a condom," he said.
The at-risk persons for new infections include the adolescents.
"What do you need them for?" is a common mocking question from social workers, orientation staff in schools, employees in pharmacies and in the health system, to which young people do not want to expose themselves, the correspondent assured.
He suggested reorienting this inefficient distribution; the condoms are supposed to be located not only behind the counter, but in dispensing devices in public toilets of facilities frequently visited by young people.
It's not supposed to promote promiscuity.
They should not be paid the beer and motel, as the Governor Emilio González replied to the question of whether preservatives would be distributed under his administration.
"Sexuality doesn't look like that; it's best to bring the condoms closer to those who are already engaged in a sexual activity," he said.
Key data from Jalisco
There are 13,435 cumulative cases (12,158 AIDS and 1,317 HIV).
The state ranks 4th nationally at number new and cumulative AIDS cases, and 13th place in terms of HIV.
92% of contagion cases are caused by sexual intercourse, 6% via blood and 2% perinatally.
It is estimated that 50,000 people live with HIV because there are 4 or 5 people for every registered case who don’t.
After the United States appeals court upheld a ruling that does not acknowledge the restructuring of the Vitro group's liabilities reached via a trade bankruptcy in Mexico, an ominous precedent is created for any native company with settlements in the neighboring country that has payment problems.
So it could be said that the procedures for the survival of the companies that allow Mexican laws are not valid in the Land of Stars and Stripes contrary to international agreements.
In practice, the June 15 recognition of the verdict by Judge Harlin Hale at the court in Bancarrotas for the northern district in Texas causes the Mexican companies to be helplessly exposed to seizing their property beyond the Bravo River.
However, the decision opens the door to the largest glass manufacturer in Mexico to appeal to the U.S. Supreme Court because of three inconsistencies.
While the judge of the case points out that creditors must be subject to United States bankruptcy law, the Fifth Circuit's appeals court, based in New Orleans, initially states that the main proceeding is the commercial bankruptcy dealt with in Mexico.
The first point would mean that international cooperation is not known in proceedings in the event of insolvencity of companies with a cross-border profile.
In fact, the United Nations Model Law was created for the unification of international trade law for this purpose, with the American Law Institute being used as an arbitrator.
Secondly, the judgment stipulates that without the votes of the companies following recognition of the liabilities in the bankruptcy mass that the branches of Vitro had with their parent company, the necessary majority would not have been achieved for the restructuring of liabilities.
However, this possibility is recognized by Mexican laws.
In fact, Vitro is not the first case in which this scheme has been approved.
There are half a dozen examples, including Agremex and Comercial Mexicana, whose liabilities have been confirmed among companies of the group by the Instituto Federal de Concursos Mercantiles.
It is also true that after deduction of the votes of the branches, the creditors of Vitro, the creditors who complained against Vitro, i.e. the vulture funds such as Aurelios Capital, Aurelios Convergencia, Elliot Internacional and Liverpool Limited, would not have reached a majority.
The vote would have been 45 percent against 37 percent.
This is not taken into account by the Court of Appeal.
On the other hand, Vitro is to blame for his difficult situation from 2008 in the context of the great economic crisis in the United States, which issued blows to the country.
For this reason, the González Sada family lodges an objection before the same Court of Appeal so that the vote takes place in plenary, i.e. by all the judges, because only three of them voted.
If this is not granted, the cerciorare objection shall be lodged, which requires the review of the proceedings by a higher authority, in this case by the Supreme Court of the United States.
The most serious thing about this case is that the court set aside a document sent by the Government of Mexide under Figura amicus curiae (Friend of the Court) describing the procedure conducted by Vitro under the Commercial Bankruptcy Act, indicating that it had strictly adhered to the agreements signed by both countries to comply with Chapter 15 of the United States Bankruptcy Act.
It should also be noted that that the country has submitted to the principles of the United Nations Commission for international trade, i.e. the established rules for the cases of cross-border insolvency that guarantee equality between debtors and creditors.
Two-band caramboles: a blow for Vitro and for the country.
