At a meeting over the weekend at a forum of the University of the Caribbean, a group of national and foreign environmentalists, academics, entrepreneurs and general members of civil society decided to create a broad front to oppose the opening of China's Dragon Mart in Cancún.
As you know, we are talking about a huge sales and distribution center in Mexico, Central America and the Caribbean with products from the country beyond the wall, complemented by a living area for the employees of 150 companies.
In the run-up, the Canacintra (the national chamber of the manufacturing industry) has reached to unite the governors in the south-east to oppose the monumental facility that partially destroyed protected areas and is the mother of all threats to the industry.
ACTA is dead
Because the government's request to declare under what conditions and circumstances the Mexican ambassador in Japan signed the anti-counterfeiting trade agreement known under the English abbreviation ACTA, the matter has already been archived according to the Mexican Institute of Intellectual Property.
