Héctor "Hetin" Reyes: "Basketball was my life"
The globetrotter of basketball, Héctor "Hetin" Reyes, has been associated with basket sports for more than 60 years and has traveled all over the world.
Few in Puerto Rico possess such a large spiritual collection of the history of the country's basketball as Héctor "Hetin" Reyes.
Before being tied to a wheelchair by a stroke in 2008, Rexes was part of this sport for 6 years, whether as a lower-class player, first national league player, plenipotentiator and leader in the BSN with the Vaqueros de Bayamón or president of the Basketball Federation.
"All my life, I've put on a lot of hats for myself in basketball, even several at the same time, when I was president of BSN, chief executive of the national team and president of the association in the '90s," Reyes recalled visiting Primera Hora at his home in Bayamón, where he resides with Isabel, his loyal wife for more than 50 years.
“Basketball was my life.”
Reyes isn't exaggerating with this statement.
The walls of his house are almost all decorated with photos and memories of his long career and testify to them.
Bayamón in the heart
The most emotional memories are reminiscent of his time at the Vaqueros from the mid-50s as a player until 1982, where he celebrated his 15th anniversary as co-managing director or managing director of the club.
"These were my best years that I enjoyed the most because I've been allowed to be part of eight championships of the Vaqueros since 1967, whether it's been as managing director, co-managing director or coach.
There have been many fulfilling years, including the five consecutive championships from 1971 to 1975.
And then I said goodbye with a championship in 1981, the year of Jerome Mincy's debut at BSN.
From then on, "Cuco" Ortiz, who was a very good steward," Reyes says.
Gene Bartow, who worked here as a coach and was employed at the University of Alabama (Birmingham), told me: "I've got a very hefty and 2 m player.
“Do you want him?”
And that was the beginning of Mincy, one of the best players Puerto Rico ever had.
Bartow then recommended free-throwing specialist Raymond Gausse, who was naturalized and was one of our best throwers.
I still know how he said that if Mincy had allowed a championship for Bayamon, Gausse would make for another.
He was granted the championship of the Vaqueros with Gausse, but already from a distance, because he was already at the top of the association in 1988.
At that time he preferred to enjoy his executions and those of Mincy in the national team.
I remember the first time we won at the pre-Olympic tournament in Mexico against the USA.
After that came the 1990 World Cup, where we finished fourth, which should have been bronze if it hadn't been Canadian referee who let us repeat the last move for the second time, Reyes said.
Was this group of the 1990 World Cup the best national team you've ever seen?
She is one of the best, as well as those who won at the 2004 Olympics against the Dream Team.
However, my favorite is the team of the pan-American games in 1991 in Cuba, where we won gold and offered the team of the USA a fight, as when they won bronze against us at the World Cup.
Not only Mincy, Gausse, Ramon Rivas, Fico López and "Piculín" (Ortiz) were back with this team, but also the young players (Javier) "Toñito" Colón and James Carter, the brothers León (Francisco and Edgar) and Mario "Quijote" Morales, who could not attend in 1990 because of a knee injury.
A team that may not have been the best in terms of personalities, but with which we won a gold medal and which gave us great pleasure, was that of the pre-Olympic tournament 1995 in Neuquén, Argentina.
With players like "Canito" Nieves, Pablo Alicea and the young Rolando Hourruitiner as replacements for the suspended players because of the occurrences at the Pan-American Games in Mar del Plata, we won the gold medal, contrary to every prognosis.
Who was Puerto Rico's best player?
This was without a doubt Piculín Ortiz.
His figures at the level of the international tournaments are impressive.
No one in Puerto Rico has dominated at this level like Piculín.
And this without taking into account his career in the various leagues in which he has played.
Who was the best coach from Puerto Rico?
That's a difficult question.
We've had a number of good coaches like Julio Toro, Flor Meléndez, Carlos Morales, Raymond Dalmau, Armandito Torres.
Of the boys, I like the work of Leo Arill well.
What do you think is the best thing you've achieved in association?
I was part of the most successful era of the national team from 1988 to 1995, which included the BSN at the beginning of the 90s up to 17 teams in one season.
What's left for you to do?
There are things that I would have introduced, such as the regionalisation of the lower categories.
For example, the guys in Ponce only play in their territory and only meet teams from other corners of the island in the national playoffs.
Today, the kids unnecessarily make a lot of trips and games
At least I see the result of the prescribed trainings and courses for trainers, table officials and referees.
I'm proud of that.
What are you doing today?
I like listening to music, watching videos of music of my time on YouTube, enjoying my grandchildren and every now and then I go to a basketball game.
And, of course, I enjoy the company of my wife Isabel, who has always been by my side.
Larry Hagman is dead
Larry Hagman was born in Forth Worth (Texas) on September 21, 1931 and became famous worldwide with his role as John Ross Ewing, better known as "J.R." in the television series "Dallas," where he played an unscrupulous, insidious and manipulating businessman.
Larry Hagman, whose role as the oil magnate J.R. Ewing in the television series "Dallas" became a symbol of the greed of the 80s, has died.
He was 81 years old.
Hagman, who came back this year as J.R. in a new season of "Dallas," died Friday afternoon due to cancer complications, according to a family press release to The Associated Press by Warner Bros., producer of "Dallas."
"Larry was back in his beloved nativial Dallas, where he once again played the iconic role he liked most," the family said.
Larry's family and his closest friends were with him in Dallas for celebrating Thanksgiving.
Linda Gray, who played his wife's role in the original and follow-up series, was with Hagman when he died at Dallas hospital, her publicist Jeffrey Lane said.
gave joy to everyone who knew him.
He was creative, generous, funny, witty, loving and talented, and I'm going to miss him a lot.
"He was an original person and lived life to the fullest," Gray said in a statement.
Hagman was diagnosed with cirrhosis in 1992, and he admitted that he had drunk a lot for years.
In 1995, a malignant tumor was detected in his liver, and he underwent a transplant.
In the years before "Dallas," Hagman became known on television as a nice guy in the years 1965 to 1970 with the sitcom "Enchanting Jeannie" of the NBC.
There he played the astronaut Tony Nelson, whose life is turned upside down when he meets an attractive "jinn" (played by Barbara Eden), who will live at his home.
He was also performer of two comedies that aired only for a short time, such as The Good Life (NBC, 1971-72) and "Here We Go Again" (ABC, 1973).
His cinematic works included critically-rated roles in "The Group," "Harry and Tonto," and "Primary Colors."
But it was his masterful interpretation of the enchantingly despicated J.R. that made Hagman a superstar.
The dramatic series of the television chain CBS about the Ewing clan and other characters in their environment was broadcast from April 1978 to May 1991.
The advertising slogan "Who shot at J.R.?", which was supposed to create expectation for an episode full of emotions, where Hagmann's character was nearly killed, led to international speculation, and millions of dollars were wagered in bettinghouses.
Thus, the series reached a spectator record at that time.
When the response became known in a chapter in November 1990, it was seen by an average of 41 million viewers who turned "Dallas" into the second most-seen entertainment program since the end of "MASH," which had 50 million viewers.
It was Kristin, the sister-in-law of J.R. (interpreted by Mary Crosby) who scored.
J.R. had impregnated her and threatened to portray her as a prostitute if she didn't leave the city. But others also had reasons to see him dead.
Hagman interpreted Ewing's role as a corrupt, insatiable man with a charismatic smile: a sneaky businessman and unfaithful husband who strived to have his alcoholic wife, Sue Ellen (Linda Gray) instructed.
"I know what's supposed to stand on J.R.'s tombstone," Hagman said in 1988.
It should mean "Here the honorable citizen J.R. Ewing rests".
This is the only business he hasn't won.
Victoria Principal, co-actress of the original series, recalled Hagman on Friday as an "enormous personality on and off the screen."
He is unforgettable and irreplaceable for millions of admirers in the world and remains in our hearts who have been fortunate enough to know and love him.
Ten episodes of the new edition of "Dallas" aired a few months ago with great success on the television channel TNT.
The recording of five chapters for the second season was completed, and a sixth chapter has just been shot, the broadcaster informed.
There was no immediate explanation from Warner or from TNT on how Hagmann's loss in the series would be dealt with.
Born in Fort Worth, Texas, he was the son of actress and singer Mary Martin, who played classical works such as "South Pacific" and "Peter Pan."
Martin was still a teenager when she got Larry during her marriage to lawyer Ben Hagman.
He attempted his luck on the theatre stages in New York in the early 1950s, after which he served from 1952 to 1956 in the air forces in England.
There he met the young designer Maj Axelsson and married her.
The couple had two children, Preston and Heidi, and they lived for a long time in the California town of Malibu, where many celebrities reside.
"Hello Darlin: Tall (and Absolutely True) Tales about My Life
"I didn't write anything that I believed could hurt someone or interfere with him in any way," the Associated Press said at the time.
After liver transplantation, he campaigned for liver donations and worked as a volunteer in a hospital to assist anxious patients.
"I'll advise them, encourage them, I'll meet with them if their operations are imminent and after that," he explained in 1996.
I want to give them some comfort, such as "don't worry, it's a bit uncomfortable the first time, but it'll be fine for you".
He also campaigned against smoking and participated in several campaigns.
beginning of the course studying the "end of the world"
Every week, students examine apocalyptic issues such as nuclear war, zombies, viruses and germs, and global warming.
Every semember, when the religious professor decided to hold a course about the end of the world, he knew he had an irresistible fishing hook: the end of the "long backward counting" of the Mayan calendar on December 21, which many people believed meant the end of the world.
But Charmé had no idea what to expect in the following two months: Destroying Hurricane Sandy, whom some have referred to as "Deudarmaggeddon," as well as a growing conflict with Israel's participation, the place that Christian theorists believe the apocalypse begins.
"I haven't realised this will be the most apocalyptic semester there has ever been," Charmé told the students at the University of Rutgers-Camden (New Jersey) this week.
If someone analyzes what happened in the world today as if we were thirty days away from it, this would be a really good period.
And he recalls that the bad is good for those with an apocalyptic mentality.
And he's not the only professor to offer courses about the "end of the world" this semester that should actually be the last in history.
In Temple, Professor Barry Vacker gives the course "Media, culture and the end of the world."
Every week, students examine apocalyptic issues such as nuclear war, zombies, viruses and germs, and global warming.
"We analyse why these ideas spread over time," he said, as well as the way hypothetical scenarios are offered that lead to certain human behavior.
For example, if nuclear material comes into the hands of terrorists, a war could break out.
This month, the students analyzed films with apocalyptic content and explored how they could be compared to real life.
"I've trieded to inform the students what's possible, probable, credible and impossible," Vacker said.
On the main campus of the University of Pennsylvania, Professor of Latin American History Matthew Restall and his colleague Amara Solari, an associate professor of art history and anthropology, have formed a team to offer a course labeled "The end of the world."
"We'ren't picking up in 2012 so we're always keeping the option of being able to re-deter the course, in case the world doesn't go down," Restall said.
Despite the "imminent demise," students will have to study, create projects and participate in graduation exams.
In Penn State, the final exam will be conducted on the eve of the apocalypse, which is why students have no other chance than "continuing to work until the night where the world is supposedly coming to an end," Restall said.
The courses were very popular.
"In two hours, there were no more places," Restall said of his high-average students course, which filled with 35 students.
For weeks before the start of the semester, we received e-mails from students asking if there were places still available.
The students, for their part, assure that the course is among the most interesting.
"It's intriguing to see what people are doing to comfort themselves," said Bridgid Robinson, a 23-year-old female student at Rutgers-Camden for religion and sociology from Haddonfield, New Jersey.
The apocalyptic sect or religious mentality is actually only a matter of consolation or its absence.
25-year-old student Will Wekesa for psychology and nursing declares to have seen all the apocalyptic films.
"Never have I heard anything about a lesson where this is taught," he admitted.
I'm enjoying it.
But none of the students surveyed - and less a professor - declared they believed in the last date of December 21.
"Our first projection dealt with the Mayans' prophecy, and in certain ways we discredit them," said Julie Zeglen, a student last year in Temple, 21 years old and from West Chester.
The Mayans never predicted the end of the world; it's just a key point on the calendar, Restall said.
However, he notes that there is an apocalyptic fear in Western society that takes back several centuries when people reacted to changes in their environment by predicting the end of the world.
The internet has heightened these speculations.
"Elsewhere, people don't think so," he says.
This is mainly the case in the English-speaking world.
Joseph Dougherty, a religious professor at the University of La Salle who teaches in the Philippines this year, quickly responds to the question of whether he would know a course there about the "end of the world."
"The Philippines does not participate at the end of the world," he wrote as a hint at an exception of a higher authority.
We have a pardon from the pope.
Restall pointed out that many days have been spoken over the years by the last court, and said that if nothing happens on December 21, "people immediately think of a new date" or philosophized that December 21 was the beginning of a seven-year period that the end of the world would come.
Students and professors take this date lightly.
Several said they would like to go to celebrations about "the end of the world."
"Maybe I'll call some friends so we can laugh together," said Samira Ford, a 20-year-old student for communications.
